\par
Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting
\par
Opportunities in Data Analytics
\par
Gary P. Schneider, Jun Dai, Diane J. Janvrin, Kemi Ajayi,
\par
and Robyn L. Raschke
\par
SYNOPSIS: The business use of data analytics is growing rapidly in the accounting
\par
environment. Similar to many new systems that involve accounting information, data
\par
analytics has fundamentally changed task processes, particularly those tasks that
\par
provide inference, prediction, and assurance to decision-makers. Thus, accounting
\par
researchers and practitioners must consider data analytics and its impact on accounting
\par
practice in their work. This paper uses the organizing principles from Mauldin and
\par
Ruchala's (1999) meta-theory of accounting information systems (AIS) to identify current
\par
data analytics use, examine how data analytics impacts the accounting environment,
\par
and discuss challenges and research opportunities.
\par
Keywords: data analytics; AIS meta-theory, task processes.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The role of accountants in developing, interpreting, and facilitating managers' use of
\par
information has changed as data gathering and processing technologies have improved and
\par
evolved (Elliot 1992). As the cost of gathering, storing, and processing information
\par
dropped, accounting moved from summarizing transaction data into generally accepted formats to
\par
gathering and aggregating information (Borthick 1996) to be used in infer, predict, and assure tasks
\par
(Institute of Internal Auditors [IIA] 2006; Vasarhelyi 2012; Geerts, Graham, Mauldin, McCarthy,
\par
Gary P. Schneider is a Professor at Quinnipiac University; Jun Dai is an Assistant Professor at
\par
Southwestern University of Finance and Economics and a Ph.D. student at Rutgers, The State
\par
University of New Jersey, Newark; Diane J. Janvrin is an Associate Professor at Iowa State
\par
University; Kemi Ajayi is an Assistant Professor at the University of Colorado Denver; and Robyn
\par
L. Raschke is an Associate Professor at the University of Nevada, Las Vegas.
\par
We thank the participants from the 2014 23rd Annual Research Workshop on Strategic and Emerging Technologies for
\par
helpful feedback, and Natalie Wingert for her research assistance.
