Corresponding Author: Diane J. Janvrin
\par
Email: djanvrin@iastate.edu
\par
719720 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
and Richardson 2013).1 The objective of this paper is to examine how data analytics will impact the
\par
accounting and auditing environment, identify emerging management and regulatory challenges,
\par
and outline new research opportunities. This is important to practitioners as well as academics
\par
because they will be using data analytics in accounting and auditing tasks and will need to specify
\par
system design characteristics needed to effectively accomplish these tasks (Vasarhelyi, Kogan, and
\par
Tuttle 2015).
\par
Today, accountants provide essential decision support to senior management by analyzing vast
\par
amounts of complex data, increasing portions of which come from sources other than traditionally
\par
defined accounting systems (Davenport and Harris 2007; Siegel 2013; Haverson 2014). For
\par
example, financial and non-financial data extracted from web server logs, Internet and mobile
\par
phone clickstream records, social media, and a variety of machine-generated and sensor-detected
\par
data, may be analyzed and used to make key business decisions (Chui, Loffler, and Roberts 2010;
\par
Bertolucci 2013a; O'Leary 2013). In addition, governments are undertaking initiatives to open
\par
third-party access both to internal administrative data and data collected from citizens and
\par
businesses as part of government operations, which will create even more data analytics
\par
opportunities (Casselman 2015; Office of Management and Budget [OMB] 2015).
\par
To incorporate and process both structured and unstructured data to support decisions,
\par
accountants are working with a new set of sophisticated tools known as data analytics (ISACA
\par
2014). Data analytics is the process of using structured and unstructured data through the
\par
applications of various analytic techniques such as statistical and quantitative analysis and
\par
explanatory and predictive models to provide useful information to decision-makers (Davenport
\par
and Harris 2007; Trkman, McCormack, de Oliveira, and Ladeira 2010; Sims and Sossei 2012;
\par
Maurno 2013). Accountants have appropriate skills for data analytics tasks since they are generally
\par
detail-oriented, trained to document their work, experienced in making judgments regarding
\par
assertions, familiar with multiple aspects of business including information technologies, and
\par
perceived as trusted advisors (Haverson 2014).
\par
The emergence of data analytics will significantly change the infer, predict, and assure tasks
\par
performed by accountants and auditors. For example, accountants and managers are implementing
\par
data analytics to identify or infer operational inefficiencies (Dai, Byrnes, and Vasarhelyi 2013), tax
\par
professionals are adopting data analytics to predict future tax liabilities (DaBruzzo, Dannenfelser,
\par
and DeRocco 2013), regulators are using data analytics to identify fraudulent financial reporting
\par
(Eaglesham 2013), and auditors can design data analytics to provide assurance by flagging risky
\par
transactions (Vasarhelyi 2013; Verver and Grimm 2013; Alles 2014; Brown-Liburd, Issa, and
\par
Lombardi 2015). However, applying data analytics to these tasks can generate concerns about
\par
organizations maintaining data confidentiality and integrity, the effects of data analytics on data
\par
biases, and whether data analytics will increase auditors' legal liability. These concerns motivate
\par
research into how data analytics is, and will be, used in accounting.
\par
We adopt the four organizing principles from Mauldin and Ruchala's (1999) meta-theory
\par
accounting information systems (AIS) model to examine how data analytics will impact the
\par
accounting and auditing environment, identify emerging management and regulatory challenges,
\par
and outline new research opportunities. These organizing principles are: task characteristics, design
\par
process characteristics, contingency factors, and task outcomes.
\par
Examining the impact of data analytics on accounting tasks is important; in contrast to early
\par
accounting information systems that did little more than speed up existing manual processes,
\par
today's data gathering and analytics technologies can fundamentally change accounting and audit
\par
1 Following IIA (2006), we define infer tasks as those that provide insights, predict tasks as those that supply decision-
\par
makers with foresight, and assure tasks as those that provide oversight.
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 721
\par
task processes (KPMG 2012; Setty and Bakhshi 2013; Titera 2013; Brown-Liburd et al. 2015). By
\par
changing task processes, today's accounting systems can provide innovative and complex decision
\par
support rather than simply increasing the efficiency of traditional accounting tasks. In addition, in
\par
contrast to prior research in data analytics that concentrates on assure tasks (Thiprungsri and
\par
Vasarhelyi 2011; Kim and Vasarhelyi. 2012; Dai et al. 2013; Jans et al. 2014; Kim and Kogan
\par
2014), we use the organizing principles of Mauldin and Ruchala's (1999) meta-theory of
\par
accounting information systems to examine how data analytics impacts not only these assure tasks
\par
but also infer and predict tasks. We believe this approach generates research questions that will
\par
interest a broader research community including financial and managerial accounting researchers as
\par
well as audit researchers.
\par
Further, auditors now use data analytics to understand and evaluate security and controls over
\par
clients' large datasets (Ernst \& Young Global Limited 2014; Cao, Chychyla, and Stewart 2015;
\par
Vasarhelyi et al. 2015). Finally, accounting curricula are affected by the growth in data analytics
\par
use in business. The Pathways Commission on Accounting Higher Education (Behn 2010),
\par
sponsored by the American Accounting Association and American Institute of Certified Public
\par
Accountants (AAA-AICPA), encourages the Association to Advance Collegiate Schools of
\par
Business International's (AACSB) new accounting program accreditation standards that require
\par
accounting students' learning experiences to include``data creation, data sharing, data analytics,
\par
data mining, data reporting, and storage within and across organizations'' (AACSB 2013, 30).
\par
The paper begins with background information on data analytics and identifies examples of
\par
how it is currently used in accounting. Next, we present the four organizing principles of the
\par
Mauldin and Ruchala (1999) meta-theory AIS model. The section on``Applying Organizing
\par
Principles to Data Analytics'' uses these organizing principles to analyze the impact of data
\par
analytics in today's accounting environment and identify research opportunities. Finally, we
\par
conclude by discussing the challenges accounting practitioners face with data analytics as well as
\par
research limitations.
\par
DATA ANALYTICS: BACKGROUND AND CURRENT EXAMPLES
\par
Data analytics includes tools that leverage current technologies to extract and analyze
\par
information (Jordan 2013). As technology changes, and data variety and volume increase, data
\par
analytics tools will also evolve (Bertolucci 2013a). Some (cf. Chen, Chiang, and Storey 2012;
\par
Minelli, Chambers, and Dhiraj 2013; Gillon, Sinan, Lin, Mithas, and Zozulia 2014) see data
\par
analytics tools as an evolutionary development within the practice of business intelligence, which
\par
combines the gathering and storage of operational data in enterprise information systems and data
\par
warehouses with analytical tools to present complex information to decision-makers (Negash 2004;
\par
Chen et al. 2012). Others distinguish data analytics from business intelligence by noting its focus on
\par
inference and prediction, its use of only approximately correct conclusions, and its lack of reliance
\par
on the collection, query, and reporting of accurate, reliable, and verifiable past information that
\par
characterizes business intelligence systems (Gualtieri and Sridharan 2014; Koch 2015).2 Early
\par
applications of data analytics involved marketing and social networks, e-government, medical, and
\par
other domain-specific information systems that contain unstructured data (Chen et al. 2012). Today,
\par
data analytics offers accountants opportunities to generate value.
\par
2 We acknowledge the helpful comment of an anonymous reviewer who noted that business intelligence focuses on the
\par
collection, query, and reporting of past information that needs to be accurate, reliable, and verifiable, while data
\par
analytics uses various statistical techniques to support inferences or predictions and only requires approximately
\par
correct conclusions.
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015722 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
Traditionally, accounting is defined as the recording of financial transactions and rules for
\par
aggregating and summarizing important information about those transactions (Ijiri 1975).
\par
Accountants use the information stored about financial transactions in a variety of ways, but
\par
accounting tasks are often classified by auditors (IIA 2006; Goodson, Mory, and Lapointe 2012),
\par
business analysts (Bloor and Jozwiak 2013), and enterprise risk managers (Heiligtag, Schlosser,
\par
and Stegemann 2014) into categories such as infer, predict, and assure tasks. Data analytics gives
\par
accountants tools to examine information from these three different perspectives. For example, an
\par
accountant might use data analytics to understand organizational spending patterns in order to infer
\par
effective cost reduction strategies and possible work flow improvements. Data analytics can help
\par
predict future sales demand or stock performance and thus aid key decision-makers in an
\par
organization. Finally, data analytics can be used in assurance tasks such as continuous monitoring
\par
or auditing (Vasarhelyi, Alles, and Williams 2010; Yoon, Hoogduin, and Zhang 2015; Zhang,
\par
Yang, and Applebaum 2015).
\par
Many practicing accountants and accounting researchers divide accounting into financial,
\par
managerial, audit, tax, and fraud domains as shown in Table 1. Each domain uses transaction
\par
information, but for different purposes. In the financial accounting domain, data analytics has been
\par
used to infer trading strategies (Bertolucci 2013b), predict future financial accounting discrepancies,
\par
and evaluate current financial performance (Eaglesham 2013). Management accounting uses data
\par
analytics tools to integrate research and development, engineering, and manufacturing information
\par
to reduce production time and improve quality (Manyika et al. 2011). Further, management may
\par
use automatic transaction correction to gain confidence that information used for decision-making is
\par
reliable (Kogan, Alles, Vasarhelyi, and Wu 2014). Data analytics in the tax domain can help
\par
identify overpayment of taxes, a problem for many multi-national organizations that store
\par
transaction information in geographically dispersed systems (Deloitte 2012a).
\par
Perhaps the most common current use of data analytics in accounting is in assurance tasks,
\par
such as fraud detection and compliance (Jans, Alles, and Vasarhelyi 2014; Brown-Liburd et al.
\par
2015). For example, auditors can use data analytics to evaluate control effectiveness and policy
\par
compliance. By analyzing transaction-level information, auditors and accountants have used data
\par
analytics to identify unusual data flows, such as unexpected large volumes of data, high-frequency
\par
transactions, manipulated transactions, or duplicate vendor payments that might indicate fraudulent
\par
activities (Dutta and Tavawala 2013). Similarly, auditors can use data analytics to identify unusual
\par
transactions or the abnormal transaction volumes indicative of money laundering activities (Siegel
\par
2013).
\par
ORGANIZING PRINCIPLES BASED ON AIS META-THEORY MODEL
\par
We develop our discussion based on the four organizing principles of Mauldin and Ruchala's
\par
(1999) accounting information systems meta-theory model. We adopt these organizing meta-theory
\par
principles because they provide a paradigm that can integrate extant data analytics research and
\par
identify synergistic opportunities from differing perspectives. Further, these principles form a
\par
framework for organizing and interpreting future research that allows researchers to evaluate how
\par
data analytics should be considered in conjunction with task achievement (cf. Mauldin and Ruchala
\par
1999). In addition, these principles allow researchers to compare and contrast data analytics with
\par
older accounting information systems.
\par
Our adaptation of the Mauldin and Ruchala (1999, 320) model for data analytics appears in
\par
Figure 1. The first meta-theory principle suggests that data analytic research should be task-focused
\par
(Mauldin and Ruchala 1999). We expand the first organizing principle to note that data analytics in
\par
accounting are generally used in infer, predict, and assure tasks. Since the task focus in data
\par
analytics drives the design process, contingency factors, and performance, these three organizing
