\par
APPLYING ORGANIZING PRINCIPLES TO DATA ANALYTICS
\par
We now apply the organizing principles to data analytics to identify future research
\par
opportunities. These future opportunities are listed at the end of each section and are summarized in
\par
Appendix A.
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 725
\par
Organizing Principle 1: Infer, Predict, and Assure Tasks
\par
The first organizing principle suggests that data analytics research should be task-focused and
\par
within this principle exist four core dimensions of task characteristics: the mental process involved
\par
with the task, the complexity of the task, the demand of the task, and the frequency of occurrence
\par
(Mauldin and Ruchala 1999).The mental processes dimension includes the information search/
\par
acquisition process, information evaluation and integration, and judgment or decision-making.
\par
From a data analytic perspective, accountants should consider the problem to address, what data are
\par
needed, and where to find the data for decision-making purposes. In accounting research, task
\par
complexity is typically considered to be a function of the amount and clarity of inputs, processing,
\par
and outputs (e.g., Bonner 1994). Schroder, Driver, and Streufert (1967) describe the relationship
\par
between information processing and environmental complexity as an inverted U-curve. In this
\par
characterization, an increase in environmental complexity initially facilitates and increases
\par
information processing. However, further increases in environmental complexity beyond the
\par
decision-maker's problem-solving threshold result in less additional information being incorporated
\par
into the decision-making process. At this point, information processing starts to decline. In the
\par
context of data analytics, environmental complexity results from the vast amount and diverse nature
\par
of data that decision-makers must process and leads to:
\par
RQ1.1: To what extent does an increase in environmental complexity impact information
\par
processing and data analytic task performance?
\par
A similar issue arises from the complexity of data analytics techniques themselves, which have
\par
various levels of inherent complexity regardless of the size and nature of the dataset being analyzed.
\par
This issue gives rise to:
\par
RQ1.2: To what extent does the inherent complexity of data analytics techniques impact
\par
information processing and data analytic task performance?
\par
The task demands dimension exists on a continuum from physical labor to symbolic, abstract
\par
knowledge (Mauldin and Ruchala 1999). Decision-making environments, or conceptual work
\par
structures, can be either concrete or abstract (Schroder et al. 1967). Concrete conceptual structures
\par
include fixed rule-sets and few judgment dimensions, which lead to relatively simple decision-
\par
making. On the other hand, tasks that involve abstract and differentiated conceptual structures
\par
require integration across multiple judgment dimensions and are more complex in nature. Problem-
\par
solving in this situation requires the simultaneous evaluation of multiple alternative courses of
\par
action. Accounting tasks that provide inference, prediction, and assurance require the possession of
\par
analytical and abstract conceptual knowledge structures. This concern leads to:
\par
RQ1.3: To what extent do the interactions between analytical and abstract conceptual
\par
knowledge structures possessed and needed by data analytics designers or users and
\par
the characteristics of infer, predict, and assure tasks impact task outcomes?
\par
Organizing Principle 2: Design Process and Design Alternatives
\par
Mauldin and Ruchala (1999) emphasize in their meta-theory that the task requirements drive
\par
the design process that, in turn, yields design alternatives. System design characteristics include the
\par
set of hardware, software, procedures, and data that combine to accomplish a specific organizational
\par
task (or set of tasks) (March and Smith 1995). March and Smith (1995) view these characteristics in
\par
a hierarchy consisting of constructs, models, methods, and instantiations. Examples of constructs in
\par
accounting include generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) and international financial
\par
reporting standards (IFRS). In data analytics, an important construct is machine learning, which is
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015726 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
used to extract predictions from, and perform error/characteristic identification in, large datasets
\par
(Siegel 2013). Models are sets of propositions that express relationships among constructs (Geerts
\par
et al. 2013). In accounting, models include double-entry bookkeeping (Gleeson-White 2012); the
\par
Resources, Events, Agents model (McCarthy 1982); and XBRL taxonomies (Amrhein 2011;
\par
Arnold, Bedard, Phillips, and Sutton 2012). In data analytics, models are not yet well defined. In
\par
fact, Mayer-Scho \textasciidieresis nberger and Cukier (2013) identify this lack of accepted models as an ongoing
\par
challenge in data analytics. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ2.1: What data analytics models will be useful in accounting infer, predict, and assure
\par
tasks?
\par
Methods for creating specific accounting system elements and processes include relational
\par
databases, spreadsheet software, software agents, and continuous auditing tools. Methods in data
\par
analytics are often grouped under the name artificial intelligence, including natural language
\par
processing, machine learning, computer vision, simultaneous localization and mapping (Bryn-
\par
jolfsson and McAfee 2014, 91), and algorithms such as MapReduce (Dean and Ghemawat 2004).
\par
Although specific software products such as Oracle Enterprise Resource Planning and SAP ERP
\par
Financials are examples of accounting system instantiations, data analytics instantiations range
\par
from computational clustering tools such as Hadoop (Zikopoulos, Eaton, deRoos, Deutsch, and
\par
Lapis 2012), to non-relational querying software products generally known as NoSQL (Sadalage
\par
and Fowler 2013). Our discussion leads to:
\par
RQ2.2: What alternative methods and instantiations work best and under what conditions to
\par
capture, store, and integrate structured and unstructured data?
\par
Contingency Factors
\par
The third data analytics organizing principle proposes that the effects of data analytics must be
\par
examined within the context of cognitive, technological, and organizational contingency factors.
\par
Cognitive Factors
\par
The cognitive perspective is suggested when examining the impact of data analytics on
\par
decision-making since technology affects decision-makers in three different ways: intended
\par
technical effects, transient effects associated with assimilation, and unintended social effects
\par
(Mauldin and Ruchala 1999). Generally, the intended technical effect of information technologies is
\par
improved decision-making performance. For example, data analytics is expected to create value for
\par
relevant stakeholders by processing complex data effectively, increasing transparency, and
\par
supporting human decision-making with automated algorithms. However, prior research has shown
\par
that the proposed benefits associated with technologies are not always realized and that those
\par
technologies can lead to unintended consequences. For example, the presence of electronic
\par
workpapers, audit expert systems, and decision aids have had mixed effects on the use of such
\par
systems and on the performance of audits (e.g., Bible, Graham, and Rosman 2005; Rosman, Bible,
\par
Biggs, and Graham 2006; Bedard, Ettredge, and Johnstone 2007). Researchers have found
\par
enterprise resource planning (ERP) implementations to have unintended social effects caused by the
\par
changing roles of managerial accountants in post-implementation environments (e.g., Arnold
\par
2006). Empirical studies of the SEC's XBRL and interactive financial reporting mandate found that
\par
financial statement users may not choose to use an XBRL-enabled technology (e.g., Hodge,
\par
Kennedy, and Maines 2004; Janvrin, Pinsker, and Mascha 2013), even when such use facilitates
\par
information acquisition and integration. Brown-Liburd et al. (2015) outline cognitive factors that
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 727
\par
could impede auditors' use of data analytics, including information overload, information
\par
relevance, pattern recognition, and ambiguity.
\par
One reason prior research has shown that technology use does not always lead to improved
\par
performance is that decision-makers have cognitive biases in the presence of decision aids (e.g.,
\par
Whitecotton 1996; Masselli, Ricketts, Arnold, and Sutton 2002; Arnold, Collier, Leech, and Sutton
\par
2004). Decision-makers tend to adopt a``computer is right'' attitude while using technology or
\par
decision aids, which in turn exacerbates cognitive biases. For example, use of interactive financial
\par
reporting can improve financial statement analysis but can also lead to overconfidence and user
\par
miscalibration (Tang, Hess, Valacich, and Sweeney 2014). Although data analytics often involve
\par
working with large volumes of structured and unstructured data, the results are only as good as the
\par
quality of the initial input. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.1.1: How do cognitive biases impact information acquisition and information integration
\par
in infer, predict, and assure tasks?
\par
Another contingent cognitive factor is information load. Information load occurs when the
\par
human cognitive structure becomes overtaxed by the amount of information that it must integrate into
\par
its decision-making process (Schroder et al. 1967). Accounting research on the effects of information
\par
load has shown that increases in the amount and diversity of information presented to managers and
\par
auditors resulted in low predictive accuracy and increase in decision-making time (Casey 1980),
\par
lower decision accuracy (Iselin 1988), reduced cue usage and decision quality (Chewning and Harrell
\par
1990), decreases in judgment consistency and consensus (Stocks and Harrell 1995), and limited
\par
information selection and processing (Simnett 1996). The use of various data analytic techniques, and
\par
the large amounts of data that must be acquired and integrated during decision making, can increase
\par
information load. Brown-Liburd et al. (2015) note that auditors working with traditional data are
\par
already inundated with exceptions and that the use of data analytics is expected to increase the
\par
number of identified exceptions and anomalies. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.1.2: How does information load impact the decision-making process in infer, predict,
\par
and assure tasks?
\par
Alternatively, the use of data visualization techniques in data analytics may aid the decision
\par
process if the representations match the decision being made and the knowledge structure of the
\par
decision-maker. For example, in a loan fraud detection task, visualization techniques such as
\par
configuring relationship graphs may allow the fraud examiner to examine relationships and
\par
behavior patterns between customers and loan officers to determine irregular loan approval (Dilla
\par
and Raschke 2015). However, data visualization techniques may further increase the demands of
\par
the task environment if they are poorly designed or if representations do not match the decision
\par
environment (Vessey 1991). Cognitive load is defined as the load that performing a task imposes on
\par
the task-doer's cognitive system (Paas, Tuovinen, Tabbers, and Van Gerven 2003). A mismatch
\par
between data visualization techniques and the accounting task could increase a decision-maker's
\par
cognitive load during a task (Vessey 1991; Vessey and Galletta 1991; Tuttle and Kershaw 1998;
\par
Dilla and Steinbart 2005). Data analytics should be focused on representations that facilitate the
\par
understanding of the underlying represented data and query results. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.1.3: How will cognitive load impact infer, predict, and assure tasks and which visual
\par
representations best match the task involved?
\par
Technological Factors
\par
Technological factors are elements of a particular technology that can affect the task or the
\par
decision-maker. Data analytics technological factors include visual analytics, use of mobile
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015728 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
applications to collect data, and advanced data mining techniques such as clustering, rule-based
\par
expert systems, decision tree, association analysis, and neural networks. These technological factors
\par
present challenges to researchers and practitioners of data analytics. One challenge is how to
\par
determine which data analytics techniques are suitable to be used in specific accounting tasks. For
\par
example, neural network techniques generally perform well when learning complex hidden patterns
\par
and are robust to noise, features helpful when detecting complex financial frauds (Fanning and
\par
Cogger 1998; Kirkos, Spathis, and Manolopoulos 2007). However, understanding the internal logic
\par
of neural networks is difficult and neural network modeling requires great computational resources
\par
(Tu 1996). These technological factors may preclude auditors' use of neural networks to detect
\par
financial frauds in real time. Rule-based expert systems are relatively more efficient in handling
\par
large datasets than neural networks. They collect experts' experiences in detecting financial frauds,
\par
convert them into rules, and then use those rules to provide recommendations on potential fraud
\par
cases. The main challenge of rule-based expert systems is the difficulty in developing high-quality
\par
knowledge (Garcia-Teodoro, Diaz-Verdejo, Macia \textasciiacute -Ferna \textasciiacute ndez, and Va \textasciiacute zquez 2009). Compared to
\par
neural networks and rule-based expert systems, decision trees have higher accuracy and
\par
interpretability and lower computational complexity (Alpaydin 2010); however, time-consuming
\par
model pruning must be performed to avoid model overfitting. Each technique has its special
\par
features and drawbacks. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.2.1: To what extent do the characteristics of infer, predict, and assure tasks interact with
\par
the characteristics of specific data analytics and how can these technologies be
\par
customized to accomplish a specific infer, predict, or assure task?
\par
Further, the increasing number of data analytics techniques makes technique selection
\par
challenging. Brown-Liburd et al. (2015) observe that the ability to choose the appropriate data
\par
analytics technique will be an important determinant of data analytics acceptance by auditors.
\par
Accountants, especially less experienced accountants, will likely need similar guidance when
\par
selecting appropriate data analytics techniques to accomplish accounting tasks. This discussion
\par
leads to:
\par
RQ3.2.2: What guidance do accountants need when selecting data analytics technologies to
\par
meet specific decision objectives of infer, predict, and assure tasks?
\par
Interpretability, which is the ability to recognize and understand the data, can be hard to
\par
achieve in visual analytics. In a typical data analytics application, large volumes of both financial
\par
and non-financial data are included. This data include both structured and unstructured data, such as
\par
textual data (e.g., comments from customers, emails between managers, and tweets of companies),
\par
audio data (e.g., recorded phone calls), and video data. Advanced data visualization techniques are
\par
often used to present data distribution and special patterns. Generating a useful output from raw
\par
data and drawing the right conclusions largely depends on the quality of the data and methods used.
\par
Meaningful visualizations and clear representations can help decision-makers perform better. This
\par
discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.2.3: What factors (such as motion, color, size, shape, dimensions, or sound) produce
\par
more meaningful visualizations and clearer representations to improve decision-
\par
making in infer, predict, and assure tasks?
\par
Companies now use interactive visualization techniques to facilitate understanding and
\par
analysis of large and complex sets of data (Kelton and Yang 2008; Dilla, Janvrin, and Raschke
\par
2010). For example, interactivity and visualization have been identified as two characteristics that
\par
could improve financial statement analysis task performance (e.g., Dilla et al. 2010; Tang et al.
\par
2014). This discussion leads to:
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 729
\par
RQ3.2.4: How will interactive technologies impact the usefulness of data analytics in infer,
\par
predict, and assure tasks?
\par
Many organizations collect data through mobile applications and sensors for use in
\par
continuous monitoring and continuous auditing applications. Dynamic business processes
\par
networks and telecommunications generate tremendous streams of time-stamped or real time data
\par
such as sensor logs, web statistics, network traffic logs, and atmospheric and meteorological
\par
information (Keim, Mansmann, Schneidewind, and Ziegler 2006). The sheer amount of this data
\par
makes effective compression and feature extraction methods necessary to manage the data,
\par
analyze it, and present the results in a meaningful and intuitive way (Keim et al. 2006). This
\par
discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.2.5: What applications and methods are most effective to collect, extract, and analyze
\par
large mobile and sensor data streams to be used in infer, predict, and assure
\par
tasks?
\par
Organizational Factors
\par
To be successful, data analytic applications should be linked to business strategy, easy for
\par
end-users to understand, and embedded into organizational processes so that users can take action
\par
at the right time (Siegel 2013). Organizational factors include strategies adopted by the
\par
organization, structure (both formal and informal) of the organization, and the organization's
\par
environment, which can include industry business practices, level and nature of competition,
\par
strategic alliances, supply chain cooperation, relevant laws and regulations, and the cultural
\par
environment in which the organization operates. Mauldin and Ruchala (1999) note that these
\par
organizational factors can determine which technologies are chosen while the choice of
\par
technologies can simultaneously cause changes to organizational features. In this section, we
\par
summarize relevant work published in three specific disciplines: contingency theory, information
\par
economics, and organizational typology.
\par
Contingency theory. Contingency theory research in accounting (Waterhouse and Tiessen
\par
1978; Chenhall 2003) examines how contextual variables affect accounting system choices
\par
(Naranjo-Gil 2004). Contextual variables studied include organizations' internal and external
\par
environments (Chenhall and Morris 1986), sizes and structures (Gul and Chia 1994), production
\par
technologies (Young and Selto 1991), information technologies (Reneau and Grabski 1987), and
\par
overall strategies (Gerdin and Greve 2004; Chenhall, Kallunki, and Silvola 2011). Kiron, Ferguson,
\par
and Prentice (2012) extend this approach to data analytics issues, identifying specific organization
\par
characteristics where data analytics will either create a competitive advantage or enhance
\par
innovation. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.1: How will organizations use data analytics to transform business models, create
\par
competitive advantage, or find new ways to innovate?
\par
Internal environment characteristics that have been found to affect accounting system choices
\par
include organizational structure characteristics such as formality (Ginzberg 1980), horizontal or
\par
vertical nature (Chenhall 2008), and modularity (Karim 2006). Research has documented the
\par
effect of other internal environment factors on the choice of accounting systems, such as the level
\par
of formal controls (Bruns and Waterhouse 1975; Chapman and Kihn 2009), degree of innovation
\par
(Bisbe and Otley 2004), and use of social networking (Chenhall et al. 2011). Characteristics of an
\par
organization's human resources function can affect its use of data analytics because the human
\par
resources function is both a facilitator of data analytics use throughout the organization and a
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015730 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
consumer of internal data analytics outcomes (Harris, Craig, and Light 2011). This discussion
\par
leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.2: How will internal environment factors, such as the level of formal controls,
\par
influence organizations' adoption and use of data analytics?
\par
An organization's external environment, in particular the degree and nature of its
\par
interorganizational relationships (Oliver and Ebers 1998), can affect its use of data analytics.
\par
Ha \r{}kansson and Lind (2004, 2007) note varying degrees of accounting and business network
\par
coordination in interorganizational settings. Although some organizations outsource business
\par
processes, including data analytics, they can find doing so inhibits innovation by reducing the
\par
development of internal expertise or proprietary intellectual property (Lacity, Solomon, Yan, and
\par
Willcocks 2011; Fogarty and Bell 2014; Lacity and Willcocks 2014). This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.3: How will external environment factors, such as the nature and degree of
\par
interorganizational relationships, affect organizations' adoption and use of data
\par
analytics?
\par
Contingency research also explores the relationships between specific production environ-
\par
ments, the controls they require, and the accounting systems that are adopted to monitor them
\par
(Gordon and Miller 1976; Bouwens and Abernethy 2000; Gerdin 2005). Studies of interactions
\par
between ERP system features and organizational factors studied (Kallunki, Laitinen, and Silvola
\par
2011; Sa \textasciiacute nchez-Rodr\i{} \textasciiacute guez and Spraakman 2012) suggest that similar interactions might exist
\par
between data analytics use and organizational factors. Field reports on data analytics
\par
implementations suggest that organizational factors affect their success and that data analytics
\par
use, in turn, affects organization structure choices (see, for example, Ferguson 2012). This
\par
discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.4: What organizational structure elements (such as specific production environments,
\par
controls, or accounting systems) will affect the ability of and likelihood that
\par
organizations adopt data analytics?
\par
As research concerning the relationships among organizational factors and accounting
\par
systems develops, more studies will likely incorporate multiple dimensions. For example,
\par
Chenhall et al. (2011) examined the relationships among strategy, innovation, and management
\par
control systems, where those systems were defined as a package of social networking, culture,
\par
and a formal set of controls. Consistent with this research, organizations have identified ways to
\par
use data analytics to change multiple organizational factors simultaneously, resulting in a
\par
transformation of their business models (Ferguson 2013). Organizations could, therefore, use data
\par
analytics to adapt their business models to achieve a particular strategic result or to achieve the
\par
goals of their existing business strategy (Miles and Snow 1978; Porter 1980). This discussion
\par
leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.5: How do organizational factors affect the ability of organizations to adopt data
\par
analytics, the likelihood they will use data analytics, and what strategies for
\par
outsourcing data analytics will be optimal?
\par
Information economics. Research into the information problems managers face as they
\par
make decisions, including transaction cost economics (Coase 1937; Williamson 1985) and
\par
agency theory (Baiman 1995; Lambert 2001), concludes that no obvious single optimal structure
\par
for organizing business activity exists. The nature, quality, and quantity of information can help
\par
determine optimal organizational structure in specific cases (Arya, Glover, and Sivaramakrishnan
\par
1997). For example, research comparing the execution of transactions in external markets to their
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 731
\par
execution within the organization (using divisional structures and transfer prices) has
\par
demonstrated the interdependence of organizational design and accounting system choice
\par
(Spicer 1988; Colbert and Spicer 1995). Data analytics can change significantly the nature,
\par
quality, and quantity of information available to the organization (Siegel 2013). As a source of
\par
new information, data analytics thus has the potential to change optimal organizational structures,
\par
their accounting systems, and how those systems will be audited (Setty and Bakhshi 2013; Cao et
\par
al. 2015; Griffin and Wright 2015; Vasarhelyi et al. 2015; Warren, Moffitt, and Byrnes 2015).
\par
This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.6: How does data analytics prompt changes in organizational structures, accounting
\par
systems, and how those accounting systems are audited?
\par
Organizational typology. Accounting systems play a role in strategy management, acting as a
\par
mechanism that enables organizational strategy (Chenhall 2003; Gerdin and Greve 2004). Miles
\par
and Snow (1978) and Porter (1980) developed a set of business strategy typologies that many
\par
researchers have used to characterize specific combinations of strategy and structure that occur
\par
frequently in organizations. The Miles and Snow (1978) organization types include defenders,
\par
prospectors, analyzers, and reactors. This typology provides a well-recognized and broadly
\par
accepted set of categories that have been validated (Shortell and Zajac 1990) and used by
\par
organization design and business strategy researchers for decades.
\par
Dvir, Segev, and Shenhar (1993) examine the impact of technology adoption on organization
\par
performance and found that different organization types obtain different levels of benefit from those
\par
adoptions. For example, prospector organizations monitor a wide range of environmental
\par
conditions, trends, and events; thus, such organizations could apply data analytics directly (Kiron,
\par
Ferguson, and Prentice 2012, 2013). Defender and reactor organizations could use data analytics to
\par
change their organization type (Ferguson 2013) and capitalize on innovation opportunities
\par
(Kruschwitz and Shockley 2010). This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ3.3.7: How does the use of data analytics to change multiple organizational factors
\par
simultaneously lead to transformation of business models?
\par
Organizing Principle 4: Task Performance
\par
Individual versus Organizational Level
\par
When evaluating the outcome of data analytics, researchers need to examine task performance
\par
at both individual and organizational levels. At the individual level, the outcome of data analytics
\par
may be measured by examining how the specific data analytics model meets its goal rather that goal
\par
is to correctly infer or identify operational inefficiencies, predict next year profits, or detect fraud. In
\par
practice, the outcome of data analytics may also be evaluated on other criteria such as time and
\par
space complexity for training and testing data analytics models, easy programmability, and
\par
interpretability (Alpaydin 2010).
\par
At the organizational level, management could consider the feasibility of data analytics,
\par
specifically economic, technical, and organizational feasibility (Murthy and Groomer 2003).
\par
Economic feasibility requires that the benefits of data analytics should outweigh their costs. Thus,
\par
the costs and benefits of data analytics should be accurately estimated. Technical feasibility refers to
\par
the technology (hardware, software, networks, etc.) existing in the organization that can support
\par
data analytics implementation. Organizational feasibility measures whether data analytics models
\par
can become an integral part of existing organizational structures without interrupting normal
\par
business activities. This discussion leads to:
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015732 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
RQ4.1: What is the best way to evaluate task performance at the individual level and at the
\par
organizational level?
\par
Evaluating task performance at the individual and organizational levels can be challenging. For
\par
example, management may question the quality and reliability of the information produced by data
\par
analytics. Recently, the AICPA's Audit Data Standard Working Group issued three voluntary
\par
uniform Audit Data Standards that define information quality for general ledger and accounts
\par
receivable sub-ledger audits (Zhang, Pawlicki, McQuilken, and Titera 2012; American Institute of
\par
Certified Public Accountants [AICPA] 2013; Alles, Vasarhelyi, and Issa 2013; Titera 2013; Brown-
\par
Liburd et al. 2015). These data standards could be applied to information produced by data
\par
analytics to provide additional assurance that this information is accurate and in a usable format to
\par
be used in infer, predict, and assure tasks. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ4.2: What is the impact of recent data standard changes on the use of data analytics in
\par
infer, predict, and assure tasks?
\par
Data analytics techniques do not provide completely accurate predictions. To evaluate the
\par
efficiency and effectiveness of data analytics, researchers have developed metrics such as false
\par
negative and false positive errors (Tan, Steinbach, and Kumar 2005). However, challenges remain
\par
in evaluating the outcomes of data analytics. For example, in classification, the costs of
\par
misclassification errors may depend on the nature of particular cases (Turney 2000) and vary
\par
largely among different accounting tasks, so that incorporating costs in evaluating data analytics
\par
outcomes is important. For example, in financial fraud detection, missing a severe fraud (false
\par
negative error) is much more costly than investigating some normal transactions (false positive
\par
error) (Phua, Lee, Smith, and Gayler 2010). However, noisy data could result in a large number of
\par
false positive errors (Yoon et al. 2015), that could lead managers to spend more resources
\par
investigating them than the resource loss caused by an undetected anomaly (false negative error).
\par
This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ4.3: What performance metrics for data analytics are best used in infer, predict, and assure
\par
tasks?
\par
Confidentiality and Privacy Implications
\par
Often the output of data analytics includes sensitive information with confidentiality or
\par
privacy implications. Confidentiality refers to the objective of controlling access to and providing
\par
protection from unauthorized disclosure of information about transactions, intellectual property,
\par
and other sensitive information (Kauffman, Lee, Prosch, and Steinbart 2011). In contrast, privacy
\par
is an individual's right to decide whether or not to disclose personal information without
\par
incurring the risk of unwanted social controls by others (Shapiro and Baker 2001). Companies
\par
generally provide data confidentiality and privacy protection from two perspectives: individual
\par
level and organization level. Companies need policies that keep accounting data confidential, e.g.,
\par
no distribution of the accounting data, requiring encryption of all company data that is stored on
\par
personal devices, etc. On the other hand, companies should also respect customers' privacy
\par
preferences. For example, companies should protect the privacy of customers' personal
\par
information and purchase behaviors.
\par
Accounting researchers acknowledge that privacy is an issue in data analytics; however, little
\par
work has been done to date on the subject (Cao et al. 2015; Vasarhelyi et al. 2015; Yoon et al. 2015;
\par
Zhang et al. 2015). Further, data analytics techniques can reveal hidden information in data. For
\par
example, association rule mining attempts to discover relationships between products by finding out
\par
which products are usually purchased together by customers (Tan et al. 2005). Recommender
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015Infer, Predict, and Assure: Accounting Opportunities in Data Analytics 733
\par
systems seek to predict preferences that customers would express for a product they had not yet
\par
considered. Companies should maintain the confidentiality of this information to protect customer
\par
privacy. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ4.4: What are best practices for maintaining privacy and confidentiality with data analytics
\par
output?
\par
Finally, smaller organizations are more likely than larger organizations to outsource data
\par
analytics work. Thus, these smaller entities could have a more critical need to consider privacy and
\par
confidentiality issues that arise when outsourcing data analytics work. This discussion leads to:
\par
RQ4.5: What policies should be formulated for outsourcing data analytics, particularly
\par
regarding data privacy and confidentiality?
\par
SUMMARY AND LIMITATIONS
\par
Data analytics involves complex procedures that extract useful knowledge from large data
\par
repositories (Jans et al. 2014). Compared with conventional approaches, data analytics offer
\par
advantages in terms of cost-effectiveness (Vasarhelyi 2013), scalability, and capability to identify
\par
new patterns in real time. Several challenges and risks may arise with data analytics. First, how can
\par
voluminous data stored in heterogeneous and differently organized data sources (e.g., legacy
\par
systems, web, scientific data repositories, sensor and stream databases, and social networks) be
\par
converted into structured, hence well interpretable, format? In doing so, uncorrelated data needs to
\par
be filtered out. The challenge is to identify what data needs to be filtered out. Further, how can
\par
structured data repositories be managed, processed, and transformed in order to derive needed
\par
information for decision-making purposes? Finally, data analytics applications often are highly
\par
scalable.
\par
We expand upon these challenges and risks via adopting the organizing principles of the meta-
\par
theory of AIS and apply it to data analytics (Mauldin and Ruchala 1999). The first principle states
\par
that data analytics research should be task-focused. Our analysis concentrated on three tasks to
\par
which accountants often apply data analytics: infer, predict, and assure. The second organizing
\par
principle notes that task requirements are the start of the process that establishes the set of system
\par
design characteristics needed. We note that the lack of accepted models of data analytics and related
\par
perceptions is a significant challenge that should be considered. The third principle suggests that the
\par
impact of data analytics on task performance should be examined within the context of cognitive,
\par
technological, and organizational contingency factors. We identify several research questions
\par
related to each of these contingency factors. Finally, the fourth principle states that the outcome of
\par
data analytics is task performance. We discuss how evaluating the infer, predict, and assure tasks
\par
completed with data analytics may occur at either the individual or organizational level. In addition,
\par
often the outcome of data analytics contains private and/or confidential information and more
\par
research is needed to examine how organizations can address their responsibilities to maintain
\par
privacy and confidentiality.
\par
Research Limitations
\par
Although the goal of this paper is to examine how data analytics impacts the accounting
\par
environment and identify future research opportunities, our conclusions have limitations. First, we
\par
adopt a commonly used definition of data analytics; however, other definitions could change the
\par
analysis and conclusions. Second, our analysis follows the organizing principles of the AIS meta-
\par
theory model (Mauldin and Ruchala 1999) whereas using a different theoretical model may lead to
\par
different results and conclusions. Third, many of the tools and techniques used in data analytics are
\par
Accounting Horizons
\par
September 2015734 Schneider, Dai, Janvrin, Ajayi, and Raschke
\par
new and rapidly developing. As new technologies and approaches are introduced, our conclusions
\par
and recommendations may need to be modified and expanded.
