\par
1. Introduction
\par
Water resource managers and decision-makers are faced with
\par
many uncertainties when planning and managing water systems
\par
including changes in future population, per capita water demands,
\par
regulatory requirements, environmental standards, and climate,
\par
among others. These uncertainties impact both short-term opera-
\par
tional decisions (e.g. water allocation) and long-term adaptation
\par
decisions (e.g. infrastructure investment). Despite the inherent
\par
uncertainty in future conditions, water planners must decide how
\par
to plan and manage their water systems with the resources avail-
\par
able to them. This study addresses these issues through a pragmatic
\par
framework for rapid assessment of climate change vulnerability for
\par
water utilities. The framework is implemented in a novel web-
\par
based tool called Vulnerability and Risk Assessment Tool for Wa-
\par
ter Utilities (ViRTUE), which is designed for small-scale water
\par
utilities that may lack the nancial or technical resources to
\par
perform more detailed climate change risk investigations.
\par
Developing effective management strategies and adaptation
\par
actions that reduce risk to water resources requires an assessment
\par
of regional climate hazards on existing system infrastructure and
\par
operations (Mastrandrea et al., 2010). Climate risk assessment of
\par
water resource systems is a process for identifying and evaluating
\par
vulnerabilities that may threaten existing infrastructure and sys-
\par
tem performance. The process often involves a series of climate/
\par
weather models, rainfall-runoff models, and systems models to
\par
evaluate the impacts of climate change and variability on system
\par
functioning. Yet, this process can be time and resource intensive,
\par
especially for smaller utilities that often lack the ability to conduct a
\par
