\par
http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2015.08.001
\par
1364-8152/\copyright{} 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.full vulnerability analysis.
\par
In general, the water resources literature focuses primarily on
\par
large systems, with relatively few applications for small-scale sys-
\par
tems. Climate change studies are typically performed for large
\par
water resource systems that are capable of investing the time and
\par
resources necessary for such analyses (Horton et al., 2011; Kirshen
\par
et al., 2008; Lettenmaier et al., 1999). However, small water utilities
\par
may be most susceptible to climate change but do not have the
\par
means to assess system performance under future uncertainty.
\par
While potentially less equipped to perform computationally-
\par
intensive climate analyses, small systems may have more exi-
\par
bility, less institutional complexity, and greater adaptive capacity to
\par
cope with climate change than larger systems (Hamlet, 2011). The
\par
development of an easily accessible (i.e. web-based) climate
\par
vulnerability tool, designed for rapid assessment of climate risks to
\par
water resources systems, would encourage smaller utilities to
\par
identify and prepare for potential vulnerabilities in the future.
\par
The need for screening-level, computer-based models and tools
\par
to integrate knowledge and provide support in decision-making
\par
and management is supported by the scienti c literature
\par
(Anderson et al., 2004; Borowski and Hare, 2006; Chapra, 1991;
\par
Welp, 2001). However, few software packages exist that are inex-
\par
pensive, simple to use, and provide these services to small water
\par
utilities. One exception is the U.S. Environmental Protection
\par
Agency's (USEPA) Climate Resilience Evaluation and Awareness
\par
Tool (CREAT) designed to help the water sector assess regional and
\par
local climate-change impacts. This desktop-based tool leads utili-
\par
ties through a self-directed exploration of potential climate change
\par
related risks and adaptation options (Travers, 2010). In contrast, a
\par
simple, web-based tool for assessing climate vulnerabilities of
\par
water systems may provide advantages such as ease of use,
\par
accessibility, collaboration, instant modi cations, and wide avail-
\par
ability (Byrne et al., 2010). A web-based screening-level tool would
\par
also help narrow the persistent gap between knowledge produc-
\par
tion and tool use by removing software dependencies, simplifying
\par
scenario testing, and providing a user-friendly interface (Lemos
\par
et al., 2012). Finally, this tool is designed to employ the decision-
\par
scaling methodology (Brown, 2010; Brown et al., 2011, 2012;
\par
Whateley et al., 2014), a vulnerability-led alternative to the GCM
\par
projection-led assessment process employed in CREAT.
\par
Recent advances in web standards, browser performance, and
\par
free and open-source software (FOSS) present a promising new
\par
avenue for developing web-based tools that are more user-friendly
\par
and accessible than traditional desktop software (Swain et al.,
\par
2015). These advances in web technologies have transformed the
\par
implementation, design, and deployment of decision support sys-
\par
tems (DSS) (Bhargava et al., 2007; Booth et al., 2011; Sun, 2013).
\par
Decision support systems provide users with computer-based tools
\par
(i.e. models and data processing capabilities) that help support
\par
complex decision-making and encourage interactive problem
\par
solving (Salewicz and Nakayama, 2004). In the last decade, web-
\par
based approaches to DSS software have increased the accessibility
\par
of decision-making tools to individuals without extensive modeling
\par
experience.
\par
The use of web applications for environmental modeling is
\par
becoming more common in the literature (Goodall et al., 2011;
\par
Walker and Chapra, 2014). For example, Walker and Chapra
\par
(2014) developed an interactive web application, WIRM, with a
\par
rapid screening model for investigating potential water quality
\par
impairments due to biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) discharges.
\par
The WIRM tool gives users the ability to interactively adjust pa-
\par
rameters for rapid evaluation and visualization of the relationships
\par
between parameter values and model output. As another example,
\par
Goodall et al. (2011) present an application for a service-oriented
\par
computing (i.e. where software systems are interconnected to
\par
S. Whateley et al. / Environmental Modelling \& Software 73 (2015) 64e75 65
\par
allow for community and multidisciplinary modeling) for modeling
\par
water resource systems using web services. Speci cally, their study
\par
seeks to develop standards and procedures for data gathering,
\par
processing, and visualization of hydrologic simulation models on
\par
the web with the objective of more robust and effective imple-
\par
mentation design. Yet, many modern web technologies such as
\par
these require prior knowledge of and experience with standard
\par
web languages (HTML, JavaScript, and CSS), making the web
\par
development process inaccessible to many researchers and
\par
practitioners.
\par
In more recent years, the development of new web frameworks
\par
offers an opportunity to create web applications directly from
\par
common scienti c languages such as R and Python. This further
\par
increases the accessibility of scienti c research and modeling tools
\par
because researchers can create web applications based on pro-
\par
gramming languages they are already familiar with, and without
\par
needing to become experts in web development. This paper pre-
\par
sents a web-based tool developed using the Shiny web application
\par
framework (RStudio, Inc) for the R statistical computing language
\par
(R. C. Team). Shiny allows users with no web development skills to
\par
create interactive and fully-featured web applications written
\par
entirely in the R language. Using Shiny, the application developer
\par
can write both the front-end (i.e. client-side) user interface and the
\par
back-end (i.e. server-side) computational engine using familiar R
\par
functions and syntax. Shiny automatically converts the user inter-
\par
face code into standard web languages (HTML, CSS, and JavaScript)
\par
that can be run in any modern web browser. Shiny also handles
\par
clienteserver communications for passing application inputs and
\par
outputs between the user and the server, facilitating rapid devel-
\par
opment of interactive user interfaces.
\par
Recently, publications of Shiny web applications have appeared
\par
in a wide range of scienti c elds, including a web-based mapping
\par
application for precision agriculture (Jahanshiri and Shariff, 2014),
\par
the development of a data exploration tool for microbial commu-
\par
nities (Beck et al., 2014), an interactive web application to assist in
\par
knowledge elicitation about water requirements of oodplain and
\par
wetland vegetation (Guillaume and Fu, 2013), and a web server for
\par
predicting transcriptional regulatory modules (Liu and Miranda-
\par
Saavedra, 2014). The role of Shiny in all of these applications is to
\par
take complex scienti c concepts and present them through an
\par
intuitive and user-friendly graphical interface. The Shiny web
\par
application framework thus offers a promising new method for
\par
developing decision support applications in the water resources
\par
community. In particular, the server side framework, which offers
\par
reactive expressions that automatically regenerate output data and
\par
gures when changes are made to the input (Wan and Hudak,
\par
2000), allows developers to create interactive web applications
\par
that are well suited for self-directed climate risk assessment of
\par
water resource systems.
\par
To the authors' knowledge, there are no web-based tools
\par
designed for exploring water supply system performance under
\par
climate change. This study addresses this gap by presenting a web-
\par
based tool that uses a vulnerability-based framework to rapidly
\par
assess climate change and other impacts on small water supply
\par
utilities (i.e. serving populations of 250,000 or less). This tool also
\par
demonstrates a new approach for enabling researchers and prac-
\par
titioners to create web-based modeling software that can be
\par
programmed entirely within the R programming language. Section
\par
2 introduces traditional methods of assessing climate impacts on
\par
water supply systems and describes the vulnerability-based
\par
framework used in ViRTUE to allow water utilities to interac-
\par
tively evaluate risks to their systems. Section 3 describes the
\par
development approach, model theory, and work ow used in
\par
ViRTUE. Section 4 illustrates an application of the tool in a case
\par
study of a water supply system in the northeastern United States66
\par
