\par
and compares the vulnerabilities identi ed using ViRTUE with
\par
output from an independent simulation model of the case study
\par
system. Section 5 describes outreach and feedback on the appli-
\par
cation and utility of the tool for small-scale water supply systems
\par
in the northeastern United States. Sections 6 and 7 end the paper
\par
with a discussion and conclusion of the tool's contributions to the
\par
water resource and environmental modeling communities.
\par
2. Climate risk assessment methodologies
\par
2.1. Scenario-based climate risk assessment
\par
Traditionally, water supply impact studies evaluate system
\par
performance by combining downscaled Coupled Ocean-
\par
Atmosphere Global Climate Models (OA/GCM) with rainfall-runoff
\par
models and reservoir operations models to predict future climate
\par
risk (Rajagopalan et al., 2009; Wiley and Palmer, 2008; Wilby and
\par
Dessai, 2010). These top-down or scenario-based approaches use
\par
projected climate change scenarios to evaluate system
\par
performance.
\par
Top-down approaches undertaken for the purposes of making
\par
adaptation or operational decisions tend to propagate signi cant
\par
errors, generating large uncertainty ranges in climate impacts and
\par
system risk (Dessai, 2009). For example, the inherent uncertainty in
\par
GCM projections related to initial condition ensembles (Deser et al.,
\par
2012), climate forcings (Stainforth et al., 2005), and model in-
\par
adequacies due to poorly understood climate physics and compu-
\par
tational complexity (New and Hulme, 2000) make it dif cult to
\par
incorporate information from these scenarios into adaptation de-
\par
cisions (Stainforth et al., 2007).
\par
Given these concerns, alternative methods of climate risk
\par
assessment have emerged that build from the concepts of decision
\par
theory and scenario planning. Rather than suggest a single, best-
\par
guess future, these methods attempt to incorporate the concept
\par
of robustness into water resources planning and design, selecting
\par
strategies that perform well across a range of generated scenarios
\par
(Lempert and Collins, 2007; Ray et al., 2013; Watkins and
\par
McKinney, 1997). These methods include Info-Gap Decision The-
\par
ory (Ben-Haim, 2001), Robust Decision Making (RDM) (Lempert
\par
et al., 2006; Lempert and Groves, 2010), Robust Optimization (Ray
\par
et al., 2013; Watkins and McKinney, 1997), Real Option analysis
\par
(de Neufville et al., 2006), Decision-Scaling (Brown, 2010), and the
\par
scenario-neutral approach (Prudhomme et al., 2010). Such`bottom-
\par
up' approaches are designed to identify system vulnerabilities over
\par
a range of plausible future conditions to aid in selecting robust
\par
adaptation strategies.
\par
2.2. Decision-scaling: a vulnerability-based framework
\par
Decision-scaling is a bottom-up methodological framework
\par
which inverts GCM-led approaches to climate risk assessment by
\par
evaluating system performance over a range of climate futures
\par
independent of any assumed probabilities (Brown et al., 2011).
\par
Rather than evaluate vulnerabilities among a small set of
\par
future climate projections as generated by the GCMs, the decision-
\par
scaling method involves systematically exploring a virtually un-
\par
limited number of future scenarios to reveal system vulnerabilities
\par
by using a stochastic weather generator. The process is
\par
generally referred to as a climate stress test (Brown and Wilby,
\par
2012). Multiple sources of climate information (i.e. GCM pro-
\par
jections, paleoclimate reconstructions, and subjective climate in-
\par
formation) can be used to evaluate risks associated with the
\par
vulnerabilities identi ed (Brown, 2010; Brown et al., 2011). This
\par
methodology uses a decision analysis framework to characterize
\par
the future climate so that climate scenarios are derived from
\par
the decision at hand. Similar to other robustness-based ap-
\par
proaches, decision-scaling de nes robust adaptation strategies as
\par
those that perform acceptably over a range of future uncertainty
\par
(Steinschneider et al., 2014; Whateley et al., 2014; Moody and
\par
Brown, 2013).
\par
In this study, the decision-scaling framework is embedded in a
\par
web-based tool designed for water utilities in the northeastern
\par
United States. In recognition of limitations in projecting the future
\par
climate, the tool uses decision-scaling to tailor the analysis to focus
\par
on the future climate states that pose the greatest threat to system
\par
performance and estimates probabilities associated with those
\par
decision-relevant climate states (Brown and Wilby, 2012). This re-
\par
duces the computational time and resources necessary for analysis,
\par
and permits rapid identi cation of vulnerabilities to climate
\par
change. It is particularly well suited for small utilities, which
\par
comprise small spatial areas and thus are not well served by coarse
\par
resolution GCM projections.
\par
3. ViRTUE: Vulnerability and Risk Assessment Tool for Water
\par
Utilities
\par
The Vulnerability and Risk Assessment Tool for Water Utilities
\par
(ViRTUE) is a web application for assessing risks to small-scale
\par
water supply systems in the northeastern United States (avail-
\par
able at https://virtue.shinyapps.io/myapp). While ViRTUE is
\par
currently designed for the Northeast U.S. in terms of data avail-
\par
ability and vetted hydrologic models, in principle it is fully
\par
generalizable to other regions. The tool provides a mechanism to
\par
understand and explore individual water utilities climate risk
\par
exposure using a stress test, in which the performance of local
\par
reservoir systems is tested over a wide range of potential climate
\par
and socioeconomic changes. The components and work ow of the
\par
application are illustrated in Fig. 1. This section will describe the
\par
development approach, model theory, and interface/work ow of
\par
ViRTUE.
\par
3.1. Development approach
\par
ViRTUE was developed using the Shiny web application frame-
\par
work for the R programming language. R is a free and open source
\par
statistical programming language that is becoming increasingly
\par
popular among environmental modelers and scientists (Muenchen,
\par
2013). Traditionally, converting a simulation model or statistical
\par
analysis to a web application required substantial knowledge of
\par
standard web languages (HTML, CSS, and JavaScript) in order to
\par
construct the user interface. This requirement presents a challenge
\par
for scientists and engineers who are not familiar with modern web
\par
languages or development practices. The Shiny web development
\par
package provides a powerful framework allowing researchers to
\par
write web applications using only R functions and syntax. Because
\par
Shiny converts the R source code into HTML/JS/CSS automatically,
\par
the developer can create an entire interactive web application in R
\par
(see Interactive Web Apps with shiny Cheat Sheet for a template of
\par
the software architecture1). Although knowledge of HTML, CSS, and
\par
JavaScript is not required, Shiny also provides the exibility to
\par
incorporate these languages to create more advanced and innova-
\par
tive web features. For example, ViRTUE incorporates an open-
\par
source JavaScript library for interactive maps, called lea et,2
\par
which enables the user to click on a map to de ne the location of
\par
their reservoir system.
