3.2. Model theory
\par
The stress test approach used in ViRTUE begins with the gen-
\par
eration of monthly time series of precipitation and temperature
\par
using a stochastic weather generator. The weather generator is a
\par
stochastic model for generating synthetic time series of climate
\par
variables. It allows exploration of user de ned climate changes,
\par
including changes in means and variability that are plausible yet
\par
not sampled by GCM projections (Steinschneider and Brown, 2013;
\par
Stainforth et al., 2007). While the use of climate change projections
\par
can reveal vulnerabilities to the projections that happen to be used,
\par
this approach reveals vulnerabilities to speci c climate changes.
\par
The monthly weather generator in ViRTUE couples a wavelet
\par
decomposition with an autoregressive model of annual precipita-
\par
tion to account for low frequency climate oscillations in the
\par
Northeast (Steinschneider and Brown, 2013). A k-nearest neighbor
\par
resampling approach is then used to disaggregate the stochastically
\par
generated annual precipitation time series to a monthly time step,
\par
preserving the covariance structure between weather variables.
\par
The weather generator is trained using historical data from a
\par
gridded observed meteorological dataset covering 1949e2010
\par
(over a 1/8 grid cell space) (Maurer et al., 2002). Multiple climate
\par
realizations (i.e. fty time series of monthly precipitation and
\par
temperature) are generated to account for internal climate vari-
\par
ability (i.e. the natural uctuations in the climate system that arise
\par
in the absence of external forcings).
\par
Linear trends in temperature and precipitation are applied to
\par
each variability realization to simulate transient climate change.
\par
This approach allows plausible climate change space to be effec-
\par
tively and exhaustively explored. Currently the changes in climate
\par
that can be explored in the tool are limited to percent changes in
\par
mean annual precipitation and absolute changes in mean annual
\par
temperature from historic values for the region of interest (i.e. the
\par
latitude and longitude coordinates of the water supply reservoir).
\par
Exploration of variability changes are also possible but have not
\par
been incorporated yet.
\par
The monthly weather variable time series, adjusted to represent
\par
climate changes, are then used as input to a lumped-parameter
\par
hydrologic model to estimate monthly stream ow. This hydrolog-
\par
ic model is adapted from the`abcd' model developed by Thomas
\par
(1981). The original`abcd' model was modi ed to account for the
\par
in uence of snow accumulation and melt on hydrologic processes
\par
in the northeastern United States, which introduces a fth
\par
parameter (e) and is commonly referred to as the`abcde' model
\par
(Steinschneider et al., 2012; Martinez and Gupta, 2010). The abcde
\par
model is calibrated to historic stream ows within ViRTUE using the
\par
shuf ed complex evolutionary algorithm (SCE), a probabilistic
\par
global optimization method designed for parameter estimation in
\par
conceptual rainfall-runoff models (Thyer et al., 1999; Duan et al.,
\par
1992). This model was chosen for use in ViRTUE because of its
\par
parsimonious nature (i.e. few parameters) and geographic and
\par
hydrologic compatibility in the Northeast.
\par
The output of the hydrologic model is then used as input for a
\par
simple, reservoir systems model that follows`standard' operating
\par
policies (i.e. meet a release target if suf cient water is available,
\par
otherwise release all available water in the current time step) so
\par
that it is generalizable to any system (Loucks et al., 2000). The
\par
reservoir model is designed for the analysis of a single reservoir
\par
system, however, multiple reservoirs can be lumped together for a
\par
crude assessment of total system risk. In practice, standard oper-
\par
ating policies are used primarily for planning purposes. Alterna-
\par
tively, the user can select a hedging option that imposes pre-
\par
speci ed operating policies that reduce releases in times of
\par
drought (i.e. when reservoir levels drop below drought severity
\par
thresholds) to explore system performance under operational
\par
practice. Most operators adopt hedging policies to save water in the
\par
reservoir for future releases in case there is an extended period of
\par
low in ows (Loucks et al., 2000). In ViRTUE, hedging policies are
\par
adapted from the Spring eld Water and Sewer Commission's
\par
(SWSC) drought severity index curves, which are typical of small68
\par
systems (Dresser, 2005; Westphal et al., 2007).
\par
3.3. Tool workflow
\par
The decision-scaling framework embedded in ViRTUE leads a
\par
user through a self-guided, six-step process arranged as a series of
\par
tabbed panels on the user interface. In the rst three steps of the
\par
process, the user performs a climate stress test of their system to
\par
identify vulnerabilities based on a wide range of potential climate
\par
change scenarios (as described above). After performing the stress
\par
test, the user is presented with additional information regarding
\par
the probabilities of these scenarios based on GCM output in order
\par
to assess the risk of not meeting water supply demands. Note that
\par
these GCM projections are best viewed as subjective probabilities of
\par
future climate change. Ultimately they categorize the climate
\par
change projections in terms of whether they indicate problems for
\par
the utility or not. A more formal approach for developing climate
\par
change probabilities is in development, although they will neces-
\par
sarily remain subjective probabilities (Steinschneider et al., 2015).
\par
ViRTUE is designed to be used by stakeholders and water
\par
managers without the need for external support from scientists or
\par
engineers by providing guiding instructions through each step of
\par
the analysis. In addition, users can download the results of each
\par
step in the analysis and save key gures for their records and for use
\par
in climate reports. The following sub-sections describe each of the
\par
six steps of the analysis in detail.
\par
3.3.1. Step 1: choose location
\par
In the rst step of ViRTUE, the user speci es the location of their
\par
reservoir system by clicking on an interactive map. The location
\par
information (i.e. latitude/longitude) is used to retrieve historical
\par
climate data for that location to create synthetic future climate time
\par
series using the weather generator described in Section 3.2. Climate
\par
changes are imposed on one of the stochastically generated
\par
weather realizations, chosen at random from the fty total re-
\par
alizations created. As a result, for each iteration of the tool a slightly
\par
different result will emerge based on the randomly selected
\par
weather realization chosen for that analysis. After all climate
\par
change scenarios are created, time series of historic monthly pre-
\par
cipitation (mm) and temperature ( C) from 1949 to 2010 are pre-
\par
sented to the user.
\par
3.3.2. Step 2: generate streamflow
\par
In the second step, the user provides historical ow data to
\par
calibrate the rainfall-runoff model described in Section 3.2. The
\par
historical ow data can be provided as either direct in ows to the
\par
reservoir or measured stream ow from a nearby monitoring gage.
\par
If ows are taken from a nearby gage station (Qgagedt), the tool uses a
\par
simple drainage area ratio method to scale the volume of water
\par
coming into the system (Arch eld and Vogel, 2010).
