\par
In addition to evaluating system performance based on historical
\par
climate conditions, the user can also interactively explore the impact
\par
of incremental changes in climate and other variables on system
\par
performance using slider bars on the user interface. Adjustments
\par
that can be made include changes in mean annual temperature
\par
(0e5 C at 0.5 intervals), changes in mean annual precipitation
\par
(75e125\% of the historic mean at 5\% intervals), changes in mean
\par
annual demand (0e200\% of the historic mean at 5\% intervals),
\par
additional storage capacity (0e200\% at 10\% intervals), and addi-
\par
tional minimum ow (0e300 MGM at 5 MGM intervals). Although
\par
most of this paper focuses on climate risks to water systems, the
\par
allocation of limited water supplies to meet both human and
\par
ecological needs remains a challenge for small utilities. As such, the
\par
capability to explore changes in population (demand) and regula-
\par
tory policies (minimum ow requirements) is included in ViRTUE.
\par
Lastly, there are two simple system alternatives that can be
\par
explored. The rst alternative is increasing reservoir size by
\par
adjusting an additional storage capacity option. This alternative
\par
approach requires signi cant capital investment. The second
\par
alternative strategy is to alter operating policies, which requires
\par
much less investment by a utility. Clicking the'Hedge' checkbox of
\par
ViRTUE imposes restrictions on water supply releases during
\par
droughts (see Section 3.2) beyond what the standard operating
\par
rules would predict. Implementing release rules provides a more
\par
realistic depiction of system performance since`standard' oper-
\par
ating policies are not often followed in practice.
\par
3.3.4. Step 4: climate change projections
\par
In step 4, ViRTUE provides information about the distributions
\par
of changes in mean precipitation (\%) and mean temperature ( C)
\par
based on an ensemble of GCM projections (RCP emission scenario
\par
4.5) from the World Climate Research Programme's (WCRP's)
\par
Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 5 (CMIP5) multi-S. Whateley et al. / Environmental Modelling \& Software 73 (2015) 64e75 model dataset. Gridded simulated data was downscaled to a
\par
monthly temporal resolution and 0.125 spatial resolution based on
\par
the bias-correction spatial disaggregation (BCSD) statistical
\par
downscaling method (Reclamation, 2013). This tab allows users to
\par
visualize the range of climate changes (centered around 2050)
\par
projected for the region where their reservoir system is located. The
\par
GCM output is speci c to the location of the user's system as
\par
speci ed in step 1. The GCM projections allow utilities to better
\par
assess the likelihood of climate risks identi ed through the stress
\par
test. The projected changes are displayed as a histogram re ecting
\par
the range and frequency of the climate changes represented by the
\par
ensemble of climate change projections. The purpose of this step is
\par
to illustrate the kinds of climate changes that a representative set of
\par
projections indicates for their location.
\par
3.3.5. Step 5: stress test results
\par
In step 5, an overview of the results from the climate risk
\par
assessment is presented to the user. This overview is shown as a
\par
climate response surface of water supply reliability. A climate
\par
response surface is a representation of system performance (i.e.
\par
contours of system reliability) across climate change space (e.g.
\par
changes in annual mean temperature and precipitation). In this
\par
69
\par
display the climate response surface is divided into regions of
\par
`acceptable' and`unacceptable' system performance according to a
\par
user-speci ed reliability threshold level. The climate change space
\par
encompasses the full range of mean changes in precipitation and
\par
temperature that can be explored in step 3.
\par
The user can adjust the reliability threshold, which changes the
\par
areas de ned as acceptable and unacceptable. Additionally, GCM
\par
projections are superimposed on the climate response surface to
\par
illustrate the distribution and range of the projections relative to
\par
the impacts (in terms of reliability) that such changes would have.
\par
In this way the projections are put into the context of their impli-
\par
cations for the system. However, rather than simply learning
\par
whether projections indicate risks or not, this visualization allows
\par
the user to determine which climate changes cause hazards,
\par
whether those changes are sampled by the projections or not. For
\par
instance, if a water utility only has trouble meeting demands when
\par
mean precipitation decreases and the GCM projections show only
\par
increases in mean precipitation in the future, they may conclude
\par
that their system is at low risk of failure.
\par
3.3.6. Step 6: climate risk
\par
In the nal step, the overall risk in terms of acceptable/
