6. Discussion
\par
Two key ndings emerge from this analysis. First, a new web-
\par
based tool, ViRTUE, was successfully created using the Shiny web
\par
framework for small-scale water supply utilities to assess risks to
\par
their systems, where ordinarily such analyses would not be
\par
feasible. With very few inputs (see Fig. 1) the simple, screening
\par
level vulnerability assessment in ViRTUE yields time series of his-
\par
toric climate, system reliability, storages, and in ows for a range of
\par
climatic and demand conditions. In addition, an ensemble of
\par
climate projections are provided, with results showing where in
\par
climate change space a system is vulnerable and the fraction of
\par
climate projections that suggest acceptable/unacceptable system
\par
performance according to a threshold of reliability. From these
\par
results, a water supply manager may choose to take action to better
\par
prepare for potential future changes that pose a risk to system
\par
performance, whether those changes include climate change,
\par
changes in demand, or additional minimum ow requirements. If
\par
their system exhibits signi cant vulnerabilities, a more in depth
\par
analysis of risks may be warranted.
\par
The performance of ViRTUE was evaluated by application to a
\par
representative water supply system in the Northeast, the Spring-
\par
eld Water and Sewer Commission's (SWSC) water supply system.
\par
The results from the screening-level analysis of ViRTUE provide the
\par
same message regarding climate risk to the system as the results
\par
found using the system-speci c simulation model of the Spring-
\par
eld system (Fig. 8), and thus demonstrate the value of the tool for a
\par
vulnerability assessment of small systems. Future versions of theS. Whateley et al. / Environmental Modelling \& Software 73 (2015) 64e75 73
\par
Fig. 7. Fraction of climate projections that suggest acceptable/unacceptable system performance.
\par
tool will also illustrate changes in internal variability within the
\par
same iteration.
\par
While this study presents an application of ViRTUE to a water
\par
supply system in the Northeast, the components of the tool can be
\par
adjusted for use in other regions of the world. To do so would
\par
require some minor adjustments, i.e., adjusting the parameters of
\par
the weather generator to account for low frequency variability in
\par
the region, uploading historical climate data for the region to the
\par
server, evaluating the performance of the`abcd' hydrologic model
\par
to ensure it is a suitable structure for many catchments in the re-
\par
gion (if not, it could be replaced with a new hydrologic model), and
\par
uploading GCM projections for the speci c region of interest to the
\par
server. It is important to note, however, that using ViRTUE in re-
\par
gions that are data-sparse may be more dif cult because of the
\par
need for long annual precipitation time series in estimating pa-
\par
rameters in the wavelet decomposition. While the tool is
\par
