\par
generalizable to other regions, it should be tested on a case-by-case
\par
basis.
\par
The second nding is that the Shiny web framework, as
\par
demonstrated by ViRTUE, introduces a relatively new approach to
\par
web application development that allows modelers to focus less on
\par
speci c web technologies, and instead focus on converting their
\par
existing models and analyses into interactive web applications. As
\par
such, it is a valuable resource for individuals with limited web
\par
development skills looking to make models and tools more acces-
\par
sible to the water resources community. There is demand for more
\par
decision-making tools in water resources, however, they are often
\par
dif cult to come by because of the divergent skill sets of scientists,
\par
engineers, web developers and decision makers. This paper dem-
\par
onstrates the use of the Shiny web framework to bridge that gap,
\par
allowing for collaborative development of web tools that can be
\par
coded in the widely-used and free R statistical computing language.
\par
Web-based tools of this nature offer opportunity for more dynamic
\par
and collaborative water resource management.
\par
7. Conclusion
\par
representatives at regulatory agencies (Massachusetts Division of
\par
Ecological Restoration, Massachusetts Water Works Association
\par
(MWWA), Executive Of ce of Energy and Environmental Affairs
\par
(Boston, MA), and the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)) who
\par
worked with us during the tool's development. The authors also
\par
thank two anonymous reviewers and the editor for their valuable
\par
insights and suggestions, which contributed to improving this pa-
\par
per. The views expressed in this manuscript represent those of the
\par
authors and do not necessarily re ect the views or policies of
\par
NOAA.
\par
The study was motivated by the belief that small water utilities
\par
may be vulnerable to climate change but lack the resources to
\par
assess their risks. A web-based climate risk assessment tool, ViR-
\par
TUE, was designed to help water utilities, particularly small-scale
\par
operators with less time and resources to invest in such studies,
\par
to identify vulnerabilities to changes in climate, demand, and
\par
environmental ow regulations. ViRTUE provides utilities the op-
\par
portunity to perform a self-directed vulnerability analysis through
\par
an easy-to-use and widely-accessible platform. A web-based plat-
\par
form offers many advantages, such as wide availability, user
\par
accessibility, instant modi cations, and the removal of software
\par
dependencies. There are also some disadvantages of using the web
\par
for such analyses including security vulnerabilities (i.e. concern for
\par
putting sensitive system speci cations online), over simpli cation
\par
of system attributes made to support web-based simulation, and
\par
slower analyses than desktop tools due to network traf c and
\par
downloading time (Byrne et al., 2010). Overcoming these potential
\par
challenges with web-based simulation is vital for widespread use
\par
and acceptance of web-based tools in the water resources
\par
community.
\par
The tool presented in this study is designed to assist water
\par
managers confronted with the potential challenges of climate im-
\par
pacts through a screening level risk assessment of water supply
\par
systems. Ultimately, decision-makers must address water resources
\par
management under uncertain future climate and socioeconomic
\par
changes. With no nancial consequences and minimal time in-
\par
vestment, utilities can interactively explore the vulnerabilities of
\par
their system and begin to assess the potential investments they
\par
may need to make in the future. The application of this tool will be
\par
particularly potent in developing countries, where utilities often
\par
lack alternative modeling tools. For small-scale water supply sys-
\par
tems, a web-based tool may be the only option to assess system
\par
vulnerabilities under future uncertainty for their planning and
\par
management.
