Introduction
\par
Conservation planning for migratory species is challenging because not only must one
\par
account for multiple factors---anthropogenic risks, species' benefits to society, biological
\par
importance and policy, budgetary constraints, and other social considerations---but these
\par
issues must be considered across multiple locations and possibly between several countries.
\par
Making decisions that aim to balance multiple objectives can be challenging (Kenney 2002).
\par
A range of approaches have been developed for prioritizing where, how, when, and why
\par
conservation actions should be taken (Wilson et al. 2009). These structured decision analytic
\par
approaches can help identify optimal courses of action in a transparent fashion. Further, a
\par
decision analytic approach offers the opportunity to learn and explicitly address uncertainty
\par
(Nichols et al. 2011). One such structured approach, multi-attribute utility functions, can be
\par
used to reconcile tradeoffs among multiple conservation objectives (Ciarleglio et al. 2009).
\par
Such tools have been used in a variety of conservation contexts to prioritize land-acquisition
\par
decisions (Andelman and Willig 2002; Carwardine et al. 2010; Wilson et al. 2009; Wilson
\par
et al. 2007). Similarly, extensive work has been done on the role of ecosystem services in
\par
conservation planning (Boykin et al. 2013; Edwards et al. 2010; Egoh et al. 2011; Nelson
\par
et al. 2009). However, only a small number of studies have examined the role of ecosystem
\par
services in the conservation of migratory species (Dobson 2009; Johnson et al. 2011; Kunz
\par
et al. 2011; Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. 2013; Semmens et al. 2011; Vanoye-Eligio 2012; Whelan
\par
et al. 2008). Here we use a multi-attribute utility function to consider population viability,
\par
pest suppression services, ecotourism, and anthropogenic risks to prioritize which Mexican
\par
free-tailed bat maternity roosts to conserve. To our knowledge, this study, Dobson (2009),
