\par
and Vanoye-Eligio (2012) are the first conservation planning studies to consider threats to
\par
population viability and ecosystem services.
\par
Mexican free-tailed bats winter in central and southern Mexico, where they disperse
\par
throughout the landscape (Villa and Cockrum 1962). Early each spring, bats migrate north,
\par
forming large maternity roosts in the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico (Davis et al.
\par
1962; Federico et al. 2008; Villa and Cockrum 1962). Ten percent of females and ninety
\par
percent of males remain in southern Mexico and do not migrate from the winter habitat to
\par
the summer breeding roosts (Federico et al. 2008; McCracken and Gassel 1997). Although
\par
most individuals return to the same maternity roost, individuals have been known to
\par
change roosts over time (Glass 1982; McCracken 2003).
\par
There is growing concern about threats to Mexican free-tailed bat maternity roosts in
\par
North America (Kunz et al. 2011; Mickleburgh et al. 2002). In the U.S. and Mexico, guano
\par
mining and the burning or dynamiting of roosts has led to the total loss of several maternity
\par
roosts (McCracken 1987; McCracken 1989). Organochlorine pesticides such as DDT may
\par
be harmful to insectivorous bats as they can acquire high pesticide loads from their diets
\par
(McCracken 1987). Pesticides are thought to have caused a sharp population decline at the
\par
roost in Carlsbad Caverns, although there may have been multiple contributing factors
\par
(McCracken 1987; McCracken 1989). Protecting maternity bat roosts offsets these threats
\par
and helps conserve and sustain bat populations (Medellin 2003).
\par
Mexican free-tailed bats provide several ecosystem services throughout their range
\par
(Federico et al. 2008; Ga \textasciiacute ndara Fierro et al. 2006; Villa and Cockrum 1962). For example, bats
\par
emerge nightly from their maternity roosts to forage on insects and several major agricultural
\par
insect pests up to 50 km away from their roosts (Lee and McCracken 2005; Williams et al.
\par
1973). The total annual cotton pest-suppression services provided by Mexican free-tailed bats
\par
across all maternity roosts in the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico has been valued at
\par
\$11.67 million USD (Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. 2014). Besides reducing the need for regional
\par
insecticide use and suppressing pest populations, Mexican free-tailed bats may also hedge
\par
against the rise of resistance among pests to transgenically modified Bt (Bacillus thuringi-
\par
ensis) crops (Federico et al. 2008; Liu et al. 2014; Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. 2014). In addition to
\par
providing pest suppression services, several large Mexican free-tailed bat maternity roosts in
\par
the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico are important for ecotourism (Bagstad and
\par
Wiederholt 2013; Davis et al. 1962; Federico et al. 2008; Villa and Cockrum 1962).
\par
Only one other study has determined conservation priorities for bat roosts taking into
\par
account the level of ecosystem services provided (Vanoye-Eligio 2012). This study ranked
\par
roosts according to several factors for each site: bat ecosystem services provided, con-
\par
servation status, bat diversity and number of threatened species, and potential value for
\par
education, economic benefit, or ecotourism (Vanoye-Eligio 2012). In contrast to our study
\par
focusing on Mexican free-tailed bats across the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico,
\par
this research was conducted in in a more restricted geographic range, northeast Mexico,
\par
and considered a range of bat species present at each site. This study did also not consider
\par
different conservation scenarios that may alter conservation priorities.
\par
To guide conservation efforts, we have developed a multi-attribute utility function to
\par
rank the most important Mexican free-tailed bat maternity roosts in the southwestern U.S.
\par
and northern Mexico. We evaluated each roost based on five attributes: the maternity
\par
roost's contribution to population viability, the pest suppression ecosystem services to the
\par
surrounding area provided by the bats residing the in roost, the ecotourism value of the
\par
roost, the risks posed to each roost structure, and the risks posed to the population of bats
\par
residing in each roost. We then tested four conservation scenarios to determine how
\par
prioritizing different attributes can impact management and conservation strategies.
\par
12366 Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82
\par
Methods
\par
We considered all known major maternity roosts with greater than 50,000 individuals in
\par
the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico. We evaluated each roost based on five
\par
attributes: the maternity roost's contribution to population viability, the pest suppression
\par
ecosystem services to the surrounding area provided by the bats residing the in roost, the
\par
ecotourism value of the roost, the risks posed to each roost structure, and the risks posed to
\par
the population of bats residing in each roost. To evaluate the biological importance of
\par
individual maternity roosts, we used data from a migratory network model which calcu-
\par
lated migratory routes from overwintering grounds to maternity roosts and simulated
\par
changes in migratory population size associated with the loss of any particular maternity
\par
roost (Wiederholt et al. 2013). For the pest suppression ecosystem services of bat maternity
\par
roosts, we used data from a valuation study of the cotton pest suppression services of
\par
maternity roosts across the southwestern United States (Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. 2014),
\par
expanding the analysis to consider several maternity roosts in northern Mexico. Our
\par
assessment of the ecotourism values of the maternity roosts drew upon annual visitation
\par
estimates from bat-viewing sites and a wildlife viewing model (Bagstad and Wiederholt
\par
2013; Loomis et al. 2008). We surveyed biologists with expertise in Mexican free-tailed
\par
bats to determine the risks to maternity roost structures and to the populations of indi-
\par
viduals residing in those roosts.
\par
We tested four conservation scenarios to determine how prioritizing different attributes
\par
can impact the management and conservation strategy. In the first scenario, all factors
\par
(roost importance, pest suppression, ecotourism, and risks posed to roosts) were equally
\par
weighted. In the second scenario, we weighted the biological importance or contribution to
\par
population viability of the bat roosts higher than the other attributes. Finally, the third and
\par
fourth scenarios prioritized the ecotourism and pest suppression services, respectively,
\par
provided by the bats occupying the roost. We hypothesized that the set of maternity roosts
\par
with the highest rankings will vary according to the conservation scenario.
\par
Biological importance attribute
\par
We considered all known major maternity roosts of Mexican free-tailed bats in the
\par
southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico (Fig. 1). We developed a database of roost
\par
locations and population abundances by combining data from a U.S. Geological Survey
\par
database (Ellison et al. 2003), our own literature search, and unpublished data from
\par
Mexican free-tailed bat experts (co-authors PC, GM, RM, and AR). We considered only
\par
the largest maternity roosts with greater than 50,000 individuals because these roosts tend
\par
to be permanent, long-lasting structures such as caves, bridges, mines, tunnels, dams, and
\par
crevices, and are more likely to have reliable location estimates (McCracken 2003; O'Shea
\par
et al. 2003). We also excluded impermanent structures such as vegetation, nest boxes,
\par
sinkholes, and buildings (Lewis 1995). By eliminating the smaller roosts from the model,
\par
we have excluded less than 1 \% of the overall count total of bats in our database. Because
\par
the combined populations of the largest summer colonies are thought to account for most
\par
of the migratory population of Mexican free-tailed bats (McCracken 2003), focusing on the
\par
dynamics of only the major maternity roosts should provide a reasonable estimate of the
\par
conservation needs to the entire bat population. Because of concerns that bat populations
\par
may have declined through the decades of the 1950s and 1960s, presumably due to DDT
\par
exposure (Betke et al. 2008), we only used abundance estimates obtained after 1970. In all,
\par
123Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82 67
\par
our model dataset consisted of 25 major maternity roosts containing a total estimated
\par
population of about 22.8 million individuals (Fig. 1).
\par
To evaluate the biological importance of individual maternity roosts, we used a
\par
migratory network approach developed by Taylor \& Norris (2010) applied to Mexican
\par
free-tailed bats (Wiederholt et al. 2013). This approach allowed us to calculate expected
\par
migratory routes based on distance-based mathematical formulations of migration costs,
\par
and to simulate changes in migratory population size associated with the loss of any
\par
particular maternity roost. The network model simulated migration between four over-
\par
wintering regions in southern Mexico (in the states of Chiapas, Hidalgo, Quere \textasciiacute taro, and
\par
Michoaca \textasciiacute n-Jalisco) and the 25 major summer maternity roosts in northern Mexico and the
\par
southwestern U.S. In the overwintering grounds, bats disperse across the landscape rather
\par
than aggregate in large roosts as they do in the summer breeding region (Villa 1956). As
\par
such, for the purposes of the network model the geographic location of overwintering
\par
region was based either on a known major roost or the geographic midpoint of all major
\par
roosts in the winter region (Wiederholt et al. 2013).
\par
Each migratory route was weighted with a survival cost derived from its length
\par
(Rayfield et al. 2011; Taylor and Norris 2010)---individuals traveling on longer routes were
\par
assumed to have lowered survival. This reflected the high mortality rates and poor body
\par
conditions reported for bats during migration (Constantine 1967). Longer migrations may
\par
increase mortality rates due to increased exposure to inclement weather and predators, and
\par
the increased difficulty of locating roosts (Constantine 1967). Mexican free-tailed bats
\par
likely use multiple stopover sites during migration. In our model, migratory routes only
\par
connected winter habitat to summer roosts. Unfortunately, due to limited data on the
\par
specific routes bats use when migrating between summer and winter habitats, we were
\par
unable to model stopover sites. As such, migratory edges represented the shortest distance
\par
between end points, not the actual course traveled.
\par
To examine the entire population's dependence on each roost, we quantified the impact
\par
(i.e. population decline) of each roost's removal on the total summer population size
\par
(Rayfield et al. 2011; Taylor and Norris 2010; Urban et al. 2009). We simulated the
\par
removal of each roost with replacement from the network model; thus only one roost was
\par
removed at a time from the model. When removing a given roost from the model, the bats
\par
were not ``killed,'' rather the individuals that would have migrated to the removed roost
\par
were allowed to disperse to other maternity roosts. Population declines for each roost were
\par
standardized on a scale from zero to ten, with zero indicating no decline and ten repre-
\par
senting the greatest population decline. Wiederholt et al. (2013) contains more details on
\par
our network modeling approach.
\par
Pest suppression ecosystem service attribute
\par
We employed an avoided-cost approach originally used by Cleveland et al. (2006) to
\par
estimate the value of bat services in reducing crop damage and pesticide use on cotton. We
\par
used data from the Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. (2014) assessment of cotton pest suppression
\par
across the southwestern U.S. and Mexico, which includes all cotton-producing areas near
\par
known major maternity roosts of Mexican free-tailed bats.
\par
Estimating the avoided costs of crop damage involved the following steps: (a) esti-
\par
mating the number of bollworms (a major cotton pest) consumed nightly by individual
\par
bats, (b) determining the hectares of cotton fields within proximity to bat maternity roosts,
\par
which allowed us to estimate (c) the value of the crops that would have been damaged in
\par
the absence of bats.
\par
12368 Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82
\par
Fig. 1 Major Mexican free-tailed bat maternity roosts and overwintering regions
\par
Conventional and molecular analyses show that moths comprise 30--60 \% of the bats'
\par
diet and indicate that each reproductively active female bat can consume 5--10 female adult
\par
bollworms (Helicoverpa zea) per night during periods of peak bollworm infestation (Lee
\par
and McCracken 2005). Since bollworms also infest other crops in the area or migrate out of
\par
the region, we conservatively estimated that only 10--20 \% of the female moths consumed
\par
each night (approximately 1.5 individuals per bat) would have dispersed into cotton and
\par
laid eggs (Cleveland et al. 2006). Due to high mortality rates during insect development
\par
(95--98 \%), the nightly consumption of 1.5 adult female moths per bat would prevent five
\par
larvae from developing and damaging cotton crops (Cleveland et al. 2006).
\par
We used data from the U.S. Department of Agriculture's National Agricultural Statistics
\par
Service (www.usda.nass.gov), the National Cotton Council (www.cotton.org), and the
\par
Mexican Department of Agriculture, S.A.G.A.R.P.A (http://www.siap.gob.mx/) on the
\par
number of cotton hectares planted per U.S. county or Mexican municipality. We estimated
\par
that, conservatively, the bats' nightly foraging distance from their roosts is 50 km (Wil-
\par
liams et al. 1973).
\par
To determine the value of reducing insecticide use, we calculated both the reduced
\par
private costs to farmers of applying insecticides, and the reduced cost to society of
\par
releasing fewer insecticides into the environment (Cornell University's Integrated Pest
\par
Management Program 2012; Kovach et al. 1992; Leach and Mumford 2008). Data on
\par
private costs of cotton insecticide applications from 1990 through 2008 were obtained from
\par
the Mississippi State University Department of Entomology and Plant Pathology's data-
\par
base on cotton losses due to insects (http://www.entomology.msstate.edu/resources/
\par
cottoncrop.asp) and S.A.G.A.R.P.A., the Mexican Department of Agriculture (http://
\par
www.siap.gob.mx/). We consider only the social costs of only those insecticides that
\par
specifically target bollworms (Helicoverpa zea) (Leach and Mumford 2008). We used data
\par
from Kovach et al. (1992) and from Cornell University's Integrated Pest Management
\par
123Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82 69
\par
Program (2012) to estimate the environmental and toxicological impacts of particular
\par
cotton insecticides. We then used a pesticide environmental accounting tool (Leach and
\par
Mumford 2008) to assign a social-cost value in dollars for each insecticide according to the
\par
degree of impact. Lo \textasciiacute pez-Hoffman et al. (2014) contains more details on our bat pest
\par
suppression evaluation. Pest suppression values per roost were standardized on a scale
\par
from zero to ten for the analysis, with ten being the highest pest suppression value.
\par
Ecotourism ecosystem service attribute
\par
We estimated the economic value of bat-related tourism for those maternity roosts that
\par
support bat viewing (Bagstad and Wiederholt 2013). In this study, we obtained annual
\par
visitation estimates through discussions with local resource managers. Although visitation
\par
occurs at some sites in Mexico, visitation data are unavailable for those caves (co-author
\par
RM, Universidad Nacional Auto \textasciiacute noma de Me \textasciiacute xico, pers. comm.). We used a wildlife
\par
viewing model from the Benefit Transfer and Use Estimating Model Toolkit (Loomis et al.
\par
2008). This model provides a meta-analysis of economic values for wildlife viewing,
\par
which we used to estimate consumer surplus for visitors to each site. We calculated the
\par
average daily consumer surplus (i.e. for eight hours) from wildlife viewing for each state
\par
where Mexican free-tailed bat viewing occurs. We then estimated each roost's distance
\par
from the nearest urban center. For roosts located within an urban area, we divided per-
\par
visitor consumer surplus by four, assuming the average visitor spends a total of two hours
\par
traveling to and engaging in bat viewing. For roosts beyond but less than two hours from an
\par
urban area, we assigned four hours of wildlife viewing value per visit, and for roosts
\par
greater than two hours from the nearest urban center, we assigned a full day's consumer
\par
surplus per visit (Bagstad and Wiederholt 2013). Ecotourism values per roost were stan-
\par
dardized on a scale from zero to ten for the analysis, with ten indicating the highest
\par
ecotourism value.
\par
Risk to maternity roosts attributes
\par
We assessed two types of risk: (a) to the roost structure itself and (b) to the roost population
\par
(i.e. the individual bats residing in the roost). To assess both types of risk, we surveyed 35
\par
bat biologists with expertise in Mexican free-tailed bat research in the region. Survey
\par
participants were given a list of 25 maternity roosts and asked to provide information for
\par
only those with which they had the most experience. The biologists were asked to score the
\par
risk of six different threats. We differentiated between risks to roost structure and risks to
\par
individuals because while protecting roost habitat may allay some threats, bats also face
\par
other direct threats while foraging outside the roost. The threats considered were devel-
\par
opment, disturbance (from authorized visitors), climate change, pollution, vandalism, and
\par
wind turbines. Respondents were asked to evaluate how these threats could affect the roost
\par
structure itself as well as the population of bats residing within the roost. Risk scores per
\par
roost were standardized on a scale from zero to ten, with zero representing the lowest risk
\par
and ten representing the highest risk.
\par
We pre-tested the survey first with a focus group of five bat biologists. Participants were
\par
invited via email to take the survey online using DatStat Illume software. The survey
\par
remained open for five weeks. For each roost and each threat, we calculated the mean of
\par
participants' response scores. We gave equal weight to each participant's answers because
\par
they had been asked to provide information about only those maternity roosts for which
\par
they had significant experience. In the U.S., we surveyed biologists representing
\par
12370 Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82
\par
universities, non-governmental organizations and the Bureau of Land Management (BLM),
\par
U.S. Geological Survey (USGS), U.S. Forest Service (USFS), and state natural resource
\par
management agencies. Additionally, we surveyed Mexican biologists including researchers
\par
from the Universidad Nacional Auto \textasciiacute noma de Mexico (UNAM), and the Instituto Polit-
\par
e
\par
\textasciiacute cnico Nacional (IPN). Overall, the bat biologists we surveyed had extensive experience
\par
working with bats (mean= 19.3 years) and with Tadarida species in particular
\par
(mean= 14.5 years).
\par
Multi-attribute utility function
\par
As described above, each maternity roost was given scores for five attributes: (1) biological
\par
importance, (2) pest suppression value, (3) ecotourism value, (4) risks to roost structure,
\par
and (5) risks to roost population. For each factor, the scores were standardized on a scale of
\par
zero to ten.
\par
We developed four scenarios for weighting the attributes. The scenarios were designed to
\par
reflect the factors that resource managers and decision-makers might consider when prior-
\par
itizing conservation actions (Table 1). The first scenario (equal weights scenario) contem-
\par
plates a situation where a manager might equally value all factors (i.e. all attributes were
\par
given equal weights). The second scenario (biological importance scenario) considers a
\par
situation where a roost's contribution to the population viability is valued above all. In this
\par
case, the biological importance attribute received a value of one (1), and the two ecosystem
\par
service attributed received a weight of zero (0). Because continued population viability is a
\par
function of the risks faced by the roost structure and the population residing within the roost,
\par
we also gave each of the two roost-risk attributes a full weight of one (1). The third and fourth
\par
scenarios (pest suppression scenario and ecotourism scenario) prioritize the ecosystem
\par
service values that that bat roosts provide. In each of these scenarios, the corresponding
\par
ecosystem service attribute (pest suppression or ecotourism) was given a full weight of one
\par
(1). In addition, the associated risks with potential to lower service values were also given a
\par
weight of one (1). For example, because pest suppression services are a function of numbers
\par
of bats foraging each night, risks that could lower the population numbers or compromise the
\par
roosting structure were given full weight (see Table 1 for details).
\par
The formula we used to determine each roost's score for each scenario was as follows:
