\par
Bayesian approach to estimate missing risk scores
\par
We use a Bayesian approach to address data gaps and uncertainty regarding parameter
\par
scores. The following maternity roosts were missing risk scores due to the respondents'
\par
lack of adequate experience with these roosts: Presa de Amistad in Tamaulipas, Mexico,
\par
Cuatrocienegas and Cueva del Consuelo in Coahuila, Mexico, Devil's Sinkhole, Fern
\par
Cave, Stuart Bat Cave, and Waugh Bridge in Texas, Merrihew Cave, Reed Cave, and
\par
Vickery Cave in Oklahoma, and Orient Mine in Colorado. As a group, there was no
\par
systematic difference between these roosts and the other roosts for which we had risk
\par
scores. The roosts for which data was missing covered a wide geographic range, were
\par
found in both countries, varied in size from 100,000 to 2 million individuals, represented a
\par
variety of structures (bridges, caves and mines), and were located in both urban and
\par
agricultural areas. Because there was no systematic bias between the roosts for which we
\par
did and did not have risk assessments, we believe a Bayesian approach to be a robust
\par
means of estimating the missing risk scores.
\par
We used a Bayesian approach to estimate the risk scores for the roosts which lacked
\par
data based on the risk scores of the roosts for which we had data. Our Bayesian prior
\par
distributions were normal distributions described by the standard deviation and the mean of
\par
the observed risk scores. We calculated total scores (U) given both uncertainty in the
\par
parameter weights (a) of our four scenarios and missing data for risk scores (v). We
\par
calculated posterior probabilities for the parameters using the procedure described above.
\par
Sensitivity analysis of ranking results to the attribute weights in the scenarios
\par
We evaluated the sensitivity of the roost ranking results to the weighting schemes in the
\par
four different scenarios. In the first scenario, which emphasized biological importance, we
\par
assessed the sensitivity of the resultant roost rankings to the weights used for both risk
\par
factors. We did so by varying the risk weights from zero to one. For the third and fourth
\par
scenarios, which prioritized pest suppression and ecotourism, respectively, we assessed the
\par
sensitivity of the resultant roost rankings to the weight given to the population importance
\par
of the roost. We did so by varying the biological importance attribute from zero to one.
\par
Sensitivity analysis of survey responses to risk
\par
We tested the sensitivity of roost rankings to variability in the respondents' assessment of
\par
the risks posed to roosts. For each roost, we assessed the sensitivity of the result to the
\par
highest and lowest risk values reported across all respondents. Using the equal weights
\par
12372 Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82
\par
scenario, we tested high- and low-risk scores for both risk to the roost structure and risk to
\par
the bat population.
\par
Results
\par
Biological importance
\par
Our results indicated that the southernmost summering areas are the most important
\par
maternity regions for maintaining the bat population (Table 2, Fig. 1; results discussed in
\par
detail in Wiederholt et al. 2013). Several maternity roosts in this area (Bracken Cave, Frio
\par
Cave, and Devil's Sink Hole Cave in Texas; Cueva La Boca in Nuevo Leon; and Cueva del
\par
Tigre in Sonora) had the greatest summer population sizes, and the removal of these large
\par
roosts from the network model resulted in the largest overall population declines (Table 2).
\par
The declines were not caused by bats being ``killed'' as the roosts were removed, because
\par
the individuals that would have resided in the removed roost were allowed to migrate to
\par
other maternity roosts. Rather, removal of these large southern roosts caused individuals to
\par
migrate greater distances, which lowered survival, and increased the negative effects of
\par
density-dependence in the roosts that subsequently had increased population sizes. Wie-
\par
derholt et al. (2013) tested the robustness of these results to the migration survival
\par
parameter and found that when migration survival was increased, removing these five
\par
roosts still caused the largest population declines.
\par
Pest suppression ecosystem services value
\par
The roosts with the largest annual value pest suppression values were Bracken Cave, Texas
\par
(\$3.42 million), followed by Frio Cave, Texas (\$2.42 million). The highest annual values
\par
per roost ( [\$1 million) were all found in Texas (Table 2). The biological importance
\par
score of the roost was significantly correlated with the pest suppression value score
\par
(r = 0.67, p value \textbackslash{} 0.01).
\par
Ecotourism ecosystem services value
\par
The total annual ecotourism value of 11 viewing sites of Mexican free-tailed bats in the
\par
southwestern U.S. was conservatively valued at \$6.51 million (Bagstad and Wiederholt
\par
2013). The sites with the highest ecotourism value included the Congress Avenue Bridge,
\par
in Austin, Texas, and the Waugh Drive Bridge, in Houston---easily accessible urban areas
\par
with established interpretive programs---as well as a more remote national park, Carlsbad
\par
Caverns (Table 2). However, population importance score of the roost was unrelated to the
\par
ecotourism value score (r = -0.05, p value= 0.82).
\par
Risks to maternity roosts
\par
The results of the Markov chain Monte Carlo analysis indicated that the highest risks to
\par
roost structures were associated with Maviri Cave in Sinaloa, Mexico, followed by Davis
\par
and Frio Caves in Texas (Table 2). The roost structures at Frio and Davis Caves are
\par
threatened by vandalism, while Maviri Cave is threatened by roost disturbance (from
\par
authorized visitors). Our results indicated that the highest risks to bat populations were to
