\par
Multi-attribute utility function
\par
In the equal-weight scenario of the multi-attribute utility function, Bracken Cave, Congress
\par
Avenue Bridge, Frio Cave, and Carlsbad Caverns had the highest overall scores (15.5 to
\par
32.5; Table 3; Fig. 2). In the biological importance scenario (where higher weights were
\par
given to the biological importance of the maternity roosts and less to the ecosystem service
\par
provision), Bracken Cave, Frio Cave, and Congress Avenue Bridge were also the top three
\par
ranked maternity roosts, although the fourth ranked roost was Davis Cave, reflecting its
\par
biological importance. The pest suppression scenario maintained the same ranking for the
\par
top four maternity roosts as the biological importance scenario. This is because roost
\par
population size was correlated with the pest suppression (r = 0.67, p value \textbackslash 0.001). In
\par
the ecotourism scenario, the top ranked maternity roosts were, in order, Congress Avenue
\par
Bridge, Carlsbad Caverns, Davis Cave, and Bracken Cave. This indicates that the eco-
\par
tourism values of the Congress Avenue Bridge and Carlsbad Caverns were important
\par
components of their overall scores, while the pest suppression services of Frio and Bracken
\par
Caves were more significant components of their scores.
\par
There were several unexpected rankings. For example, Davis Cave ranked relatively
\par
highly in the ecotourism scenario, and Maviri highly in the pest suppression scenario
\par
despite the fact that they do not provide high ecotourism or pest suppression values; this
\par
counter-intuitive result arises from their high scores for certain risk attributes that were
\par
considered in the scenario (Table 2). In most cases, however, roosts with no ecosystem
\par
service values were ranked low in these scenarios. In the ecotourism scenario, 11 out of 14
\par
roosts with no ecotourism values were placed in the lowest 14 ranked roosts, while in the
\par
pest suppression scenario seven out of nine roosts with no pest-suppression values were
\par
placed in the lowest 13 ranked roosts. Even though five roosts composed the four highest
\par
ranked roosts in each scenario, their individual rankings varied between scenarios, as did
\par
the rankings of other roosts with lower scores. In sum, our results lend support for our
\par
hypothesis that preferentially valuing different attributes (e.g. biological importance over
\par
ecosystem service value) will change the roost rankings.
\par
Sensitivity analysis of weights
\par
Our sensitivity analysis revealed that altering the weights of the risk factors in the bio-
\par
logical importance scenario did not alter the overall rankings for most maternity roosts
\par
(Appendix S1, Table S1). Altering the weight of biological importance attribute in the pest
\par
suppression scenario and ecotourism scenario lowered the rankings of the several roosts
\par
(Appendix S1, Table S2--3). Some of these maternity roosts had high ecosystem service
\par
values, and placing greater weight on their biological importance lowered their overall
\par
ranking. Thus, the rankings are sensitive to the weight placed on the biological importance
\par
of the roost. However, our scenarios either excluded this factor or gave it full weight (1),
