Sensitivity analysis of survey responses to risk
\par
The sensitivity analysis revealed that altering the survey responses did not greatly alter the
\par
roost rankings (Appendix S1, Table S4). Altering the roost structure risk scores to the
\par
lowest and highest individual responses shifted the rankings of four and six roosts,
\par
respectively. Altering the bat population risk scores to the lowest individual responses
\par
shifted the rankings for five roosts, while altering the risk scores to the highest individual
\par
responses shifted the rankings of seven roosts.
\par
Discussion
\par
We used a multi-attribute utility function to determine the most critical maternity roosts to
\par
protect to maintain the population viability and ecosystem services of Mexican free-tailed
\par
bats. Our multi-attribute utility function tool can help prioritize management actions under
\par
different conservation scenarios and can be easily adapted to other species and to include
\par
additional factors. The results support our hypothesis that preferentially valuing different
\par
attributes (e.g. population importance over ecosystem service value) will change the pri-
\par
oritization rankings for roost protection. For example, the results from the weighted sce-
\par
narios reveal that four out of the following five maternity roosts were ranked highly across
\par
all scenarios: Bracken Cave, Congress Avenue Bridge, Frio Cave, and Davis Cave in
\par
Texas; Carlsbad Caverns in New Mexico; and Cueva del Tigre in Sonora, Mexico
\par
(Table 3, Fig. 2). The preservation of these five roosts would help protect over [ 10.2
\par
million individuals. However, the individual ranking of these top five roosts varied
\par
123Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82 79
\par
between scenarios. Beyond these top five roosts, the roost rankings varied greatly between
\par
scenarios.
\par
Maternity roosts with high biological importance are more likely to provide high levels
\par
of pest suppression and have greater risks posed to their populations. It would be worth-
\par
while for conservation organizations to work with landowners to protect several roosts that
\par
are not formally protected, such as Davis and Frio Caves, which survey participants
\par
indicated were potentially threatened by vandalism or unauthorized visitors. Even pro-
\par
tected roosts face threats. For example, a housing development (composed of 3,800 units,
\par
personal comm. Merlin Tuttle) is planned to be sited less than 400 m from Bracken Cave,
\par
and there is concern that vandalism and roost disturbance will be inevitable given the
\par
proximity of such a large number of residents.
\par
The lack of independence within the scientific expert group we surveyed and our
\par
missing risk scores may be a shortcoming of our work. To obtain a reasonable under-
\par
standing of Mexican free-tailed bat roosts, we conferred with the most prominent members
\par
of a very small community familiar with the species. Small science communities can
\par
perpetuate complex forms of bias (Baddeley et al. 2004), resulting in a lack of indepen-
\par
dence (Burgman 2005). It is extremely difficult to guard against these community-wide
\par
biases (Kuhn 2012). Finding experts who are independent of one another and who have not
\par
worked with or benefited from the science of others within small expert communities is
\par
nearly impossible (Doswald et al. 2007). We believe, however, that our assessment of
\par
expert opinion provides the best insight possible given the lack of pertinent data on roost
\par
risks. We also had missing risk score data for several roosts; we used our Bayesian analysis
\par
to obtain estimates of these risks. However, these roosts did not differ systemically in terms
\par
of location or size from other roosts for which survey participants provided risk score data.
\par
Finally, our sensitivity analysis of survey responses to risk also indicated that the roost
\par
rankings were not greatly altered by variation in risk scores. Our Bayesian approach lends
\par
itself to updating of roost rankings should new information become available; coupled with
\par
the sensitivity analyses, our results point to areas where research would benefit the most
\par
from reducing uncertainty in the decision process.
\par
Comprehensive, region-wide management strategies for bats are critical, especially in
\par
the face of emerging, large-scale risks posed to their populations (Vanoye-Eligio 2012).
\par
For instance, Vanoye-Eligio (2012) found multiple risks posed to bat roosts across
\par
northeast Mexico; to ameliorate bat conservation efforts, they suggested several strategies
\par
including general public, environmental education programs focused on bats, and measures
\par
to prevent vandalism and unauthorized visitors in roosts. Our results reinforce this idea
\par
that, although roost protection is an important conservation measure, additional manage-
\par
ment actions are needed. Even though several of the roosts we identified as priorities for
\par
conservation do enjoy formal protection, the bat populations residing in those roosts still
\par
have significant threats. Mexican free-tailed bats are exposed to a variety of factors outside
\par
these roosts both during migration from overwintering sites in central and southern
\par
Mexico, and during their nightly foraging activities. For instance, climate change and wind
\par
turbines are emerging threats to bats. The U.S.-Mexico border region is predicted to
\par
become warmer and drier, with the potential for increased drought frequency (Intergov-
\par
ernmental Panel on Climate Change 2007). It is thought that under warmer and drier
\par
conditions, bats will experience increased water stress, which will compound the already
\par
high rates of water loss occurring during migration and potentially reduce survival (Adams
\par
and Hayes 2008; Popa-Lisseanu and Voigt 2009). In addition, wind turbines are thought to
\par
cause large numbers of fatalities to migrating bats, including Mexican free-tailed bats
\par
(Arnett et al. 2008; Cryan and Barclay 2009; Miller 2008; Piorkowski and O'Connell
\par
12380 Biodivers Conserv (2015) 24:63--82
\par
2010). The deployment of wind turbines for energy production has been increasing in
\par
North America and is expected to grow in the future. In 2011, Texas, which contains many
\par
important maternity roosts, had the highest production of wind energy of any state in the
\par
U.S. according to the U.S. Energy Information Administrations (2012). We note, however,
\par
that publically available data on bat fatalities from that state are lacking (Arnett et al.
\par
2008). To effectively tackle such large-scale risks, bat conservation and management
\par
planning will undoubtedly benefit from the involvement of a wide variety of stakeholders
\par
working across administrative and political boundaries.
\par
In summary, we developed a framework that ranks different habitats to inform man-
\par
agement decisions that can be easily adapted to other species. We found that placing higher
\par
values on different roost attributes (e.g. biological importance over ecosystem service
\par
value) can change the rankings of roosts, although five maternity roosts were among the
\par
four highest-ranked roosts across all scenarios. This indicates that the values we place on
\par
various conservation objectives can alter which habitats are determined to be optimal for
\par
preservation. Conservation strategies focused on landscape-level protection incorporating
\par
diverse groups of stakeholders are needed. We suggest that conservation efforts be focused
\par
on protecting the most highly ranked maternity roosts while also addressing region-wide
\par
factors that may threaten bat populations.
