\par
Given exponential growth in the size of big data, its multi-channel sources and variability in
\par
quality that create challenges concerning cost-effective use, firms have invested significantly
\par
in databases and analytical tools to inform decision-making. In this regard, one means to
\par
avoid the costs associated with producing less than insightful reports and negative effects on
\par
performance through wasted resources is prioritizing data in terms of relevance and quality.
\par
The aim of this study is to investigate this approach by developing and testing a scale to eval-
\par
uate Big Data Availability and the role of Big Data Prioritization for more effective use of big
\par
data in decision-making and performance. Focusing on the context of supply chain manage-
\par
ment (SCM), we validate this scale through a survey involving 84 managers. Findings support
\par
a positive association between Big Data Availability and its use in SCM decision-making, and
\par
suggest that Big Data Prioritization, as conceptualized in the study, has a positive impact on
\par
the use of big data in SCM decision-making and SCM performance. Through developing a
\par
scale to evaluate association between Big Data Availability and use in SCM decision-making,
\par
we make an empirical contribution to value generation from big data.
\par
\copyright{} 2020 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
To succeed in dynamic and competitive markets, organizations are challenged to respond to customer and market needs that
\par
require more targeted products and/or services, more flexible processes, improved co-ordination between suppliers and resources,
\par
together with cost management. To do so, managers need to integrate operational and strategic concerns with financial and non-
\par
financial information in order to make appropriate decisions (Bhimani and Willcocks, 2014; Mundy, 2010). Herein, traditional
\par
accounting information systems have lost some value relevance, with the completeness, relevance and timeliness of their infor-
\par
mation being debated (Johnson and Kaplan, 1987; Dontoh et al., 2004; Alles, 2015) as traditional accounting and auditing
\par
methods predominately provide lag indicators that inform budgeting and planning with a sense of what has happened (Kaplan
\par
and Norton, 1996), but lack the component of immediate, situational business intelligence required for future planning in a
\par
technology-intensive, service-oriented economy (Dontoh et al., 2004; Vasarhelyi, 2012; Chaudhry and Sam, 2018). In response,
\par
digitization is altering both the``dynamics [between strategy, structure and finance function activities] and the consequences
\par
for internal financial reporting, analysis and decision making'', with the realization that new forms of data may be significant
\par
and relevant sources of business intelligence (Bhimani and Willcocks, 2014, 470).
\par
