\par
1467-0895/\copyright{} 2020 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.2 C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
\par
Of increasing value are new forms of data, referred to as big data, which includes structured, unstructured, visual and social
\par
media data (Grover et al., 2018; Zhan and Tan, 2018; Tiwari et al., 2018) that can provide complementary evidence concerning
\par
the phenomena of interest (Yoon et al., 2015). As``high-volume, high-velocity and/or high-variety information assets that demand
\par
cost-effective, innovative forms of information processing\dots{} [to] enable enhanced insight, decision making, and process automa-
\par
tion'' (Gartner, 2019, 1), big data is widely regarded as an invaluable resource that can provide real-time information for mana-
\par
gerial and financial accounting, and business reporting in relation to improving firm performance (Warren et al., 2015). In
\par
contrast to``designed accounting systems'', which track economic transactions, big data can be a``significant \dots{} source of business
\par
intelligence'' (Bhimani and Willcocks, 2014, 475). For example, one study shows that firms ``in the top third of their industry in
\par
the use of data-driven decision making\dots, [are] on average, 5\% more productive and 6\% more profitable than their competitors''
\par
(McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012, 65). As business information is typically concerned with finance, operations, administration or
\par
compliance, big data offers a rich resource for``information of a non-directly financial nature such as human resources, produc-
\par
tion, marketing, supply chain, and R\&D'' (Vasarhelyi et al., 2015, 385), which complements organizations' accounting information
\par
(Bhimani and Willcocks, 2014). Thus, whilst big data is a key resource for effective and timely decision-making by accountants
\par
and executives, its unprecedented exponential growth (particularly of non-standard data) creates new challenges and opportuni-
\par
ties (Borthick and Pennington, 2017; Richins et al., 2017). In this regard, Business First Magazine (2016) predicts that by 2020 1.7
\par
megabytes of data will be created every second for every person on earth, in part by searches carried out by 6.1 billion smart
\par
phone users. By 2025, it is estimated that 463 exabytes of data will be created each day globally (Desjardins, 2019).
\par
Given big data's characteristics of volume, velocity and variety, processing it into information (Brinch, 2018; Schmarzo, 2013;
\par
Zhan and Tan, 2018) requires``use of advanced analytic techniques against very large, diverse data sets'' (IBM, 2019, 1; Inside Big
\par
Data, 2017). These techniques, termed Big Data Analytics (BDA), include``text analytics, machine learning, predictive analytics,
\par
data mining, statistics and natural language processing to gain new insights from previously untapped data sources independently
\par
or together with existing enterprise data'' (IBM, 2019, 1). As investment in these databases and tools is significant and costly
\par
(Schoenherr and Speier-Pero, 2015; Grover et al., 2018), with forecasts that big data and BDA revenues will increase 12\% over
\par
2018 figures, reaching \$274.3 billion in 2022 (International Data Corporation [IDC], 2019), there is need to ensure that organiza-
\par
tions define their related objectives and rationale for selecting tools. Whilst this growth suggests that firms see strong potential in
\par
the information that may be leveraged from big data, the performance outcomes that arise are contingent upon the use-value1
\par
received being greater than the associated exchange (monetary) value (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010). Thus, for positive firm
\par
performance, it is critical that the calculated gain (i.e., business value) justifies the investment in tools to mine, process, and un-
\par
derstand big data (M\"uller et al., 2018; Grover et al., 2018; Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010).
\par
Amid this deluge of data, and associated with big data's characteristic high-volume, high-variety and high-velocity, some data
\par
is likely to be irrelevant, inaccurate, or unreliable. Accordingly, selecting (or prioritizing) the big data to input into BDA in order to
\par
cost-effectively leverage useful information not only requires new technologies, but also new ways of thinking (Chen et al., 2014).
\par
Here, prior research has noted that``scientific evidence for the business value of BDA is scarce'' (M\"uller et al., 2018, 491), with
\par
``substantial differences in returns from BDA'' and IT-intensive and very competitive industries being the only ones to show mea-
\par
surable productivity improvements (M\"uller et al., 2018, 488). One means to improve BDA's cost-benefit effectiveness is to reduce
\par
the risk of analyzing irrelevant or inaccurate big data (Clarke, 2016; Brown-Liburd et al., 2015) by prioritizing data based on its
\par
relevance, quality and perceived value. For example, interrogating big data in terms of its generally agreed characteristics
\par
(i.e., Volume, Velocity and Variety) should enhance identification of which big data should be prioritized as being more effective
\par
inputs for BDA based on its quality (accuracy, relevance, consistency, timeliness, validity, appropriateness and completeness),
\par
source reliability and perceived value (Appelbaum et al., 2017a, 2017b; Hazen et al., 2014). Whilst this proposition responds to
\par
calls to address data quality (Hazen et al., 2014) and has been referenced in general terms (i.e., Schmarzo, 2013), the means of
\par
prioritizing big data is yet to be empirically explored.
\par
Consequently, the aim of this study is to investigate the role and performance implications of establishing additional selection and
\par
quality control criteria for big data as an input for BDA, a concept that we theorize as Big Data Prioritization (BDP). In conceptualizing
\par
BDP, we define its role as targeting relevant and good quality data (Tsai et al., 2015) in order to deliver information for decision-
\par
making in a cost-effective manner (Waller and Fawcett, 2013; Schmarzo, 2013). Our view of BDP aligns with the process-based
\par
value generation perspective for big data proposed by Curry et al. (2014) with his Big Data Value Chain framework (see also Curry
\par
et al., 2016; Lyko et al., 2016). This Value Chain depicts``the information flow within a big data system as a series of steps needed
\par
to generate value and useful insights from data'' (Curry et al., 2016, 36). The initial step is data acquisition, which involves``the process
\par
of gathering, filtering, and cleaning data before it is put in a data warehouse or any other storage solution on which data analysis can
\par
be carried out'' i.e., before data analysis, data curation, data storage and data usage (Curry et al., 2016, 39). Our focus on BDP is moti-
\par
vated by the emerging challenge for data, which concerns the mechanisms that``connect the data acquisition with the data pre- and
\par
post-processing (analysis) and storage, both in the historical and real-time layers'' (Lyko et al., 2016, 51). Thus, we position BDP as a
\par
step after data acquisition but before BDA. By controlling data inputs for BDA processes, BDP should theoretically enhance the value/
\par
cost-effectiveness of BDA processes and outputs related to the improved quality of information for accounting and other executives'
\par
decision-making. By investigating the role of BDP, our study addresses the call to investigate mechanisms to connect data acquisition
\par
with pre-processing (Lyko et al., 2016). Further, prior research has reviewed data processing and the use of data in decision-making
\par
(McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012; Davenport et al., 2012), demonstrating how the use of big data contributes to value creation in firms
\par
