\par
3
\par
(Wang et al., 2016; C\^orte-Real et al., 2017; Wamba et al., 2017; Gunasekaran et al., 2017). Accordingly, in the context of BDP being an
\par
input to BDA, we also consider the issue of Big Data Availability (BDAvail), which we define as the quality of big data being able to be
\par
used or obtained (Oxford Living Dictionary, 2019)2 and investigate the relationship between BDAvail, BDP and big data's use in man-
\par
agerial decision-making. Given the variety of settings in which big data and BDA are used, we focus on the context of supply chain
\par
management (SCM). As such, we explore the implications of BDAvail on BDP, and the effect of BDP on SCM decision-making and
\par
SCM performance, thereby addressing the call for empirical investigation of the constituent elements of the value proposition of
\par
big data (M\"uller et al., 2018; Grover et al., 2018).
\par
Results from our survey of 84 managers support a positive association between BDAvail and use of big data in SCM decision-
\par
making which, in turn, has a positive influence on SCM performance. Further, results suggest a positive association between
\par
BDAvail and BDP, with BDP having a positive influence on use of big data in SCM decision-making. The significance of BDP con-
\par
cerns enabling a balance between optimizing the amount and diversity of big data that requires processing for decision-making,
\par
and the costs associated with collecting and processing big data through BDA tools. Accordingly, through identifying the impor-
\par
tance of BDP as a control mechanism in managing available big data, this research contributes knowledge concerning positive out-
\par
comes arising from the use of big data in SCM contexts. Specifically, whilst prior literature has theorized the relevance of big data
\par
to SCM performance, this study provides empirical evidence that links increased BDAvail and use in SCM decision-making, with
\par
positive effects on SCM performance. However, our findings are limited, as further enhancement of the scale is required, partic-
\par
ularly with respect to two constitutive elements of the formative construct BDAvail (Variety and Value/Cost-effectiveness).
\par
Thus, our key contribution is as an empirical study that has initiated exploratory steps in developing a scale to appraise the
\par
value proposition concerning the use of big data for improved SCM decision-making and performance.
\par
The remainder of the paper consists of five sections. In Section 2, we present the theoretical background, research model and
\par
hypotheses, whilst in Section 3 we outline the research approach. In Section 4, we report analysis of the survey data, with findings
\par
discussed in Section 5. Section 6 presents the conclusions and limitations.
\par
2. Background
\par
2.1. Big data and its characteristics
\par
As defined above, big data describes data that has grown exponentially in volume and type (Gartner, 2019) beyond the capa-
\par
bility of traditional data methods associated with storage, processing, and analysis (Bihl et al., 2016; Appelbaum et al., 2017a,
\par
2017b). This growth is enabled by technologies such as Radio-Frequency Identification (RFID) scanners and Global Positioning
\par
System (GPS) sensors that create, collect, store and share volumes of assorted data (Warren et al., 2015), as well as growing
\par
use of smart phones, social media, and the internet (Bihl et al., 2016; Hofmann, 2017). To leverage big data and generate effective
\par
information, firms require strategies, including faster and more efficient use of BDA tools (Chen et al., 2014; Kambatla et al.,
\par
2014).
\par
In appraising big data as an input to BDA's information-generating processes, research initially typified it in terms of three core
\par
characteristics-- Volume, Variety, and Velocity (Gartner, 2019; McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012). More recently, Veracity and Value
\par
were added to reflect increased focus on data quality and required outcomes from BDA (Wamba et al., 2015; Arunachalam et al.,
\par
2018).3 These five characteristics are conceptualized as:
\par
{$\bullet$} Volume-- refers to``the magnitude of the data'' (Gandomi and Haider, 2015, 139) and its growth (Chen et al., 2014). The expo-
\par
nential growth in big data has necessitated similar increases in memory capacity, storage devices and databases (Wamba et al.,
\par
2015). Volume, variously quantified in terms of time, scope and counting records or files (Russom, 2011), is advantageous be-
\par
cause it enables numerous observations about specific topics (Hofmann, 2017; Rikhardsson and Yigitbasioglu, 2018).
\par
{$\bullet$} Variety-- is conceptualized as``the structural heterogeneity in a dataset ... [including] various types of structured, semi-structured,
\par
and unstructured data'' (Gandomi and Haider, 2015, 139). Structured data is well defined and organized in the form of spreadsheets,
\par
with clearly determined items presented in terms of time series (e.g., point of sales data). In contrast, unstructured data is typically
\par
replete with text such as audio, video, email or social media posts that offer potentially valuable insights. But as these either lack a
\par
predefined data model or are not organized in a predefined manner, this data is hard to search as it may lack the coherence required
\par
for technological analysis. Alternatively, semi-structured data (e.g., XML), despite not conforming to strict standards, is more readily
\par
analyzable (Wamba et al., 2015; Gandomi and Haider, 2015; Russom, 2011). Thus, Variety either signals or potentially enables the
\par
extraction of extra information in various forms from a variety of sources (Hofmann, 2017).
\par
{$\bullet$} Velocity-- is defined as a``firm's ability to successfully process data at a high speed'', possibly using real-time data as it is up-
\par
dated (Hofmann, 2017, 5110). Besides relating to the speed with which big data is created, transferred and processed (Wamba
\par
et al., 2015), real-time data is more reflective of natural outcomes, including trends or market updates (Gandomi and Haider,
\par
2015). Given the pace of change, big data offers a valuable, time-sensitive perspective of what is happening, thereby informing
\par
responses to changing circumstances as they occur (Hofmann, 2017; McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012).
\par
2 Oxford Living Dictionary (2019) defines availability as``The quality of being able to be used or obtained''. The resource is available at https://en.oxforddictionaries.
\par
com.
\par
3 It is acknowledged that big data is, at times, characterized by more`Vs' such as Volatility, Variability, and even Visualization, but due to the constraints of the study,
\par
the scope is limited to the five characteristics outlined above.4 C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
\par
{$\bullet$} Veracity-- As the``[i]nherent unpredictability of some data requires analysis\dots{} to gain reliable prediction'' (Wamba et al., 2015,
\par
236), there is need to ascertain accuracy and consistency of big data (Sivarajah et al., 2017; McAbee et al., 2017). This includes
\par
whether the collected data is truthful or relevant to a given situation (Gandomi and Haider, 2015; Janssen et al., 2017). Also, as
\par
big data increasingly becomes available from multiple sources, it is important to ascertain the trustworthiness of data sources
\par
(Arunachalam et al., 2018). In this study, Veracity is premised on the basis that identifying data that is true is more important
\par
than identifying data that is valid.4
\par
{$\bullet$} Value-- refers to``[t]he extent to which big data generates economically worthy insights and or benefits through extraction and
\par
transformation'' (Wamba et al., 2015, 236). As such, the focus concerns the overall cost-effectiveness of big data measured in
\par
terms of the monetary and non-monetary benefits gained compared to costs, regarding the quality and costs of alternative
\par
sources for decision-making (Gandomi and Haider, 2015). As an intangible resource (Brinch, 2018), big data's Value is evident
\par
when its use-value is greater than its exchange-value (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010). Compared to the traditional methods
\par
previously used in data management, it is now easier to collect, store and analyze big data (Bihl et al., 2016; Appelbaum
\par
et al., 2017a, 2017b), leading to benefits from big data's use, including utility, desirability and overall monetary value compared
\par
to cost (Rikhardsson and Yigitbasioglu, 2018; Brinch, 2018).
\par
To derive optimal value from big data, users are required to collect and analyze large volumes of data from a sufficiently di-
\par
verse range of sources, as well as gauge that the data is timely and of good quality for interpretability. As depicted in Fig. 1, this
\par
requires consideration of the balance between use-value and exchange-value of big data (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010; Brinch,
\par
2018).
\par
Thus, generating optimal value from big data is an issue for decision-making in a variety of fields, including accounting, oper-
\par
ations and SCM (Grafton et al., 2010; Wall and Greiling, 2011; Choi et al., 2018; Wamba et al., 2018). With most world trade
\par
(80\%) and global production (60\%) occurring within supply chains, their reliance on technological coordination (ITUC, 2016;
\par
Cheung et al., 2018) and their independencies mean that timeliness and reliability of data for management decision-making
\par
are important. Hence, supply chains were chosen as our context.
\par
2.2. Big data in supply chain management
\par
Profitable supply chains with agile and lean processes (Arunachalam et al., 2018) require carefully designed``product and pro-
\par
cesses \dots{} [as well as] marketing, accounting, and performance-tracking tools'' (Kleindorfer et al., 2005, 489). Globalization and
\par
competitive market forces have engendered increasing use of IT in SCM, enabling more automated systems (Manyika et al.,
\par
2011). As such, the use of big data in SCM relates to its leverage in informing decision-making concerning the development of
\par
new business strategies, identification of trends in costs and performance, and optimization of production and logistics
\par
(Hofmann, 2017; Hazen et al., 2014). Brinch (2018) theorizes this as a relationship between business problems and data, articu-
\par
lating it in terms of three stages-- discovery, creation, and capture (see Fig. 2). Specifically, his framework outlines how analytics
\par
can be mobilized to explore and exploit big data to enable discovery of potential sources of value and how value is created in key
\par
business processes through the use of big data in decision-making, ultimately resulting in value capture via economic improve-
\par
ments in efficiency or effectiveness. In achieving this, given that big data does not provide value unless applied in some process
\par
to produce a desired output (Sivarajah et al., 2017; Arunachalam et al., 2018), BDA plays a key role as it transforms big data into
\par
information that can be used in decision-making. Thus, in improving SCM performance, BDA requires accurate information in a
\par
timely manner that is relevant to identified accounting, operational and strategic activities (Jain et al., 2017; Wamba et al.,
\par
2017; Ro\ss mann et al., 2018; Vasarhelyi et al., 2015).
\par
In summary, as a result of technological capability, the amount of data available to firms is dramatically increasing, with
\par
greater inclusion of data related to external and historical factors (Hofmann, 2017; Arunachalam et al., 2018). In SCM, the use-
\par
value of big data derives from more accurate predictions, more informed strategic planning (Sivarajah et al., 2017; Rikhardsson
\par
and Yigitbasioglu, 2018) and more effective decision-making (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018; Hazen et al., 2014).
\par
2.3. Challenges with big data
\par
With respect to how BDA provides value for SCM (Wamba et al., 2017; Gunasekaran et al., 2017), current research focuses on
\par
data processing and use of data in decision-making (McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012), rather than the critical value of data inputs
\par
(Kambatla et al., 2014; Hazen et al., 2014). Yet there is relevance in focusing on the properties of big data itself, as big data is
\par
growing faster than the development of BDA tools (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018; Janssen et al., 2017). Prior research into use of big
\par
data in facilitating SCM decision-making typically consists of conceptual studies that focus on opportunities (Lamba and Singh,
\par
2017; Chen et al., 2014; Waller and Fawcett, 2013) rather than on how BDA might actually impact decision-making quality
\par
(Rikhardsson and Yigitbasioglu, 2018). Given this, there is merit in empirically investigating methods that might enhance the pos-
\par
itive effects of big data on decision-making and performance in SCM (M\"uller et al., 2018; Ro\ss mann et al., 2018). One way to
\par
achieve this is by focusing on the methods involved in managing the processing of big data (Zhong et al., 2016; Arunachalam
\par
et al., 2018), where concerns have been raised regarding data security, data privacy, the deluge of data, and data quality
\par
