\par
(Sivarajah et al., 2017; Hazen et al., 2014; Huerta and Jensen, 2017). In this study we focus particularly on two of these: data del-
\par
uge and data quality.
\par
2.3.1. Data deluge
\par
A simultaneous increase in Volume, Variety and Velocity of big data (see Fig. 1) creates data deluge, which generates data pro-
\par
cessing difficulties, and increased cost of extracting insights for SCM decision-making (Addo-Tenkorang and Helo, 2016; Janssen
\par
et al., 2017). Data deluge problems arise when the amount of big data generated by a firm's supply chain grows beyond the
\par
capabilities of available BDA tools (Chen et al., 2014; Bihl et al., 2016; Sivarajah et al., 2017). The effects of Volume present diffi-
\par
culties for data collection, including storage and processing (Chen et al., 2014), and data redundancy (Arunachalam et al., 2018),
\par
all of which negatively affect value due to the costs of capturing quality data from which to extract meaningful and timely infor-
\par
mation for effective decision-making, thereby creating value (Brinch, 2018). Similarly, the effects of Variety are manifest in the
\par
proliferation of perspectives, with consequential difficulties with interpreting conflicting perspectives. Further issues arise as in-
\par
corporating larger amounts of big data may not always deliver better signal saliency due to the amount of noise in the dataset
\par
(Sivarajah et al., 2017) that requires removal (Janssen et al., 2017). This requires analytics professionals to be involved in analyz-
\par
ing and interpreting big data, as BDA requires advanced statistical and programming skills (McAbee et al., 2017; Waller and
\par
Fawcett, 2013). Thus, despite Volume and Variety providing benefits that address statistical concerns regarding data, the deluge
\par
associated with Volume and Variety may, due to system limitations, affect the Velocity of data processing. Hence, data deluge af-
\par
fects representation of the phenomena of interest, including possible uncertainty regarding big data's credibility and validity,
\par
which require evaluation prior to use (McAbee et al., 2017).
