\par
2.3.2. Data quality
\par
With the increasing complexity of big data, monitoring quality (accuracy, relevance, consistency, timeliness, validity, ap-
\par
propriateness and completeness) and source reliability becomes a crucial task in order to achieve effective managerial
\par
decision-making (Hazen et al., 2014; Arunachalam et al., 2018). Erroneous or inaccurate big data provides contrary value to
\par
decision-makers due to wasted effort and resources (Janssen et al., 2017; Arunachalam et al., 2018). In particular, it is esti-
\par
mated that 95\% of big data is unstructured, originating from sources such as social media or online store websites that contain
\par
useful information such as customer feedback (Wamba et al., 2015; Gandomi and Haider, 2015). Here, technologies that cap-
\par
ture this data may cause issues with format and/or consistency, especially in consolidating data from different sources (Zhong
\par
et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2016) where problems may arise regarding relevance, comprehensibility and bias (Waller and
\par
Fawcett, 2013; Huerta and Jensen, 2017).
\par
Thus, although the exchange-value of big data is realized through improvements in decision-making, its use-value and quality
\par
as a source of information are``not solely dependent on the [quality of the] data, but also on the process in which the data is col-
\par
lected and the way data is processed'' (Janssen et al., 2017, 338). As challenges to data processes and data products are yet to be
\par
fully addressed in the literature (Hazen et al., 2014; Brinch, 2018), we seek to address this need by empirically examining some of
\par
the issues and challenges related to the use of big data in SCM decision-making. In particular, we argue that prior to processing
\par
big data, SCM should prudently ensure its quality and relevance in order to optimize its use and value (Sivarajah et al., 2017). If
\par
big data lacks relevance, reliability or timeliness, decision-makers may encounter difficulties with interpretation, leading to poten-
\par
tially negative consequences (Hazen et al., 2014; Sivarajah et al., 2017). Given the issues caused by data deluge and data quality
\par
(Sivarajah et al., 2017; Russom, 2011), that relate to big data's increasing Volume, Variety and Velocity (Arunachalam et al., 2018;
\par
McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012), strategies to improve rationalization before applying advanced analytic techniques (BDA) appear
\par
advantageous. Thus, to maximize the value from data as a source of information in decision-making, we investigate the concept of
\par
BDP and its role in assuring the quality of big data for BDA.
\par
2.4. Big data prioritization
\par
Two theories inform our premise that to manage data deluge and quality, it is necessary to prioritize certain types of big
\par
data used in information processing and decision-support. Information Processing Theory relates the effectiveness (quality
\par
and performance outcomes) of decision-making to a firm's capability to meet its information processing needs through suffi-
\par
cient levels of information processing capacity (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018). Concurrently, Information Theory suggests that to
\par
achieve efficient storage and benefits from information, it is necessary to shed superfluous and/or redundant data (M\"uller
\par
et al., 2018).
\par
Thus, we theorize that in light of business objectives, and whilst considering associated trade-offs, BDP is a mechanism by
\par
which to prioritize certain data types over other data, thereby controlling data inputs by determining what big data should be
\par
collected and/or stored as an input for BDA. In this manner, BDP should positively contribute to improving the use/value of big
\par
data in SCM decision-making. Moreover, drawing on exchange-value (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010), BDP may be regarded as
\par
a method to address data deluge and data quality issues through prioritizing big data into refined data inputs that display desir-
\par
able characteristics and qualities (Sivarajah et al., 2017; Arunachalam et al., 2018).
\par
In theorizing the role of BDP, we draw upon Rational Choice Theory and Utility Theory. In accord with Rational Choice Theory
\par
(Zey, 2001; Burns and Roszkowska, 2016), managers are deemed to be rationally-minded, basing their decisions on economic
\par
benefit (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010). Given this, we argue that managers involved in SCM are concerned with finding the
\par
best means to a chosen end i.e., managers make choices to achieve objectives such as improved performance or profit maximiza-
\par
tion (Wang and Ruhe, 2007; Burns and Roszkowska, 2016). Further, by assuming that managers are aware of costs and potential
\par
benefits of available big data, we argue that they evaluate both costs and benefits of applying BDA to big data to achieve an out-
\par
come that maximizes a utility or value function (Briggs, 2015). This utility, according to Utility Theory, is measured in terms of the
\par
potential monetary and non-monetary payoffs that provide worth or desirability (Briggs, 2015). In the context of big data, we
\par
posit that utility is defined in terms of Volume, Variety, Velocity, Veracity and Value/Cost-effectiveness related to data quality
\par
(McAfee and Brynjolfsson, 2012; Russom, 2011; Wamba et al., 2015; Sivarajah et al., 2017). As managers make choices in deter-
\par
mining what big data is needed, it is presumed that they are aware of the associated risks and consider some choices as undesir-
\par
able in terms of relevance, availability, and/or interpretability (Arunachalam et al., 2018).
\par
In theorizing and investigating the role of BDP in SCM, we address a research gap which, to the best of our knowledge, has
\par
not been examined in prior studies. Whilst we acknowledge that some research shows how BDA can make big data inputs
\par
more visually appealing for SCM decision-making (Chen et al., 2014; Wamba et al., 2015), we also note an important distinc-
\par
tion. We posit that BDP, as a subset of BDA, should assist with rationalizing big data prior to BDA through its role in enabling
\par
the attribution of differential weightings concerning how big data inputs to BDA correspond to the intended purpose of data
\par
use in decision-making. Thus, by using an identification structure that accords with the characteristics of big data, BDP con-
\par
cerns filtering out data that is less relevant prior to applying specific BDA procedures and decision processes (Wang and
\par
Strong, 1996; Hazen et al., 2014; Gunasekaran et al., 2017). Therefore, in accord with these two theories, BDP is defined as
\par
the process of controlling and prioritizing big data in terms of relevance and quality to optimize use-value and exchange-value
\par
(Wang and Strong, 1996; Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010; Arunachalam et al., 2018). From a practical standpoint, our aim
\par
concerns providing SCM with a mechanism to address growing issues with data deluge and data quality in a manner that
\par
maximizes utility.C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
