\par
2.5. Development of the research questions
\par
Whilst conceptual papers discuss the benefits of big data in the context of SCM decision-making (Zhong et al., 2016; Janssen
\par
et al., 2017), limited research has looked at the resources managed by BDA in SCM (Barbosa et al., 2018). As SCM requires in-
\par
formed decision-making regarding demand, production, customer relationships and delivery (Brinch, 2018; Barbosa et al.,
\par
2018), big data is a key resource for improving forecasts, plans and related decisions (Zhong et al., 2016; Lamba and Singh,
\par
2017). Whilst larger firms have the capacity to invest in BDA tools to process and use big data on a greater scale than smaller
\par
firms, the pay-off and utility from big data should benefit smaller firms in similar ways (Radanliev et al., 2019; Sen et al.,
\par
2016). Accordingly, we argue that since all firms similarly recognize and seek to access the benefits of big data, the relevant
\par
issue is availability. In this context, as previously defined, BDAvail is concerned with the quality of big data being able to be used
\par
or obtained. Further, in accord with prior research's identification of big data's five characteristics-- Volume, Velocity, Variety, Ve-
\par
racity and Value/Cost-effectiveness (Roden et al., 2017; Brinch, 2018)-- we characterize these as five constitutive elements of
\par
BDAvail. Thus, to investigate the association between BDAvail and the use of big data for BDA in SCM decision-making, our
\par
first research question is as follows:
\par
RQ1. To what extent is Big Data Availability (BDAvail) associated with the use of big data in SCM decision-making?
\par
Further, we aim to investigate the implications of BDAvail on BDP, and BDP on the use of big data in SCM decision-making. In
\par
Section 2.4 we theorized that the more big data is available to decision-makers, the more firms need to perform controls on the
\par
inflow of big data (BDP) in order to determine the data inputs that should be used and processed in BDA to generate information
\par
for SCM decision-making. In so doing, BDP addresses two issues with big data, namely data deluge and poor data quality. More-
\par
over, BDP should enable use of more targeted big data in SCM decision-making, because BDP's purpose is to identify big data with
\par
greater value creation potential for SCM decision-making. Whilst no prior empirical studies document a relationship between
\par
BDAvail and BDP, and between BDP and the use of big data in SCM decision-making, we theorize that BDP should play a positive
\par
role in filtering and optimizing big data for BDA prior to use in SCM decision-making. In other words, we predict that with an
\par
increase in BDAvail, the relevance and need for BDP will also increase, to assure appropriateness of data inputs for BDA. Thus,
\par
in exploring the relationship between BDAvail and BDP, our second research question is:
\par
RQ2. To what extent is Big Data Availability (BDAvail) associated with Big Data Prioritization (BDP)?
\par
As discussed previously, we theorize that the more BDP is performed, the greater the likelihood that value is discovered in the
\par
use of big data (Brinch, 2018) and, consequently, the greater the likelihood of big data being used in SCM decision-making as a
\par
value creation mechanism. Herein SCM decision-making is defined in terms of managing demand forecasting (Wang et al., 2016;
\par
Jain et al., 2017), which facilitates production decisions (Croxton et al., 2001; Zhong et al., 2016) that subsequently have effects on
\par
optimizing delivery logistics (Jain et al., 2017; Lamba and Singh, 2017), and managing customer relationships (Ro\ss mann et al.,
\par
2018; Jain et al., 2017). Thus, we argue that firms which adopt analytical tools to process big data will foresee the benefits of
\par
BDP in enhancing the value of information. Consequently, we anticipate that the more big data is subject to BDP, the greater
\par
the likelihood that use of big data in SCM decision-making will lead to value creation (Brinch, 2018). Thus, our third research
\par
question is:
\par
RQ3. To what extent is Big Data Prioritization (BDP) associated with the use of big data in SCM decision-making?
\par
Finally, we seek to extend research regarding the role of big data in SCM performance. Here, empirical studies are limited, as
\par
prior research is typically based upon anecdotal evidence, review and/or general observation (i.e., Addo-Tenkorang and Helo,
\par
2016; Arunachalam et al., 2018; Brinch, 2018; Chen et al., 2014; Lamba and Singh, 2017; Tiwari et al., 2018; Zhong et al.,
\par
2016). Empirical studies include: two Delphi studies (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018; Jain et al., 2017); a simulation study (Hofmann,
\par
2017); a single case study (Hazen et al., 2014; Jain et al., 2017); and a 2011 survey (Russom, 2011). In extending this body of
\par
research, we aim to empirically explore the association between use of big data in SCM decision-making and SCM performance.
\par
Management accounting-related improvements in SCM performance are shown to derive from informed decision-making, with
\par
the qualities of big data (accuracy, relevance, consistency, timeliness, validity, source reliability, appropriateness and complete-
\par
ness) improving the quality of this decision-making through better management of working capital and cash flow (Logistics
\par
Bureau, 2019) by: (1) reduction in inventory costs due to better forecast accuracy and reduced inventory holding costs and ob-
\par
solescence (Wang et al., 2016); (2) improvements in demand forecasting, thereby reducing uncertainty in production planning
\par
and optimizing production processes due to minimizing lead times with suppliers (Wang et al., 2016; Lamba and Singh, 2017);
\par
(3) using data from social media and web browsing histories to inform the development of products that cater to customers' fu-
\par
ture demands and needs (Zhan et al., 2018); and (4) improving delivery decisions by using real-time data to optimize delivery
\par
(Jain et al., 2017).
\par
Thus, the use of big data in SCM for`value creation' through informed decision-making enables`value capture' by the firm
\par
(Brinch, 2018). Accordingly, to investigate the relevance of big data in supporting SCM decision-making to inform these processes
\par
and improve SCM performance, our fourth research question arises, namely:
\par
RQ4. To what extent is the use of big data in SCM decision-making associated with SCM performance?8 C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
\par
In this regard, research questions 1 and 4 are designed to investigate the extent to which our study corroborates findings from
\par
prior research, which establishes a basis for evaluating the findings related to research questions 2 and 3 that have not been em-
\par
pirically tested before.
\par
3. Research approach
\par
3.1. Justification of the research approach
\par
In investigating the four research questions related to this exploratory study, akin to Brinch (2018), we theorize their relation-
\par
ship in the research model shown in Fig. 3. To statistically test the relationship between the study's key constructs, we frame our
\par
research questions as four hypotheses, namely:
\par
H1. Big Data Availability (BDAvail) is positively associated with the use of big data in SCM decision-making.
\par
H2. Big Data Availability (BDAvail) is positively associated with Big Data Prioritization (BDP).
\par
H3. Big Data Prioritization (BDP) is positively associated with the use of big data in SCM decision-making.
\par
H4. The use of big data in SCM decision-making is positively associated with SCM performance.
\par
In accord with the research aim, namely to provide a general understanding of the topic at a specific point in time, an anon-
\par
ymous online survey (see Appendix 1) was developed to test the hypotheses. Deployed using Qualtrics (Dillman, 2007), the sur-
\par
vey allowed us to ascertain common relationships across firms and generalizable statements about the topic (Gable, 1994;
\par
Neuman, 2011). With the exception of two aspects related to strategic priorities and attitudes towards performance, where a
\par
slider scale (1--100) was used, as well as questions related to participant demographics, a seven-point Likert-scale with three an-
\par
chor points was used to measure responses.
\par
To test these hypotheses, we use the partial least squares (PLS) path model, a non-parametric statistical technique (Chin and
\par
Todd, 1995; Chin, 1998). PLS, as``a component-based approach to estimation that places minimal demands on sample size and
\par
residual distributions (Chin, 1998)\dots{} permits simultaneous analysis of both the measurement model and structural model''
\par
(Lewis et al., 2003, 665). Further, it avoids inadmissible solutions and factor indeterminacy (Chin and Todd, 1995; Chin, 1998),
\par
and is valid for small sample sizes (Hair et al., 2013). Whilst it does not require distributional assumptions and places minimal
\par
demands on measurement scales (Smith and Langfield-Smith, 2004; Gong and Ferreira, 2014), PLS requires bootstrapping in
\par
order to evaluate the statistical significance of path coefficients (Chin, 1998). In this regard, we use a bootstrapping procedure
\par
with 10,000 subsamples. As depicted in Fig. 3, our PLS-SEM model is reflective-formative (Becker et al., 2012), which we test
\par
using a Repeated Indicators Approach (Van Riel et al., 2017; Hair et al., 2013; Wetzels et al., 2009; Becker et al., 2012). Herein,
\par
the manifest indicators in the first-order constructs were re-used in the second-order construct (Wold, 1982; Van Riel et al.,
\par
2017), as we regard the construct BDAvail to be a composite construct i.e., caused by the indicators (Van Riel et al., 2017). We
\par
justify this approach on the basis that``the lower-order constructs are reflectively measured constructs that do not share a com-
\par
mon cause but rather form a general concept that fully mediates the influence on subsequent endogenous variables'' (Becker
\par
et al., 2012, 364).
\par
3.2. Development of the constructs
\par
Aside from respondents' background information concerning experience and firm size, our survey constructs are framed as
\par
BDAvail, BDP, use of big data in SCM decision-making, and SCM performance. Given our theorization of BDAvail and BDP, there
\par
were no instruments in the literature by which to measure these constructs. Accordingly, by grounding our investigation in the
\par
body of knowledge concerning big data's five characteristics (Volume, Variety, Velocity, Veracity, and Value/Cost-effectiveness),
\par
we developed 18 items to measure BDAvail (see Table 1). In determining conceptual meaning, we assume that each of the five
\par
characteristics is a constitutive element of BDAvail (thus, we model BDAvail as a formative construct). Given that the five charac-
\par
teristics may be inversely correlated, based on Jarvis et al. (2003) we did not calculate internal consistency reliability for BDAvail,
\par
as it is not an appropriate standard by which to evaluate a formative construct (Becker et al., 2012).
\par
BDP is theorized as a mechanism that allows firms to prioritize the types of big data that should be collected and analyzed (see
\par
Section 2.4). Given the lack of prior research on which to base development of our items and considering prioritization as a ra-
\par
tional choice related to decision-making (Wang and Ruhe, 2007), in developing items related to BDA, we focused on categoriza-
\par
tion strategies that are likely to be adopted in SCM. Accordingly, our items relate to processes or algorithms of filtration and
\par
selection that firms are likely to apply to big data (Gandomi and Haider, 2015; Kwon and Sim, 2013). These items constitute
\par
the indicators of the construct BDP (see Table 2).
\par
We base items that measure the use of big data in SCM decision-making on the SCM processes outlined in Croxton et al.'s
\par
(2001) seminal paper and prior literature that conceptualizes the implications of big data in SCM decision-making (i.e., Jain
\par
et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2016; Ro\ss mann et al., 2018). Whilst big data has implications in all SCM processes, we focus on
\par
accounting-related decisions concerned with demand, production, customer and delivery decisions in SCM, as these are areas
\par
where prior research foreshadows big data as having significant value potential (see Table 2).C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
