3.3. Pilot testing
\par
Prior to administering the survey, a pilot study was conducted with twelve individuals who were academics and/or Honours
\par
students. Based on their feedback, a reverse-coded question was added as a mechanism to examine respondents' attentiveness.
\par
The pilot study also provided insight into the completion time, suggesting a minimum of 9 minutes was required. As this impacts
\par
the quality of responses, this timing measure is used in the main study to gauge whether participants engaged meaningfully with
\par
the survey.
\par
3.4. Data collection method
\par
Using a non-probability sampling method, the anonymous online Qualtrics survey was deployed through Amazon Mechanical
\par
Turk (MTurk). MTurk is increasingly used as a survey tool for research, with positive reviews regarding the relevance and quality
\par
of the data generated, particularly when compared to results from student surveys and professional panels (Kees et al., 2017;
\par
Buhrmester et al., 2011; Peer et al., 2014). Its advantages include that bias is avoided because there is no interaction with the re-
\par
searcher (Paolacci et al., 2010) and``results derived from convenience samples like Amazon's Mechanical Turk are similar to those
\par
obtained from national samples'' (Coppock, 2019, 613).
\par
Prior studies show two important issues in using platforms like MTurk in research, namely accessing populations of interest
\par
and data quality (Hunt and Scheetz, 2019; Kees et al., 2017; Keith et al., 2017). With respect to accessing the appropriate popu-
\par
lation, as we sought responses from individuals in the U.S. with at least one year's experience with SCM decision-making, we
\par
targeted middle-managers who had experience with collecting big data and thus were positioned to analyze data to facilitate
\par
decision-making. Using the available screening tools, we selected: (1) professions associated with managerial experience, in par-
\par
ticular entrepreneurs, managers, sales, office workers, engineer/computer science; and (2) current status as employee, business
\par
owner, unemployed, retired and other. Since the direct option of SCM was not available, under the`industry tab' we chose options
\par
including office workers, entrepreneurs, manufacturing or sales, scientist (i.e., data scientists), engineer/computer science, hi-tech
\par
(i.e., data input and processes), finance professional, business service professional, marketing/advertising and other. This tab en-
\par
abled exclusion of students on the assumption that they lacked work experience.
\par
With regards to data quality, prior research shows that it is improved through the addition of attention-check questions (Peer
\par
et al., 2014; Rouse, 2015; Hunt and Scheetz, 2019). Whilst realistic compensation rates do not affect data quality, task length hasC. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
\par
11
\par
an effect on participation rates (Buhrmester et al., 2011). With respect to attentiveness, our reverse-coded question (Q4c) resulted
\par
in the elimination of one completed survey. Further, to check attention, pilot testing showed that survey completion should take
\par
between 9 and 18 minutes. As screening showed that for 59 responses the completion time was b9 minutes, these responses
\par
were deleted. Finally, respondents were compensated at a moderate rate of \$1.75. This aligns with prior research showing that
\par
compensation for 10--15 minute tasks may be as low as \$0.05 (Kees et al., 2017), but for comparable surveys, typically ranges
\par
from \$0.75 to \$3 (i.e., Morrow et al., 2018; Chen et al., 2016; Levay et al., 2016). Further, drawing on social exchange theory,
\par
this aligns with findings that monetary compensation is not the sole consideration in survey participation (Dillman, 2007).
\par
In summary, we targeted individuals in positions unrelated to big data collection, instead focusing on users of data outputs
\par
who perform a decision-making role. Whilst our sampling frame limits generalizability of the findings, careful filtering resulted
\par
in 144 responses from individuals matching our sampling criteria.
\par
4. Findings
\par
4.1. Initial screening and respondents' demographics
\par
Of the 144 responses received, 60 were deemed to be problematic, 59 due to short completion times, and one failing the val-
\par
idation check related to the reverse-coded question.5 Thus, the final usable sample was 84 participants. Analysis of response dis-
\par
tributions for each item shows that all response categories were used for all items, indicating that the items tap into a wide range
\par
of effects (see Table 4).
\par
On average, respondents had been employed with their firm for 8.2 years, with most having been in their firm for 3--5 years
\par
(see Table 3), suggesting respondents were likely to be experienced and familiar with their firm's environment and business strat-
\par
egy. There were evident linkages to supply chain processes, as analysis of the respondents' firms show that 22.6\% were in the ser-
\par
vice industry, 21.4\% in wholesale and retail trade, and 19\% in the manufacturing industry. More specifically, 15\% of respondents
\par
had {$\geq$}10 years' experience in SCM, 15\% had 6--9 years' experience, 29\% had 3--5 years' experience, with 40\% indicating
\par
1--2 years' experience. Similar results are evident regarding experience with big data, where 12\% indicated 6--9 years' experience,
\par
36\% 3--5 years, and 43\% 1--2 years' experience. Thus, as the majority of respondents have experience with big data and SCM, there
\par
is reason to assume that they possessed knowledge relevant to the investigation.
\par
4.2. Confirmatory factor analysis
\par
Using SmartPLS, we performed Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) on our instrument to investigate construct reliability and
\par
validity. The descriptive statistics and loadings of all BDAvail items (i.e., Volume, Variety, Velocity, Veracity, Value/Cost-
\par
effectiveness), as well as the constructs BDP, use of big data in SCM decision-making, and SCM performance, are presented in
\par
Tables 4 and 5, with cross loadings reported in Appendix 2.
\par
For the purpose of CFA, based upon Hair et al. (2014), we deemed a Cronbach Alpha of 0.50 as being appropriate, whilst
\par
Fornell and Larcker (1981) suggest that in order to conclude that a construct reliably measures a latent variable, the minimum
\par
acceptable value for Composite Reliability is 0.7 and 0.5 for Average Variance Extracted (AVE). Based on this and the low loading
\par
of some items (b0.60), our results suggest removing eight items (Q1c, Q2b, Q2d, Q3a, Q5b, Q5c, Q8b and Q8d), most pertaining to
\par
BDAvail, in particular, Variety, where Cronbach Alpha was b0.5, Composite Reliability b0.5 and AVE b0.5. Despite comments in the
\par
literature that 95\% of big data is unstructured (Gandomi and Haider, 2015), our results suggest removing Q2d (see Table 4) where
\par
the words``unstructured data'' appear to inadequately convey the intent of the item. It is possible that respondents saw variety in
\par
terms of structured data, with some being transformed from unstructured data as part of the extraction process involved in
\par
transforming critical information into more comprehensible forms (Baars and Kemper, 2008). Velocity produced similar, but
\par
less significant issues. Removal of items Q5b and Q5c, related to Value/Cost-effectiveness, may reflect that improved technology
\par
for BDAvail makes collection and processing of data less of an issue (Kambatla et al., 2014; McAbee et al., 2017). Alternatively,
\par
the poor loadings for items Q8b (i.e., big data enabling reduced costs) and Q8d (i.e., use of big data contributing to fewer break-
\par
downs in the supply chain) may imply that respondents are more focused on Value than Cost.
\par
As Volume, Variety, Velocity, Veracity and Value/Cost-effectiveness are constitutive elements of BDAvail, it is not appropriate to
\par
use an internal consistency reliability statistic (Jarvis et al., 2003), as this assumes that measures are substitutes for each other,
\par
rather than constituents. Equally, our analysis shows Velocity, Volume, Value/Cost-Effectiveness, and Veracity are the strongest
\par
contributors towards BDAvail.
\par
With respect to the other key constructs-- BDP, use of big data in SCM decision-making, and SCM performance-- loadings of
\par
the retained items exceed the 0.6 threshold (see Table 5), whilst Cronbach's alpha and Composite Reliability were N0.7 (see
\par
Table 6). Hence, although the AVE value concerning the use of big data in SCM decision-making was b0.5, these constructs
\par
were accepted as measures of a respondent's use of big data in SCM decision-making (Duarte and Raposo, 2010). Regarding
\par
use of big data in SCM decision-making, strongest support relates to managing customer relationships (mean 5.66, SD 1.29)
\par
and delivery decisions (mean 5.51, SD 1.47). However, there is support for big data contributing to increased profit (mean
\par
5.67, SD 1.10) and customer satisfaction (mean 5.76, SD 1.23).
