4.3. Results from hypothesis testing
\par
Following testing and validation of the inner model, we report testing of our hypotheses (see Fig. 4). The path coefficients and
\par
p-values from this analysis are reported in Table 8, with the full structural model shown in Appendix 3.
\par
As depicted in Fig. 4, and outlined in Table 8, results indicate that all hypotheses are supported at the 5\% level of significance.
\par
Thus, we find that BDAvail has a positive, significant association with the use of big data in SCM decision-making (H1), suggesting
\par
that the more big data is available to firms, the more it is used in SCM decision-making. H2 is also supported, as BDAvail is shown
\par
to have a strong positive, significant association with BDP, indicating that the more big data is available to firms, the more the
\par
firm undertakes processes to prioritize big data. Analysis also shows support for the prediction of a positive, significant association
\par
of BDP and use of big data in SCM decision-making (H3), giving credence to the argument that the more firms undertake pro-
\par
cesses to prioritize big data, the more firms use big data in SCM decision-making. Finally, analysis shows a positive, significant
\par
association between use of big data in SCM decision-making and SCM performance, providing support for H4. Thus, results sug-
\par
gest that greater use of big data in SCM decision-making has a beneficial effect on SCM performance.6
