5. Discussion
\par
5.1. Findings regarding the survey instrument
\par
Our results show the potential of our survey instrument in appraising the value of big data, and specifically the BDP mecha-
\par
nism. Whilst refinement is required, our conceptualization of BDAvail in terms of its constitutive elements (i.e., Volume, Velocity,
\par
Variety, Veracity and Value/Cost-effectiveness) is a new empirical approach. In general, with respect to BDAvail, findings demon-
\par
strate strong support for: Veracity (regarding big data being precise, reliable and accurate); Volume (in terms of the quantum of
\par
big data available); Value/Cost-effectiveness (in terms of big data representing good value); Velocity (related to big data being
\par
frequently updated and in real-time); and Variety (in terms of richness of big data). Further, results regarding Value/Cost-
\par
effectiveness suggest that improved technology for BDAvail makes collecting and processing data less of an issue (Kambatla
\par
et al., 2014; McAbee et al., 2017) and that respondents are more focused on value than cost. Analysis of the confidence intervals
\par
for the individual contribution of each`V' to BDAvail (see Appendix 5) suggests a balanced weight with respect to the importance
\par
of Variety, Velocity, Volume, and Value/Cost-effectiveness in our sample, with the weight of Veracity being higher. The relatively
\par
low weight of Value/Cost-effectiveness (see Appendix 5) aligns with two strands of research, namely: (1) emerging literature
\par
concerning the complexity of conducting cost-benefit analysis of big data (Skourletopoulos et al., 2017); and (2) market pressure
\par
to use big data as a source of competitive advantage (Kubina et al., 2015). Conversely, exposure to multiple risks associated with
\par
analyzing irrelevant or inaccurate big data (Brown-Liburd et al., 2015; Clarke, 2016) may play a role in the reported findings re-
\par
garding Veracity (path weight of 0.418 versus an average of 0.228).
\par
5.2. Findings regarding BDAvail, BDP and use of big data in SCM decision-making
\par
With respect to our theoretical predictions concerning the role of BDP in firms' use of big data in SCM, confirmatory results
\par
concerning H1 and H4 (i.e., ascertaining the extent to which our study corroborates findings from prior research) establish our
\par
base for findings related to H2 and H3 that have not been empirically tested before. This enables both theoretical and empirical
\par
contributions to knowledge.
\par
Regarding the role of data-driven decisions in SCM, there is``a long history of using data and information to drive decisions
\par
and the focus on data and information flows are therefore, not new to business processes'' (Brinch, 2018, 1602). For example,
\par
whilst not specifying big data, Croxton et al. (2001) show the significant role of data management in decisions related to eight
\par
supply chain processes. Thus, unsurprisingly our findings confirm that more big data being available to firms does mean more
\par
use in SCM decision-making (H1). In demonstrating this significant, positive relationship, we confirm prior research
\par
(i.e., Hofmann, 2017), which similarly acknowledges that firms increasingly value data and BDA for effective SCM
\par
(Arunachalam et al., 2018; Hazen et al., 2014). This is supported by McAfee and Brynjolfsson (2012, 64) who state``[d]ata-driven
\par
decisions are better decisions'' as big data improves the accuracy of forecasting and reduces uncertainty (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018;
\par
Arunachalam et al., 2018).
\par
Similarly, our findings of a positive association between use of big data in SCM decision-making and SCM performance (H4)
\par
confirm prior research showing that the use of big data in SCM decision-making enhances SCM performance through the value
\par
generated by: improvements in business processes and more effective decision-making (Brinch, 2018; Barbosa et al., 2018); stra-
\par
tegic sourcing (Wang et al., 2016); and decreasing delivery costs and breakdowns (Jain et al., 2017).
\par
A key aim of our study concerns investigation of the role and performance implications of BDP, a concept theorized to screen
\par
available big data according to a set of requirements set by the firm regarding what data inputs should be processed. The ratio-
\par
nale for BDP is as a method to minimize costs (or exchange-value) in collecting and processing big data and maximize use/value
\par
through the production of quality, insightful data that informs reports for decision-making (Bowman and Ambrosini, 2010;
\par
Brinch, 2018). Our findings demonstrate the importance of BDP, demonstrating that BDAvail is positively and significantly as-
\par
sociated with BDP (H2), and that BDP is positively associated with use of big data in SCM decision-making (H3). Thus, findings
\par
suggest that there is benefit from the role that BDP performs, which may contribute to effectiveness in use of big data, and in-
\par
directly SCM performance. Whilst it can be argued that, by not considering all available factors, BDP limits potential insights
\par
and information acquired for decision-making, prudent use of BDP involves consideration of trade-offs between the benefits
\par
gained from using prioritized big data and the rising costs associated with collecting, processing, and analyzing it. The practical
\par
significance of such cost/benefit evaluations is evident from figures such as those related to the Petabyte Hadoop cluster, which
\par
costs approximately \$1 million to obtain and \$0.5 million annually to run, store and analyze large amounts of big data
\par
(Mukherjee and Shaw, 2016).
\par
5.3. Supplementary testing
\par
Additional testing of the data provides further support for our findings. Firstly, results from the robustness tests suggest that in
\par
the presence of control variables, such as firm size, differentiation strategy, calculative culture, and environmental dynamism, the
\par
relationship of the latent variables retain their significance and predicted direction i.e., findings are not sensitive to the influence
\par
of these control variables.
\par
Secondly, to ascertain whether use of big data in SCM decision-making results in non-financial benefits (e.g., better quality or
\par
productivity), we investigated whether results from statistical tests hold under an alternative outcome variable, namely firm18 C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
\par
performance, as measured by items derived from Ittner and Larcker (2001). Results indicate that increased use of big data in SCM
\par
decision-making has a beneficial effect, suggesting that use of big data in SCM decision-making benefits both SCM performance
\par
and firm performance. This finding that use of big data has implications on non-financial performance, supports the link between
\par
SCM performance and firm performance.
\par
5.4. Implications of the indirect effects
\par
Based on our findings that show the path coefficients of BDAvail, BDP and use of big data in SCM decision-making are sig-
\par
nificant and positive, we observe a complementary partial mediation (Nitzl et al., 2016), suggesting that the intermediate var-
\par
iable BDP explains, in part, the relationship between BDAvail and use of big data in SCM decision-making. In other words, BDP
\par
becomes a driver concerning use of big data in SCM decision-making. We also note that inclusion of BDP in the model signif-
\par
icantly increases the variance explained (i.e., R2) in the use of big data in SCM from 0.332 to 0.429. Accordingly, our findings
\par
suggest that BDP may be regarded as a method to assist accountants and other decision-makers in addressing issues of data
\par
deluge and data quality by prioritizing what big data should be processed and analyzed for use in SCM decision-making. As
\par
shown by our analysis of indirect effects, BDP captures approximately half of the magnitude concerning the relationship be-
\par
tween BDAvail and use of big data in SCM decision-making. Hence, our findings suggest that by prioritizing big data available
\par
for BDA, thereby maximizing the value-cost ratio, more informed SCM decisions are likely to be made, leading to better SCM
\par
firm performance.
\par
Further, results suggest that BDP has a significant positive indirect effect on SCM performance. This positive association sug-
\par
gests that there is a mediated effect in which BDP exerts an indirect effect on performance through use of big data in SCM
\par
decision-making (Nitzl et al., 2016). Whilst this does not substantiate the role of BDP, it suggests merit in future research to in-
\par
vestigate this relationship and the significance of BDP in indirectly adding value to firms. One avenue for future research regards
\par
investigating whether use of BDP enables managers and accountants to make more timely and accurate SCM decisions
\par
(i.e., Brinch, 2018).
\par
6. Conclusion
\par
Prior research has typically reviewed the fundamentals of how big data is used in SCM (Addo-Tenkorang and Helo, 2016;
\par
Arunachalam et al., 2018; Brinch, 2018; Chen et al., 2014; Lamba and Singh, 2017; Tiwari et al., 2018; Waller and Fawcett,
\par
2013; Zhong et al., 2016). Moreover, empirical studies are limited to: Delphi studies (Ro\ss mann et al., 2018; Jain et al., 2017); a
\par
simulation study (Hofmann, 2017); a single case study (Hazen et al., 2014; Jain et al., 2017); and a survey used to explore
\par
how industry views BDAvail (Russom, 2011). More recently, investigations have reported results from a single survey within
\par
one company (Addo-Tenkorang and Helo, 2017) and factors that affect assimilation of big data predictive analytics in supply
\par
chain performance (Gunasekaran et al., 2017). In contrast, we have piloted and tested an instrument comprising a formative mea-
\par
sure of BDAvail. In doing so we address calls for specific research that``explicitly deploys the 3Vs methodology of big data in any
\par
area of O\&SCM [Operations and SCM]'' (Lamba and Singh, 2017, 888), which may lead to governance mechanisms that ensure big
\par
data quality (Janssen et al., 2017).
\par
Whilst we acknowledge that limitations arise from initial application of our survey instrument, our study offers a new
\par
pathway for future research related to targeted use of big data to provide real-time information that complements and ex-
\par
tends current managerial accounting, financial accounting, and financial reporting practices. Whilst we have conceptualized
\par
BDP as occurring after data acquisition, and thus as an input to BDA, future research may also wish to consider an alternative
\par
by positioning it in the Big Data Value Chain (Curry et al., 2014) as a step after data storage, before data usage. We further
\par
acknowledge several limitations including that only 58\% of respondents had N3 years' experience with big data and only
\par
58\% had N6 years' experience with computer systems. We also acknowledge that disqualification of a significant portion of
\par
participants limits reliability of the findings. It is a challenge to develop a new scale and use it and its data to test theoretical
\par
predictions in the same study, but without some testing, practical relevance is hard to establish. Hence H1 and H4 were in-
\par
cluded as corroborative measures to establish our base for findings related to H2 and H3 that have not been empirically tested
\par
before. Regarding application of our survey instrument, whilst we endeavored to address issues of attentiveness and data
\par
quality related to use of crowdsourcing platforms such as MTurk, further refinement of the scale should consider a range
\par
of issues including development of some new items and verifying participants' knowledge of and experience with big data.
\par
In particular, two of the constitutive elements (Variety and Value/Cost-effectiveness) used to form the formative construct
\par
BDAvail need further development. Thus, whilst still requiring further refinement, this study makes a first and necessary
\par
step to develop a scale that measures the value proposition concerning the use of big data for improved decision-making
\par
and performance.
\par
In conclusion, findings of a positive association between the two variables BDP and SCM performance suggest that there is a
\par
mediated effect in which BDP exerts an indirect effect on SCM performance through use of big data in SCM decision-making. Thus,
\par
our instrument contributes a new approach by which to investigate the value proposition concerning big data in SCM perfor-
\par
mance (M\"uller et al., 2018). Whilst not substantiating the role of BDP in causing better SCM performance, our exploratory
\par
study suggests merit in future research that investigates this relationship and the significance of BDP in indirectly adding value
\par
to firms though enabling and adding value from big data as a new source of information that complements and extends tradi-
\par
tional sources of accounting information.C. Wilkin et al. / International Journal of Accounting Information Systems 38 (2020) 100470
