Abstract
\par
The ``value of information'' (VOI) is a generic term for the increase in value
\par
resulting from better information to guide management, or alternatively, the
\par
value foregone under uncertainty about the impacts of management (Yokota
\par
and Thompson, Medical Decision Making 2004; 24: 287). The value of informa-
\par
tion can be characterized in terms of several metrics, including the expected
\par
value of perfect information and the expected value of partial information. We
\par
extend the technical framework for the value of information by further develop-
\par
ing the relationship between value metrics for partial and perfect information
\par
and describing patterns of their performance. We use two different expressions
\par
for the expected value of partial information to highlight its relationship to the
\par
expected value of perfect information. We also develop the expected value of
\par
partial information for hierarchical uncertainties. We highlight patterns in the
\par
value of information for the Svalbard population of the pink-footed goose
\par
(Anser brachyrhynchus), a population that is subject to uncertainty in both
\par
reproduction and survival functions. The framework for valuing information is
\par
seen as having widespread potential in resource decision making, and serves as
\par
a motivation for resource monitoring, assessment, and collaboration.
\par
Introduction
\par
The ``value of information'' (VOI) is a generic term for
\par
the increase in value resulting from better information to
\par
guide management. Alternatively, it can be viewed as
\par
value foregone under uncertainty about the impacts of
\par
management (Yokota and Thompson 2004). One of many
\par
applications might use the value of information to inform
\par
the assessment of monitoring effectiveness. For example,
\par
an increase in the value expected from reducing uncer-
\par
tainty can be compared against opportunity and other
\par
costs associated with collecting and analyzing informa-
\par
tion, to determine whether and how monitoring should
\par
be undertaken (Hauser et al. 2006; McDonald-Madden
\par
et al. 2010).
\par
The concept of a value of information has been
\par
recognized for several decades and is now well developed.
