Application to pink-footed geese
\par
The need for informed management of European goose
\par
populations is increasingly urgent because many popula-
\par
tions have grown dramatically in recent decades (Mad-
\par
sen et al. 1999; Fox et al. 2010). Although geese are
\par
regarded as a valuable resource, the growth in numbers
\par
and their tendency to concentrate on farmlands have
\par
growth agricultural conflicts in wintering and staging
\par
areas. In some Arctic regions, increasing goose abun-
\par
dance has resulted in overexploitation of vegetation,
\par
causing long-term degradation of tundra habitats.
\par
The Svalbard population of the pink-footed goose
\par
(Anser brachyrhynchus) was recently selected as the first
\par
test case in Europe for development of an international
\par
harvest management plan to help address these con-
\par
cerns. The Svalbard population breeds primarily in
\par
Spitsbergen, migrates through Norway, and winters pri-
\par
marily in Denmark, the Netherlands, and Belgium.
\par
Although these geese have been well studied, much
\par
uncertainty remains about population dynamics and the
\par
effects of harvest. Managers are currently considering
\par
nine alternative population models that are based on
\par
different combinations of three survival and three repro-
\par
ductive models (Johnson et al. 2014b). These models
\par
represent a wide range of possibilities concerning
\par
whether demographic rates are density dependent or
\par
independent, and whether spring temperatures on the
\par
breeding grounds affect vital rates.
