Hierarchical Uncertainty Factors
\par
In the above development, the models incorporate two
\par
forms of uncertainty, with different combinations of indi-
\par
ces k0 and k00 denoting the models under consideration.
\par
Here we consider a somewhat different uncertainty situa-
\par
tion, in which the models are separated into two distinct
\par
groups according to some distinguishing feature. There is
\par
uncertainty about which group more effectively represents
\par
system dynamics, and the challenge is to select the appro-
\par
priate group.
\par
In this situation, the probability structure for model
\par
uncertainty is hierarchical rather than combinatorial, with
\par
k0 now denoting group identity and k00 specifying a par-
\par
ticular model within a group. As above, both indices are
\par
needed to identify an individual model in the complete
\par
set of candidate models. The hierarchical structure of
\par
uncertainty is captured in the conditional nature of the
\par
joint probability of these indices: q(k0
\par
, k00)= q(k0)q(k00\textbar k0).
\par
The issue here is to assess the value of information in the
\par
grouping.
\par
The expected value of partial information provides a
\par
natural approach for this situation. Thus, the residual
\par
value of information, obtained as in Eq. (8) by subtract-
\par
ing the average ``within-group'' value of information from
\par
the overall expected value of perfect information, pro-
\par
duces a metric to help focus on the most appropriate
\par
group of models in reducing the loss of information from
\par
uncertainty. Thus, similar values for group-specific EVPIs
\par
can produce a small residual value in Eq. (8). In conse-
\par
quence, a low value for EXPXI suggests that all groups
\par
contribute to the loss of value from uncertainty, so one
\par
cannot safely focus on a specific group to reduce that
\par
loss. On the other hand, a large value for EVPXI can
\par
result from substantial differences among group-specific
\par
EVPIs, suggesting that the loss from uncertainty can be
\par
addressed by focusing on select groups with higher
\par
within-group EVPIs.
\par
This framework allows one to consider the value of
\par
information for an individual model. To see how, let the
\par
models be divided into two groups, with one group con-
\par
sisting of a single model and the other consisting of the
\par
remaining models. Letting k= 2,. . ., K for the models in
\par
the larger group and k= 1 for the model in the singleton
