\par
The general patterns for EVPI carry over for EVPXI. Just
\par
as similarities among model value functions tend to reduce
\par
the magnitude of EVPI[x, q], so too do similarities in the
\par
value functions for models with index k0 tend to reduce the
\par
magnitude of EVPI(x, qk0 0\textbar k0). If these similarities hold
\par
across all values of k0, then Pk0 q\dh k0\TH EVPI\dh x; qk00jk0 \TH{} will be
\par
small and in consequence EVPXIK0 (x, q) will be large.
\par
Essentially, small ``within-group variation'' in model values
\par
results in a large residual value of information that is
\par
expressed in EVPXIK0 (x, q). Conversely, relatively large dif-
\par
ferences in the value functions for models with a common
\par
index k0 means that EVPI(x, qk00 \textbar k0 ) will be large, so that
\par
EVPXIK0 (x, q) will be small.
\par
We noted above that change in value represented by
\par
EVPI is relatively small for the pink-footed goose, a result
\par
that is consistent with other wildlife applications. There
\par
may be a tendency to over-interpret this pattern as sug-
\par
gesting that uncertainty is not an important factor in
\par
resource management. However, we take issue with such
\par
an interpretation. In fact, EVPI indicates how much more
\par
value could be gained with decision making if the
\par
remainder of the uncertainty facing decision makers could
\par
be eliminated. But that in no way devalues the use of
\par
information about resource status and processes in deci-
\par
sion making. From the above, EVPI consists of a compar-
\par
ison of the average of optimal values assuming full
\par
process understanding versus the optimal value that is
\par
attainable in the face of limited understanding. As such it
\par
is effectively a marginal analysis, addressing the marginal
\par
value of additional information gathering and assuming
\par
an ongoing if imperfect effort to inform decision making.
\par
Monitoring is required for the state- and process-based
\par
information on which the optimal resource decision mak-
\par
ing depends, and the question here is whether additional
\par
monitoring is justified by the potential increase in value
\par
that would be produced. Whether to increase the effort
\par
depends on the answer to this question; whether to termi-
\par
nate monitoring altogether does not. It would simply be
\par
incorrect to conclude that small EVPI means uncertainty
\par
is ``not a big deal,'' or that monitoring information is
\par
unneeded.
\par
Finally, it should be emphasized that the value of infor-
\par
mation, although useful as a measure of the potential
\par
change in management performance, does not fully cap-
\par
ture the overall value of smart decision making in the face
\par
of uncertainty. The framework articulated above for assess-
\par
ing the value of information, involving specified manage-
\par
ment objectives, a range of potential actions, sources of
\par
ª 2015 The Authors. Ecology and Evolution published by John Wiley \& Sons Ltd. Value of Information in Natural Resource Management
\par
uncertainty, models that forecast resource changes, and
\par
measures of model confidence, provides a platform for
\par
comparative assessment among alternative decisions,
\par
whether or not decision making is optimal and whether or
\par
not the value of information is assessed. The framework
\par
helps to motivate and justify postdecision monitoring and
\par
assessment as a way to track resource responses and evalu-
\par
ate progress toward objectives. Importantly, it can serve as
\par
a mechanism for collaboration and shared decision mak-
\par
ing, and thereby lower potential contentiousness and con-
\par
flict among stakeholders. These and other benefits accrue
\par
to a structured process for decision assessment and identi-
\par
fication. The value of information can certainly contribute
\par
to management, but it should be recognized that it is not
\par
the only measure of management effectiveness.
