\par
The level of firms' information security investment has recently become a critical issue in the manage-
\par
ment of IT infrastructure. Prior studies have not considered attack types and firms interconnection
\par
simultaneously when investigating the optimisation of such investment. Using game theory, we demon-
\par
strate that the optimal security investment level of an interconnected firm against targeted attacks is dif-
\par
ferent from that against opportunistic attacks. Our model shows that not all information security risks are
\par
worth fighting against. As the potential loss increases, it is unadvisable to increase the security invest-
\par
ment proportionately. Firms should increase investments with intrinsic vulnerability when facing target
\par
attacks, but focus on those systems that fall into the midrange of intrinsic vulnerability when facing
\par
opportunistic attacks. Firms are unwilling to invest in security and often offload reliability problems onto
\par
others when the trusted interdependence relationship becomes tighter in the absence of economic incen-
\par
tives. Thus we also discuss two economic incentives to motivate firms: liability and security information
\par
sharing. We find that if the rules are set properly, both economic incentives are effective to not only inter-
\par
nalise the negative externality and improve a firm's security level, but also reduce the total expected cost.
\par
We show that firms' optimal investments of liability always increase with the increasing number of firms,
\par
but the optimal investments on security information sharing increase only when the number of firms is
\par
large enough. These insights draw attention to many trade-offs firms often face and the importance of
\par
accurate assessment of firms' security environment. Future research directions are discussed based on
\par
the limitations and possible extensions of this study.
\par
