\par
1. Introduction
\par
Information security investments are usually decided based on
\par
various economic models which assume that there is no difference
\par
in attack types and firms' information systems are independent of
\par
each other. However, these two assumptions deviate from the rea-
\par
lity that firms usually face different types of attacks and their sys-
\par
tems are interconnected with one another. The security of
\par
information systems can be seriously affected by attack types
\par
and firms interconnection. For example, according to the CSI
\par
(Computer Security Institute) survey (Richardson, 2011), the
\par
respondents who suffer from malware infections is four times as
\par
