\par
many as those who suffer from denial of service, and the loss from
\par
theft of information is three times as much as that from virus.
\par
Another survey by PWC shows that 22\% of the respondents have
\par
begun to conduct incident response planning with their partners,
\par
in which they agree to share information or allow network access
\par
with each other (PWC, 2013). In December 2013, the security
\par
breach of the giant US retailer, Target Corporation, exposed credit
\par
card and personal data of more than 110 million consumers. It
\par
started with a malware-laced phishing email sent to employees
\par
at an HVAC firm that is a vendor of Target (Krebs, 2014). Because
\par
Target's information systems are difficult to breach, the hackers
\par
chose to attack the HVAC firm's information systems that are con-
\par
nected with the Target's but easier to breach. Thus, the effect of
\par
attack types and system interconnection is not limited to a single
\par
firm's security but also the security of its partners.
\par
Firms continuously face many different types of attacks. CSI
\par
classifies attacks into three categories: basic attacks, malware
\par
attacks and attack 2.0 (Attack 2.0 refers to the advanced persistentY. Wu et al. / Expert Systems with Applications 42 (2015) 6132--6146 6133
\par
threats) (Richardson, 2011). Another research segregates attacks
\par
into two types: ``High-Frequency-Low-Impact'' and ``Low-
\par
Frequency-High-Impact'' attacks (Wang, Chaudhury, \& Rao, 2008).
\par
This classification is similar to many other researchers': they segre-
\par
gate attacks into two categories, targeted attack and opportunistic
\par
attack, based on whether the attacks have a specific target (targeted
\par
attack) or a number of intermediate targets to fulfil the hacker's end
\par
goal (opportunistic attack) (Casey, 2003; Collins, Gates, \& Kataria,
\par
2006; Huang \& Behara, 2013; Huang, Hu, \& Behara, 2008). For
\par
instance, denial of service, website defacement and a purposeful
\par
penetration into a bank's system to steal money are typical targeted
\par
attacks, while a virus, worm, malware infection and spam e-mail are
\par
typical opportunistic attacks (Huang \& Behara, 2013).
\par
The trusted interdependence relationship between firms is re-
\par
flected in two ways based on the PWC survey (PWC, 2013): net-
\par
work connection and information sharing. First, firms'
\par
information systems are physically interconnected via a trusted
\par
network such as a joint design network. Because the configuration
\par
of a network is composed of various interconnected systems, the
\par
network becomes vulnerable if any one of the systems is insecure.
\par
An organisation's system is at risk if a hacker gains access to its
\par
partner's system (Zhao, Xue, \& Whinston, 2013). For instance,
\par
Walmart allows Proctor \& Gamble (P\&G) to access information in
\par
Walmart's information system via a trusted point-to-point
\par
Electronic Data Interchange (EDI), and vice versa. Their ongoing
\par
communication and collaboration are conducted through the EDI.
\par
This makes it possible that a virus or a hacker breaches the infor-
\par
mation systems of P\&G through the Internet firstly, and then
\par
probabilistically break into Walmart's systems via the EDI link.
\par
This is possible because Walmart trusts the EDI connection with
\par
P\&G and therefore will not reject the access request. Second, many
\par
firms achieve product innovation or value creation via network
\par
economy. As a result, many firms' information is shared with their
\par
partners. These firms could suffer information loss together
\par
because of information sharing. For example, Walmart and P\&G
\par
share retail sales information on P\&G products at Walmart stores.
\par
The retail sales information is stored on servers of both firms. If
\par
hackers breach Walmart's server, they can obtain Walmart's pri-
\par
vate information, which causes losses to Walmart directly, as well
\par
as the retail sales information of P\&G, which imposes losses on
\par
P\&G indirectly. Thus sharing valued information is also a form of
\par
the trusted interdependence relationship between firms.
\par
Given that the consequences of security breaches are influenced
\par
by attack types (Ponemon, 2013) and the interconnectivity of infor-
\par
mation systems has increased their insecurity (Gordon, Loeb, \&
\par
Lucyshyn, 2003), this research investigates the impacts of attack
\par
types and firms interconnection on the information security invest-
\par
ments. In this study we use game theory to model the information
\par
security investment problem for two firms that attempt to min-
\par
imise their total expected losses from security breaches. Because
\par
of the prisoner's dilemma1 in the information security investment
\par
game, firms are not always willing to invest in security and often off-
\par
load reliability problems onto others. The only way to encourage
\par
firms to invest in security when they face the possibility of contam-
\par
ination from others is to develop a set of economic incentives (either
\par
positive or negative) that make it more attractive for firms to make
\par
more investments (Kunreuther \& Heal, 2003). Therefore, after
\par
investigating the features of optimal information security invest-
\par
ment, we also discuss two effective economic incentives: liability
\par
and security information sharing to solve the prisoner's dilemma.
\par
Our findings shed light on firms' information security invest-
\par
ment behaviours. First, we demonstrate that the optimal security
\par
investment level of an interconnected firm against targeted attacks
\par
is different from that against opportunistic attacks. Second, in the
\par
absence of economic incentives, an interconnected firm is unwilling
\par
to increase its security investment when its trusted interdepen-
\par
dence relationship with partners becomes tighter. In addition, if
\par
the rules of economic incentives are set properly, both liability
\par
and security information sharing are effective to not only inter-
\par
nalise the negative externality and improve a firm's security level,
\par
but also reduce the total expected cost. We find that the firm's opti-
\par
mal investment of liability always increase as the number of firms
\par
increases, but the optimal investment of security information shar-
\par
ing increases only when the number of firms is large enough.
\par
The rest of the paper is organised as follows. In Section 2, we
\par
review the literature on the economics of information security. In
\par
Section 3, we introduce the features of information systems, attack
\par
types and network vulnerability. In Section 4, we investigate the
\par
features of an interconnected firm's optimal information security
\par
investment for both attack types. In Section 5, we discuss two eco-
\par
nomic incentives for information security investments. We extend
\par
our model to the case of three or more firms in Section 6. We pre-
\par
sent the study's conclusions in Section 7.
\par
2. Literature review
\par
Information security has been a focus of the information sys-
\par
tems discipline since the 1990s and become a main stream topic
\par
recently (e.g. Parker, 1997; Straub, 1990; Straub, Goodman, \&
\par
Baskerville, 2008). Although research into the information security
\par
has received some attention, economics considerations related to
\par
information security investments are rare. As an important deci-
\par
sion of information security, information security investments face
\par
many uncertainties and should be taken seriously. Since attack
\par
types play an important role in information security investment
\par
decision, many researchers have studied this issue. Gordon and
\par
Loeb (2002) use an economic benefit maximisation method to
\par
analyse a firm that faces two different breach probability functions.
\par
They show that a firm's optimal information security investment
\par
would not exceed 36.8\% of the potential loss. Extending the
\par
Gordon and Loeb model, Huang et al. (2008) use expected utility
\par
theory to analyse a firm facing two attack types: targeted attack
\par
and distributed attack. They identify a minimum potential loss,
\par
below which a firm does not necessarily invest in information
\par
security, and indicate that the information security investment
\par
does not necessarily increase with a higher level of risk aversion.
\par
Huang and Behara (2013) study the allocation mechanism of a
\par
firm's limited information security budget to concurrently defend
\par
against two attack types (targeted and opportunistic attack).
\par
They find that a firm with a limited security budget should allocate
\par
most or all of the investment to prevent one type of attack, even
\par
when they simultaneously face different attack types. Cezar,
\par
Cavusoglu, and Raghunathan (2014) group the nature of security
\par
function into two categories (prevention and detection) and pro-
\par
pose a complementarity mechanism to enhance the advantages
\par
offered by both functions. Huang, Behara, and Goo (2014) examine
\par
the investment made by an organisation in a Healthcare
\par
Information Exchange to prevent opportunistic attacks. Besides
\par
the economics of information security investments, attack types
\par
have also been examined from other information security perspec-
\par
tives. For example, He, Chen, Su, and Sun (2014) propose a scheme
\par
to protect users from identity theft attacks in online social net-
\par
working sites. A commonality of these previous studies is that they
\par
focus on a single firm who faces different attack types. However,
\par
the security investment decisions may be very different when mul-
\par
tiple firms are involved, because firms' interconnection through
\par
trusted networks and information sharing make it possible for a
\par
firm to suffer from indirect attacks due to other firms'
\par
1 The prisoner's dilemma is a classical phenomenon in economics games. It
\par
suggests that two purely rational individuals will not always cooperate, even if it
\par
appears that cooperation is in their best interests.6134 Y. Wu et al. / Expert Systems with Applications 42 (2015) 6132--6146
\par
vulnerability. Thus, this paper complements the IT security litera-
\par
ture by considering interconnectivity between firms under differ-
\par
ent types of attacks.
\par
Protecting interconnected information systems from viruses or
\par
hackers can be considered as an interdependent security (IDS)
\par
problem. Many problems, such as fire protection, theft protection,
\par
vaccinations and airline security, are typical IDS problems. All IDS
\par
problems share a common characteristic: the network consisting
\par
of the interconnected agents has negative externality, i.e. the
\par
agents in the network will increasingly attempt to offload reliabil-
\par
ity duties onto other agents as the degree of interconnectivity
\par
increases. Kunreuther and Heal (2003) study the airline security
\par
interdependence problem and find that an airline has much fewer
\par
economic incentives to invest in a security system if it believes that
\par
other airlines will not make similar investments. Varian (2004)
\par
provides a simple model to explain the free rider problem with
\par
three prototypical interdependence cases.
\par
In reality, network insecurity is somewhat like air pollution or
\par
traffic congestion, in which a firm that connects insecure machines
\par
to the Internet does not bear the full consequences of its actions
\par
(Anderson \& Moore, 2006). In addition, each firm within a network
\par
can make its own decision on security investment, but a firm's secur-
\par
ity risks depend not only on its own security practices but also on the
\par
security practices of other firms (Zhao et al., 2013). Traditional eco-
\par
nomic models such as economic benefit maximisation and expected
\par
utility theory do not allow a firm's information security investment
\par
behaviour to influence another's. Yet, a model for analysing informa-
\par
tion security investments should capture the strategic interaction
\par
between interconnected firms (Cavusoglu, Raghunathan, \& Yue,
\par
2008). Game theory is appropriate to model such strategic interac-
\par
tions. The game players could be firms trying to protect their infor-
\par
mation systems and hackers trying to attack the information
\par
systems. Alternatively, players could be interconnected firms that
\par
try to individually or jointly fend off attacks. Cavusoglu, Mishra,
\par
and Raghunathan (2005) apply game theory to explain that a firm
\par
can obtain a positive value from an intrusion detection system if
\par
the detection rate is greater than a threshold and will obtain a non-
\par
negative value from an optimally configured intrusion detection
\par
system. Hui, Hui, and Yue (2012) use game theory to analyse how
\par
the system interdependency risks interact with a mandatory secur-
\par
ity requirement to affect the equilibrium behaviours of a managed
\par
security service provider and its clients.
\par
Because of the negative externality of interdependent security,
\par
many studies apply economic incentives to solve the IDS problem.
\par
Gordon et al. (2003) use game theory to show that information shar-
\par
ing can increase the level of information security and propose some
\par
incentive mechanisms for sharing information. Zhao et al. (2013)
\par
examine two alternative risk management approaches (risk pooling
\par
arrangements and managed security services) to solve the
\par
interdependency risks. Fang, Parameswaran, Zhao, and Whinston
\par
(2014) use game theory to model the interdependent security risk
\par
of inter-organisational information systems and propose an incen-
\par
tive mechanism to solve this problem. Only a few studies investigate
\par
the interdependent security risk from economic consideration of
\par
information security investments. For example, Ogut, Menon, and
\par
Raghunathan (2005) use game theory to analyse the impact of
\par
interdependent risks in cyber insurance and IT security investment
\par
and find that the interdependence of cyber-risk reduces a firm's
\par
investment in security technologies and cyber insurance. Kolfal,
\par
Patterson, and Yeo (2013) analyse optimal security investment deci-
\par
sions based on customer response to adverse IT security events.
\par
Our literature review shows that prior studies have focused on
\par
many aspects of information security with interdependent risks.
\par
However, little research reveals how interdependent risks affect
\par
firms' information security investments when facing different
\par
attack types, and how economic incentives should be actualised
\par
Fig. 1. The conceptual description of the model.
\par
to solve the negative externality of information security invest-
\par
ments. We intend to fill these research gaps by developing a
\par
game-theoretical model to consider the optimal information secur-
\par
ity investments and the optimal economic incentives when inter-
\par
connected firms face different attack types.
\par
3. Model preliminaries
\par
To model information security investments, we consider a sin-
\par
gle-event, single-period security breach,2 with a probability p, of
\par
two risk-neutral3 firms who face two attack types, targeted attack
\par
and opportunistic attack. The two firms' information systems are
\par
interconnected through trusted network or storing mutual informa-
\par
tion, as shown by Fig. 1. The case of three or more firms is similar
\par
and will be discussed in Section 6.
\par
3.1. Information system features
\par
When attackers successfully breach a firm's information system
\par
that stores confidential information, the firm may suffer a loss. We
\par
use L to denote the totality of loss this firm suffers. L includes not
\par
only direct losses such as those resulting from bank accounts sto-
\par
len but also indirect losses such as the damage to a firm's rep-
\par
utation due to the security breach.
\par
Because of the limitation of security technology and the com-
\par
plexity of security issues, perfect security is impossible for an
\par
information system (PWC, 2013; Zhang, Deng, Wei, \& Deng,
\par
2012). A firm could spend a certain amount of money to decrease
\par
the risk by reducing the breach probability. Many previous articles
\par
(e.g., Gordon \& Loeb, 2002; Huang et al., 2008) show that the
\par
breach probability of a given information system can be charac-
\par
terised by three parameters: v, t and S. Let p be the breach proba-
\par
bility, expressed as p\dh v; t; S\TH. The first parameter, v, denotes the
\par
information system's intrinsic vulnerability, i.e. without security
\par
protection, the success probability of an attack once launched.
\par
Note that the parameter v is intrinsic to the given information sys-
\par
tem and is only determined by the information system's config-
\par
uration, i.e. v is fixed for a given information system and is not
\par
affected by the external environment, such as attack types.
\par
Because v is a probability, 0 {$<$} v {$<$} 1.
\par
The second parameter, t, represents the attack probability, or
\par
the probability for the information system to receive a certain type
\par
of attacks. We assume that a firm's security investment is
