3.3. Network vulnerability
\par
As shown in Fig. 1, attackers can successfully attack firm 1 (or
\par
firm 2) in two ways: direct or indirect. A direct breach of firm 1
\par
occurs when attackers breach its information system directly, that
\par
is, the direct breach happens because of the firm's own security
\par
lapse. An indirect breach of firm 1 occurs when attackers breach
\par
the security of firm 2 firstly and the breach spreads to firm 1
\par
through their trusted interdependence relationship. We assume
\par
that the probability that an indirect breach of firm 1 occurs is a
\par
constant, q, given that firm 2 has been breached. This parameter
\par
measures the extent of trust interdependence relationship
\par
between a firm and its partner, and does not change with
\par
self-security investment. q is high when the extent of system
\par
access authority is high. q is also high when firms share more
\par
information. Because q is a probability, 0 {$<$} q {$<$} 1. Based on the
\par
description of network vulnerability, firm 1's investment can only
\par
reduce its own direct breach probability but cannot reduce its
\par
indirect breach probability. Firms can reduce indirect breach
\par
probability by redefining the trusted interdependence relationship
\par
with their partners (for instance, reducing the extent of system
\par
access authority or the extent of information sharing). We make
\par
the following assumptions about the total breach probability:
\par
Assumption 3. The total breach probability of firm 1 depends on
\par
not only the probability of direct breaches but also the probability
\par
of indirect breaches which is equal to the direct breach probability
\par
of firm 2 multiplied by the network vulnerability, q.
\par
Thus the total probability of a successful breach for firm 1 can
\par
be expressed as follows:
