We now show the impact of potential loss on the optimal
\par
investments of firms.
\par
Proposition 1. For both attack types, there exists a minimum
\par
potential loss, below which an interconnected firm does not need
\par
to invest in security and above which the firm's optimal invest-
\par
ment increases with the potential loss at a decreasing rate, but
\par
ultimately reaches a plateau.
\par
Several interesting implications emerge from this proposition.
\par
First, if the potential loss caused by an information security breach
\par
is sufficiently small, firms benefit from bearing the risk and not
\par
investing in security, even though firms simultaneously face direct
\par
and indirect attacks. In general, the minimum potential loss of tar-
\par
geted attacks below which firms have no incentive to invest in
\par
security is greater than that of opportunistic attacks. However,
\par
the minimum potential loss of opportunistic attacks can be greater
\par
than that of targeted attacks under some tight conditions. This1
