\par
In a secure-configuration, firms are willing to invest more in
\par
security as the intrinsic vulnerability increases, irrespective of
\par
attack types. In a dangerous-configuration, firms are still willing
\par
to invest more as the intrinsic vulnerability increases when they
\par
face targeted attacks, but are inclined to invest less as the intrinsic
\par
vulnerability increases when they face opportunistic attacks. Each
\par
firm strikes an appropriate balance between its risk exposure and
\par
the opportunity to mitigate the risk through investments in secur-
\par
ity (Cavusoglu et al., 2008). Thus firms face two risk types when
\par
they decide the security investment: risk of loss from security
\par
breach (security risk) and risk of over-spending in security (invest-
\par
ment risk). In a secure-configuration, firms are more concerned
\par
with security risks, irrespective of the attack type. In a dangerous-
\par
configuration, firms are still more concerned with security risks
\par
when they face targeted attacks, but care more about investment
\par
risks when they face opportunistic attacks.
\par
Thus, firms should identify which attack type they mainly face
\par
and the extent of intrinsic vulnerability before deciding on infor-
\par
mation security investments. When firms mainly face targeted
\par
attacks, they can ignore systems that have low intrinsic vulnerabil-
\par
ity and invest in systems that have a moderate or high intrinsic
\par
vulnerability. Because the security risk always outweighs the
\par
investment risk, firms should correspondingly increase
\par
investment, irrespective of the level of intrinsic vulnerability.
\par
This consequence is understandable because attackers are more
\par
likely to attack a system with a high intrinsic vulnerability if two
\par
systems are of same value to attackers. In addition, once a
\par
determined attacker decides to hack the targeted system, he/she
\par
is not easily stopped and will make every effort to complete the
\par
attack, and the loss caused by the targeted attack is usually
\par
catastrophic. Thus the security risk is always greater than the
\par
investment risk and firms should be more cautious and prevent
\par
breaches in their system as much as possible when they are under
\par
targeted attacks.
\par
When a firm mainly faces opportunistic attacks, it can ignore
\par
systems that have an overly low or overly high intrinsic vulnerabil-
\par
ity and invest in systems that have a moderate intrinsic vulnerabil-
\par
ity. We can explain this conclusion by analysing the features of an
\par
opportunistic attack. As described above, opportunistic attacks are
\par
pervasive, frequent, easy to address and tend to cause less damage
\par
to firms, and an initial investment has a more significant effect
\par
against opportunistic attack. Thus, opportunistic attacks do not
\par
easily breach the system when the intrinsic vulnerability is suffi-
\par
ciently small. However, an opportunistic attack could easily breach
\par
the system when the intrinsic vulnerability is sufficiently high so
\par
that the system is in a dangerous configuration. Furthermore,
\par
opportunistic attacks are usually contagious because they are per-
\par
vasive and frequent. In this situation, additional investment cannot
\par
prevent infections. Thus the investment risk outweighs the secur-
\par
ity risk and firms become more cautious about the investment risk.
\par
Ultimately, they are inclined to decrease the amount of invest-
\par
ment. Because the intrinsic vulnerability is decided by the config-
\par
uration of information system, firms should redefine system
\par
configuration that would reduce intrinsic vulnerability rather than
\par
invest against opportunistic attacks when the system is in a
\par
dangerous- configuration.
