\par
to explain this conclusion. Since the network consisting of the
\par
interconnected information systems shows negative externality,
\par
the firms in the network are more likely to attempt to offload relia-
\par
bility duties onto other firms when they become more intercon-
\par
nected. In order to solve the IDS problem, besides redefining the
\par
trusted interdependence relationship with their partners (for
\par
instance, reducing the extent of database access authority or the
\par
extent of information sharing) to reduce the network vulnerability,
\par
some economic incentives can be designed to internalize the nega-
\par
tive externality of information security. We discuss two economic
\par
incentives in Section 5.
\par
5. Economic incentives
\par
In Section 4, we have shown that if economic incentives are
\par
lacking, an interconnected firm tends to invest less in security as
\par
the network vulnerability increases. It would be more attractive
\par
to a firm if there are some economic incentives that not only can
\par
improve the firm's security level but also can reduce its total
\par
expected cost. In this section, we discuss two such effective eco-
\par
nomic incentives: liability and security information sharing. As a
\par
benchmark, we start by characterising the socially optimal welfare.
\par
Then we show how to employ the two economic incentives to
\par
induce socially optimal welfare.
\par
