\par
5.3. Security Information sharing
\par
Sharing information related to computer security breaches and
\par
unsuccessful breach attempts is a desirable way of supplementing
\par
the technical solutions to security problems for firms (Gordon
\par
et al., 2003). Because sharing alliances yield greater benefits in
\par
more competitive industries (Gal-Or \& Ghose, 2005), the US gov-
\par
ernment has developed many security-based information sharing
\par
organisations, such as the CERT Coordination Centre, the
\par
Information Sharing Analysis Centres, the Secret Service Electron
\par
Crimes Task Force, etc. We discuss the benefit of security informa-
\par
tion sharing and provide insight into the impact of security infor-
\par
mation sharing on both attack types in this section.
\par
We follow the formulation of Gordon et al. (2003) in defining
\par
security information sharing of information security investment.
\par
That is, if a firm shares security information with the other firm,
\par
a portion of the former's information security investment will
\par
benefit the latter without diminishing (or enhancing) the benefit
\par
to the former. Essentially, the intuition is that the disclosure of vul-
\par
nerabilities in a particular type of security technology by one firm
\par
leads the other firm to invest less in that technology or procure a
\par
smaller amount of that product. A direct consequence of such
\par
security information sharing would be pre-emptive cost savings
\par
in technology investment (Gal-Or \& Ghose, 2005). For simplify,
\par
we make the following assumptions about the security informa-
\par
tion sharing:
\par
