\par
Compared to the case of two firms in Proposition 4 and
\par
Proposition 5, Proposition 6 offers some new insights. First, with
\par
increasing number of firms, the optimal security investment of
\par
individual decision always decreases, the optimal security invest-
\par
ment of liability always increases, the optimal security investment
\par
of joint decision increases only when the number of firms is not too
\par
large, and the optimal security investment of security information
\par
sharing increase only when the number of firms is large enough.
\par
Second, the portion of liability (or the portion of security
\par
information sharing) that can enable both economic incentives to
\par
internalize the negative externality of interconnection is decided
\par
not only by the breach probability, but also by the number of firms.
\par
These findings highlight the importance of adequate assessment of
\par
the number of firms' partners, especially for those associations of
\par
security information sharing.
\par
7. Conclusions
\par
Although research into the information security has received
\par
some attention, economics considerations related to information
\par
security investment are rare. The current understanding of the
\par
optimal information security investment and the optimal
\par
economic incentives for interconnected firms is limited. In this
\par
paper, we employ game theory to model the relationship between
\par
the optimal information security investment and the characteris-
\par
tics of firms' security environment, and propose two economic
\par
incentives to solve the interdependent risk problem. In summary,
\par
we have made the following contributions to research. First, we
\par
model the optimal information security investments of firms by
\par
taking into account the reality that firms face different attack
\par
types. We follow prior studies by identifying targeted and oppor-
\par
tunistic attacks as two attack types that firms face and provide
\par
insights into firms' characteristics to better understand of their
\par
behaviours under different scenarios. Second, our model considers
\par
the information systems of interconnected firms, which is a more
\par
realistic assumption than the individual systems assumed by prior
\par
studies. Lastly, our study extends prior studies by discussing two
\par
effective economic incentives that not only can internalise the
\par
negative externality and improve a firm's security level but also
\par
can reduce its total expected cost.
\par
Our results offer some insights into information security man-
\par
agement practices.
\par
(1) Not all information security risks are worth fighting against.
\par
As the potential loss increases, it is unadvisable to increase
\par
the security investment proportionately. A firm is better
\par
off not investing in security until the potential loss reaches
\par
a certain value for a given attack type. Firms should stop
\par
investing in security and adopt other measures when the
\par
potential loss is catastrophic. These findings emphasise the
\par
importance of adequate assessment of firms' potential loss
\par
and identifying the nature of attacks.
\par
(2) A firm should correspondingly increase investment with
\par
intrinsic vulnerability when facing targeted attacks while
\par
focus on those systems that fall into the midrange of intrinsic
\par
vulnerability when facing opportunistic attacks. Since intrin-
\par
sic vulnerability is decided by the configuration of informa-
\par
tion system, firms should redefine system configuration
\par
that would reduce intrinsic vulnerability rather than invest
\par
against opportunistic attacks when the system is in a danger-
\par
ous- configuration.
\par
(3) Firms are unwilling to invest in security and often offloading
\par
reliability problems on others when the trusted interdepen-
\par
dence relationship becomes tighter in the absence of eco-
\par
nomic incentives. When the network vulnerability is less
\par
than e 2, the network vulnerability has a stronger impact
\par
on a firm's investment when firm faces targeted attacks than
\par
facing opportunistic attacks.
\par
(4) The optimal investment of joint decision can increase the
\par
security level, decrease the total expected cost, and internalise
\par
the negative externality of network vulnerability. Firms have
\par
more incentives to jointly decide their investments when they
\par
mainly face opportunistic instead of targeted attacks.
\par
(5) In order to solve the prisoner's dilemma in the information
\par
security investment game, besides redefining the trusted
\par
interdependence relationship with their partners to reduce
\par
the network vulnerability, liability and security information
\par
sharing, two economic incentives, can be used to internalize
\par
the negative externality of information security. We find
\par
that if the rules are set properly, both of them can effectively
\par
internalise the negative externality, improve a firm's secur-
\par
ity level, and reduce the total expected cost. For liability,
\par
the legal system should enact rules to specify the appropri-
\par
ate portion of liability. The negative externality of intercon-
\par
nection will not be overcome if the portion of liability is too
\par
low but overinvestment in security could result if the por-
\par
tion of liability is too high. For security information sharing,
\par
associations of security information sharing should stipulate
\par
the rule that each member's portion of security information
\par
sharing should be greater than an ``effective value'' in order
\par
to overcome the negative externality of interconnection.6146 Y. Wu et al. / Expert Systems with Applications 42 (2015) 6132--6146
\par
(6) Both economic incentives are effective in the case of three or
\par
more firms. With more firms, the optimal investment of lia-
\par
bility always increases but the optimal investment of secur-
\par
ity information sharing increases only when the number of
\par
firms is large enough. In the case of three or more firms,
\par
the effective portion of liability (or the effective portion of
\par
security information sharing) is decided not only by the
\par
breach probability, but also by the number of firms. These
\par
insights draw attention to the many trade-offs firms often
\par
face and the importance of accurate assessment of firms'
\par
security environment, including potential loss, the nature
\par
of attacks, intrinsic vulnerability, network vulnerability
\par
and the number of partners. Firms can evaluate these factors
\par
by using many methods like the expert grading method and
\par
decision tree. For example, Huang, Lin, Lin, and Sun (2013)
\par
formulate an analysis model to express the security grades
\par
of software vulnerability. Andoh-Baidoo and Osei-Bryson
\par
(2007) use decision tree to analyse the observed cumulative
\par
abnormal stock market return, which is one measure of the
\par
loss of the breached firms.
\par
As with all analytical models, this study has limitations. First,
\par
the information security investment game has two participants:
\par
firms and hackers. In our analysis, we ignore the behaviour of
\par
hackers and only consider the firms' behaviours. Second, we use
\par
two breach probability functions to represent targeted and oppor-
\par
tunistic attacks, assuming that they are independent of each
\par
other. However, targeted and opportunistic attacks may occur
\par
simultaneously in the real world, which we do not consider in
\par
this study. Our study points to several future directions for
\par
research. For instance, this work could be extended by modelling
\par
the behaviours between firms and hackers when firms are inter-
\par
connected and hackers share information. Another interesting
\par
research is to design the incentive mechanisms that could
\par
encourage firms to decide jointly and share security information,
\par
and guide the legal system to stipulate rules to enforce compli-
\par
ance of firms. In addition, information security investments will
\par
be different in the situation of multiple breaches, and our work
\par
can be extended to include this situation. Lastly, the managerial
\par
implications of our findings can be examined with empirical data
\par
in a future study.
