\par
Beekeeping innovation for sustaining rural
\par
livelihoods. A success story
\par
Nonita T. Yap* and John F. Devlin
\par
School of Environmental Design and Rural Development,
\par
University of Guelph,
\par
Guelph, ON Canada N1G 2W1
\par
Email: nyap@uoguelph.ca
\par
Email: jdevlin@uoguelph.ca
\par
*Corresponding author
\par
Abstract: Over the last six decades, international development assistance
\par
agencies have tried to introduce innovations in developing countries to
\par
stimulate development and improve the quality of life of the poor. The results
\par
have been mixed. This paper presents the results of an evaluation of a project
\par
introducing modern beekeeping practices to increase household incomes among
\par
small farmers in Vietnam. The Project exceeded its targets in terms of the
\par
number of farmers who adopted the innovation and the number of households
\par
with increased incomes. The farmers also reported development benefits
\par
beyond those originally envisioned -- improved health, happier family
\par
relationships, and greater respect. The successful diffusion is attributed to the
\par
readily observable, personally and collectively felt benefits of beekeeping,
\par
harmony with the social practice of sharing, and the efforts of the change agent
\par
to reduce the complexity of the innovation and to integrate local farmers'
\par
knowledge in the training and extension activities.
\par
Keywords: Vietnam small farmers; DOI; diffusion of innovation; modern
\par
beekeeping; development success; innovation for sustainability.
\par
Reference to this paper should be made as follows: Yap, N.T. and
\par
Devlin, J.F. (2015) `Beekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods.
\par
A success story', Int. J. Innovation and Sustainable Development, Vol. 9,
\par
No. 2, pp.103--117.
\par
Biographical notes: Nonita T. Yap holds a PhD in chemistry from the
\par
University of Alberta and a Masters' degree in Environmental Studies from
\par
Dalhousie University. A professor at the University of Guelph, she teaches
\par
Environmental Impact Assessment, Disaster Planning and Management and
\par
Planning Industrial Ecology. She writes on diffusion of innovation, cleaner
\par
production, corporate social responsibility, disaster planning and environmental
\par
impact assessment. Conversant in six languages, she has worked in nearly
\par
20 countries in Asia, the Americas, Africa and the Middle East developing,
\par
managing, monitoring or evaluating projects.
\par
John F. Devlin holds a PhD in Rural Studies from the University of Guelph.
\par
His primary areas of academic interest include: the role of the state in
\par
development; environmental assessment and public participation; and rural
\par
policy. He is currently researching collaborative regional economic
\par
development, local food systems, community-based water monitoring, and
\par
optimism bias in mega-projects. He has co-edited one book, Ecological
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2015 Inderscience Enterprises Ltd.104 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
Agriculture in Eastern and Southern Africa, and numerous articles and
\par
conference presentations on environment and development issues. He has
\par
conducted research or provided consulting services in Honduras, Bolivia, the
\par
Philippines, Vietnam, Egypt, Mozambique, South Africa, Zambia, Zimbabwe
\par
and Ukraine.
\par
1 Introduction
\par
Over the last 60 years, international development assistance agencies, seeking to
\par
stimulate development and improve the quality of life of the poor, have tried to introduce
\par
innovations in developing countries -- new crop varieties, water harvesting and sanitation
\par
management, family planning, policy reforms. They have met with varying degrees of
\par
success (e.g., Moris, 1981; Rogers, 2003; Rondinelli, 1983; Smillie, 1991). The factors
\par
influencing the rate of diffusion need to be better understood since all development
\par
interventions seek to introduce innovations and unless an innovation is widely adopted its
\par
contribution to economic or social development is minimal.
\par
This paper seeks to explain the outcomes of a donor-funded development project that
\par
sought to introduce modern beekeeping practices among small scale farmers in Vietnam.
\par
An end-of-project evaluation found far more adopters than originally targeted and far
\par
more households with increased incomes than anticipated. Equally interesting, the
\par
farmers reported benefits beyond those envisioned by the Project -- improved health,
\par
happiness, and respectful relationships. The authors draw upon insights in the diffusion of
\par
innovation (DOI) scholarship to analyse these rather atypical development outcomes.
\par
The paper first defines some key terms used in the paper, then reviews the relevant
\par
literature on diffusion of innovation. The project context is summarised specifically, the
\par
Project objectives, targets and project site. The methodology adopted is described,
\par
followed by a discussion and analysis of the findings. The paper closes with some
\par
reflections on the contribution and limitations of the study, suggesting areas that merit
\par
further investigation.
\par
1.1 Definitions of terms
\par
In this paper innovation refers to ``any non-trivial change in product or process, if there
\par
has been no previous experience'' (Nelson and Winter, 1977, p.48), not different from
\par
Roger's (2003, p.12) ``an idea, practice, or object perceived as new by an individual
\par
or other unit'', and consistent with the stipulation in OECD's Oslo Manual that ``\dots{} for
\par
change \dots{} to be considered an innovation, it must be new (or significantly improved) \dots ''
\par
(OECD, 2005, p.17). Technology is viewed as consisting of both hardware and software
\par
(Galtung, 1979). It is ``a more complex bundle of knowledge, with much of it embodied
\par
in a wide range of different artefacts, people, procedures \dots{} know-how and arrangements
\par
needed to integrate these elements in an enormously variable range of different
\par
production systems'' (Bell and Albu, 1999, p.1717). As used here, diffusion refers to both
\par
planned and spontaneous spread of innovation. The focus is on diffusion as a process, not
\par
as an outcome.Beekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 105
\par
1.2 The challenge of diffusing innovation
\par
The literature on diffusion of innovation is vast and spans many research traditions.
\par
The studies cover different items of innovation introduced to different categories
\par
of actors. They range from corn hybrid among Iowa farmers (Ryan and Gross,
\par
1943), HIV-AIDS prevention in San Francisco's gay community (Rogers, 2004),
\par
information technology among small and medium enterprises (Konstadakopulos, 2006),
\par
organisational models among large businesses (Fligstein, 1985), policy reforms among
\par
states (Berry, 1994), and national governments (Pitlik, 2007), to protest concepts and
\par
repertoires of action among social movements (Andrews and Biggs, 2006; Myers, 2000).
\par
A common observation is that the rate of diffusion is generally slow.
\par
Economists with a neoclassical orientation attribute the rate of adoption to the actor's
\par
response to price (e.g., Griliches, 1957; Mansfield, 1961) suggesting that diffusion is
\par
unproblematic, or if it is problem, not a very interesting one (Geroski, 2000). Others view
\par
the adoption decision as a more involved process. Dixit and Pindyk (1995) for example
\par
argue that the decision to adopt an innovation is similar to any other investment decision.
\par
Uncertainty about future benefits and irreversibility (and therefore potential sunk costs),
\par
enter importantly, and delay is a real value option. This is echoed by Hall (2003). ``The
\par
choice being made is not a choice between adopting and not adopting but a choice
\par
between adopting now or deferring the decision until later'' (p.3). Students of
\par
organisational behaviour see this calculation as strongly influenced by the potential
\par
adopter's resources and technological capabilities (Barney, 1991; Hart, 1995), past
\par
experience, absorptive capacity (Cohen and Levinthal, 1990), ability to forge horizontal
\par
relations with others and vertical relations with stakeholder institutions, to make informed
\par
choices (Lichtenthaler and Lichtenthaler, 2009).
\par
The economic historian Rosenberg (1972) reviewed the diffusion of innovations
\par
during the industrial revolution and concluded that there are additional factors affecting
\par
the diffusion rate. There is feedback between adoption and implementation. The skill
\par
level of the workers (to adapt the innovation to the context and needs of the adopter)
\par
as well as the state of the supplying capital goods sector matter. Rosenberg's notion
\par
of feedback and reinvention has seen rich empirical support in the policy and
\par
management literature (e.g., Hays, 1996), and more recently, in the rural development
\par
literature (e.g., Ahmad et al., 2007; Araya et al., 2007; Gebey et al., 2010).
\par
Others frame the diffusion problem as a social process involving many people making
\par
choices, often in an interdependent manner (Geroski, 2000). The decision criteria are
\par
socially constructed and developed internally by the community of potential adopters
\par
(Nelson et al., 2004).
\par
Each research tradition has tended to focus rather exclusively, on different
\par
independent variables. Among the earliest attempts to bring together these different
\par
disciplinary perspectives was that by Katz et al. (1963). They defined diffusion as ``the
\par
acceptance, over time, of some specific item -- an idea or practice, by individuals, groups
\par
or other adopting units, linked to specific channels of communication, to a social
\par
structure, and to a given system of values, or culture'' (p.237). They reviewed diffusion
\par
studies in anthropology, marketing, sociology, public health and uncovered major
\par
challenges in the task of integration. There was inconsistent definition of what constitutes
\par
`acceptance' -- first use or sustained use, acceptance of form or of form, meaning and106 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
function, methodological difficulties in determining time of acceptance, classification and
\par
complexity of item being diffused, lack of information on channel used in transmitting
\par
information or influence, and on the characteristics of the adopter and the social
\par
environment. The poverty of theory in diffusion studies, they argue, arises from ``the
\par
ostensible incommensurability of diffusing item, their refusal to hold still in transit, the
\par
complexity of their interactions with the cultures, social structures and media systems in
\par
which potential adopters are embedded and the difficulty in reconciling voluntary action
\par
and external imposition\dots ''(p.145).
\par
Analytical integration was also attempted by the rural sociologist Rogers (1962,
\par
2003). Between 49 to 87\% of the variance in the adoption rate can be attributed to 5 sets
\par
of variables. The first is the perceived attributes of the innovation. This refers to
\par
{$\bullet$} its relative advantage over the existing or alternative practice
\par
{$\bullet$} its compatibility with existing norms, values, beliefs, past experiences and needs of
\par
potential adopters
\par
{$\bullet$} its technological complexity
\par
{$\bullet$} whether the innovation is amenable to experimentation
\par
{$\bullet$} its performance, observable.
\par
All but one -- technological complexity -- is positively related to the rate of adoption.
\par
The second variable is the type of communication channel, mass media or
\par
interpersonal contact, used to deliver the information to the potential adopter. The
\par
characteristics of the social system, i.e., norms, degree of network interconnectedness,
\par
collective or individualistic decision-making, forms the third group of variables. The rate
\par
of adoption is also influenced by the change agent's promotion efforts. Finally the rate
\par
varies with the type of innovation decision, whether it is made by an individual member
\par
of a system, by the entire system collectively, or by a relatively few individuals in the
\par
system.
\par
Rogers' framework influenced the design of the survey questionnaire and the
\par
questions posed in the semi-structured key informant and focus group interviews held
\par
with the participants in this research (Mayring, 2007).1
\par
2 Beekeeping development and rural extension project
\par
The Beekeeping Development and Rural Extension Project (The Project) was funded by
\par
the Canadian International Development Agency (CIDA) through the Association of
\par
Universities and Colleges of Canada (AUCC). It was administered by the University of
\par
Guelph and ran from 2007 to 2012.
\par
The Project partnered with the Vietnam Bee Research and Development Center
\par
(BRDC) to enhance rural livelihoods by introducing modern beekeeping among small
\par
scale farmers. BRDC is the primary organisation in Vietnam involved in training rural
\par
farmers and extension agents in beekeeping. BRDC extension approaches had tended to
\par
be top down, and not acknowledge farmers' local knowledge. A secondary goal was thus
\par
to improve BRDC's training capacity with participatory methodologies.Beekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 107
\par
2.1 The context
\par
The Project was implemented in six communes in Ha Tinh, a coastal province in
\par
North Central Vietnam, with a population of 1.3 million in 2006 and with over 90\% of its
\par
workforce described as low skilled, compared to the national average of 75\% (ISPONRE,
\par
2009). Located about 340 km south of Hanoi, Ha Tinh has an area of 6025.6 km2
\par
and more than 400 km of small and major rivers. There are three natural geographic
\par
zones -- high mountainous zone; hilly, mountainous zone and coastal plains.
\par
The province has extreme hot summers and severe cold winters, with floods and
\par
storms in the fall. The highest temperature is over 40{$^\circ$}C, observed from April to June.
\par
The lowest temperature is between 0.7--7{$^\circ$}C observed from December to February. The
\par
average annual rainfall is between 2300--3000 mm with two rainy seasons, April to May
\par
and November to December (ISPONRE, 2009).
\par
There are over 300,000 ha of forest land with natural forests constituting about 55\%,
\par
production forests, 30\% and protected forests around 15\%. The plantation forests are
\par
primarily planted to eucalypti, acacias, rhamnoneuron, and casuarinas (ISPONRE, 2009).
\par
Agriculture, forestry and fishery contribute 35.5\% of total GDP. The main plants
\par
cultivated are food crops (rice, corn), short-duration cash crops (bean, peanut), long-
\par
duration industrial crops (tea, rubber) and fruit plants. The main livestock raised are
\par
cattle, poultry and deer (ISPONRE 2009). The average number of farm plots held per
\par
household is 2.18 and the average landholding is 0.40 ha.
\par
2.2 Beekeeping and sustainable livelihoods
\par
Bees are a renewable resource freely available from the environment. Beekeeping
\par
does not require ownership of land and little capital is needed. Small-scale beekeeping
\par
is not particularly labour intensive; tending to bees can be done after peak work times.
\par
Honey bees pollinate many local cash crops. In Vietnam these are rambutans, longans,
\par
lychees, durians, star fruits, beans, squashes, melons, sesame seeds, and coffee.
\par
Beekeeping can therefore present income generating opportunities in rural areas and
\par
complement farming incomes. Beekeeping is also environmentally friendly because
\par
flowering trees need to be protected and use of pesticides discouraged (Ingram and
\par
Njikeu, 2011).
\par
Several honey producing indigenous bee species are found in Vietnam with A. cerana
\par
and A. dorsata being the two primary species. A. cerana, a cavity-nesting species,
\par
can be managed in hives as simple as a hollowed-out section from the trunk of a coconut
\par
tree. A. dorsata on the other hand, builds large exposed nests high in trees and usually in
\par
forests.
\par
At the start of the Project many households were already engaged in honey-hunting of
\par
A. dorsata bee colonies in the forest. A few maintained log hives but these are difficult to
\par
manage. There were some top-bar hives, usually made of a vertical hollow log, with
\par
notches cut into the interior wall of the hive to support a frame used by the bees to build
\par
combs. Because the cavity is circular and each top bar of a different length, top bars are
\par
not interchangeable. There were a few `modern' square-box hives. The yields of honey
\par
were very low, usually in the range of 2--3 kgs per year.108 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
2.3 Why the need for innovation
\par
In traditional beekeeping the hives cannot be divided (to increase the number) or joined
\par
(to strengthen weak ones). Nor can they be examined to check the condition of the queen
\par
and worker bees. Traditional beekeepers will generally not be familiar with the seasonal
\par
cycle of colonies. Because of the type of hives used they do not know how to rear queens,
\par
detect diseases and brood conditions, or how to divide or join colonies. To collect honey
\par
they have to cut out and destroy the combs (honey squeezing). This traditional activity is
\par
therefore inefficient.
\par
One of the many advantages of modern beekeeping is the use of light movable
\par
frames. The movable frames enable regular inspection of the conditions of the queen and
\par
the worker bees. Their light weight allows easy handling by women and children.
\par
2.4 Choosing the project beneficiaries and introducing the innovation
\par
The Project wanted to target the poorer communes in two mountainous districts
\par
of Ha Tinh province -- Huong Son and Vu Quang. The Peoples' Committees of the two
\par
districts recommended which communes in their districts would participate in the Project,
\par
based on the available floral resources and the number of traditional beekeepers.
\par
Son Truong, Son Tho, Son Diem, Son Mai in Huong Son District and Son Tay and
\par
Duc Linh in Vu Quang District were eventually selected. Commune meetings were held
\par
with BRDC staff. Between 50 and 60 farmers attended each meeting.
\par
The farmers were asked to recommend who should participate in the Project with
\par
three conditions: maximum of 30 farmers from each commune, 30\% of whom should be
\par
female, and 30\% of whom should be from poor households. There was a greater demand
\par
for participation than was planned for. In the 5-year period of the Project, 195 farmers,
\par
71 of whom were female (36\%), received training on basic and advance beekeeping
\par
practices in two workshops, delivered one year apart in each of the six communes. These
\par
workshops were designed and delivered by BRDC personnel who themselves received
\par
prior training in participatory techniques. Table 1 outlines the beekeeping technology
\par
package introduced.
\par
Table 1 Beekeeping technology package
\par
Hardware Software Hives, movable frames, wax foundation, honey extractor, bee garments (masks,
\par
gloves), and smoker during harvesting
\par
Honeybee biology; lifescyle and processes at individual bee and colony level such
\par
as swarming and absconding; how to identify pollen and nectar sources; how to
\par
inspect a hive; how to capture and hive a swarm of bees; how to divide and
\par
combine colonies; how to extract honey; how to feed bees sugar and pollen; how to
\par
rear queens under normal and emergency conditions; how to build most of the
\par
beekeeping hardware; how to install sheets of wax foundation in frames; basic
\par
information on bee pests -- moths, wasps, sacbrood and foulbrood, and bee diseases;
\par
how to ward off pests and treat diseases; seasonal management during low season;
\par
buildup, honey flow and harvest; monitoring of honey quality and marketing
\par
At the end of the basic training each participant was given two beehives, and each
\par
commune prodded to form a beekeeping club. All six did. The Project developed and
\par
distributed a Beekeeping Manual to all participants. A DVD on selected audiovisualBeekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 109
\par
materials on beekeeping was developed by 2008 and used in all training courses. Copies
\par
were left with each beekeeping club.
\par
3 Methodology
\par
The authors were engaged to undertake the end-of-project evaluation. It was decided to
\par
adopt both audit and participatory evaluation approaches (Armytage, 2011).2
\par
The evaluation was in two stages. The first was a two-week reconnaissance mission
\par
(February--March 2012) during which the authors met with farmers in all six communes
\par
to explain the purpose of the evaluation, what it entailed and how much of their time it
\par
would likely demand. Those who agreed to participate were asked for their preference in
\par
terms of days of the week, time to start and to stop. This was adhered to in the formal
\par
evaluation undertaken in April 2012.
\par
Project documents were systematically reviewed after which permission from the
\par
Project Director was secured to collect more data than was needed for evaluating Project
\par
performance. From a development perspective it was important not only to determine the
\par
effectiveness of the Project but also to understand why.
\par
A mixed methods research strategy, specifically mixed-method design (Johnson and
\par
Onwuegbuzie, 2005, p.20)3 was seen as offering the best opportunities for answering the
\par
research questions of interest. A quantitative (sociometric) survey would indicate whether
\par
the Project was successful or not, and to what extent, but not why. Key informant and
\par
focus group interviews would. They would allow the researchers to see beyond the
\par
numbers (e.g., what did the increased incomes mean for the household?). Bringing
\par
together the results from surveys, focus groups, and interviews of different stakeholders
\par
also allowed data triangulation (Johnson and Onwuegbuzie, 2004).
\par
The survey questionnaire administered had two parts. Part 1 collected information on
\par
socio-economic background, farming practices, other livelihood activities, honey
\par
production levels and incomes of the participating households. Part 2 posed targeted or
\par
cued questions and queried into, inter alia:
\par
{$\bullet$} the participant's initial sources of beekeeping information
\par
{$\bullet$} problems encountered during initial implementation and source of technical
\par
assistance then and now
\par
{$\bullet$} benefits and disadvantages of beekeeping
\par
{$\bullet$} impacts if any, of the innovations on household decision making process and work
\par
distribution.
\par
Those who expanded their beehives were asked when they made that decision; whether
\par
or not they had trained others in these new bee management techniques, and if so, how
\par
many. Where the question required selection of responses, `OTHER' was one of the
\par
options. That the diffusion questions involve recall and retrospection was not considered
\par
problematic because the experience and events happened within the previous four years.
\par
The survey was followed by semi-structured interviews and focus group discussions.
\par
These were used to elaborate upon Part 2 of the survey questionnaire as well as pose
\par
additional questions, e.g., continuing Project relevance to farmers' needs, impacts if any
\par
on gender relations in the household, whether or not BRDC solicited their knowledge and110 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
integrated their feedback, and if yes, how. These were conducted by two teams; each
\par
consisted of a senior researcher, a BRDC staff, a graduate research assistant and a
\par
translator.
\par
A total of 193 farmers participated in the April evaluation.4 Data were collected from
\par
192 completed survey questionnaires (1 was discarded), 111 semi-structured interviews
\par
(102 beekeepers and 9 BRDC staff involved with the Project), and 16 focus group
\par
discussions (1 with all BRDC staff, 9 with district and commune extension agents,
\par
and 6 with farmers from each individual commune).
\par
The data from Part One of the survey were analysed using EXCEL 2007 to calculate
\par
descriptive statistics. Analysis of the data from Part Two and the extensive key informant
\par
and focus group interviews was very time consuming but fairly straightforward, as they
\par
were not exclusively open ended but included dichotomous and targeted questions (Hsieh
\par
and Shannon, 2005).
\par
4 Results and discussion
\par
The average age of the participating farmers was 47.3 (range 23--71). More than half
\par
(62\%) had prior experience in beekeeping -- primarily in traditional beekeeping. Son
\par
Truong had the highest number (78\%), followed closely by Son Tho (76\%). Son Mai and
\par
Son Diem each reported 64\% and Duc Linh, 51\%. Son Tay had the least (39\%).
\par
All 192 respondents adopted the innovation and expanded their number of hives, with
\par
65\% doing so within six months after the basic training, an additional 10\% after one year,
\par
and the rest within six months after the advance training (i.e., 1 and a half year after the
\par
basic training).
\par
One of the Project targets was to have ``70\% of trainees become successful
\par
beekeepers with 15\% making more than Cdn\$200/yr from beekeeping''. This included
\par
income from the sale of all bee products, i.e., honey, hives, frames and queens. The end
\par
of the project saw virtually 100\% of trainees still producing honey and 68 beekeepers
\par
(36\%) reporting income over Cdn\$200 per year.
\par
An overwhelming majority (97\%) claimed to have trained others -- spouses, children,
\par
other family members, neighbours and customers to a total of 1171. Son Diem claimed
\par
the most (307) and Son Tay the least (114). The commune extension agents also claimed
\par
to have trained 72 additional farmers within and outside the original 6 communes. These
\par
results were interpreted as indicating a high level of diffusion.
\par
How might the successful diffusion of modern beekeeping among the target
\par
population be explained?
\par
4.1 Perceived attributes of the innovation
\par
To the question, ``In adopting and implementing beekeeping did you have to make major
\par
changes in your daily routine?'' majority in Truong Son district (Son Diem, 85\%, Son
\par
Tho, 87\%, Son Truong, 91\%, and Son Mai, 94\%) answered `No', but 91\% of Duc Linh
\par
and 90\% of Son Tay respondents, both of Vu Quang District, said `Yes'. Note that these
\par
last two communes had the lowest number of participants with prior experience in
\par
beekeeping. Most of the changes in Duc Linh were in economic activities (e.g., wage
\par
labour), those in Son Tay, in non-economic (e.g., cooking, washing). Interestingly, when
\par
asked whether there were any disadvantages brought about by beekeeping, the responseBeekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 111
\par
was a unanimous `No', suggesting that the cost of the changes did not outweigh the
\par
benefits.
\par
The top three benefits of the new bee management techniques were `increased
\par
income' (cited by 94\%), followed by `improved health' (83\%), and `improved family
\par
relationship' (51\%). Asked to elaborate on the non-economic benefits of beekeeping, the
\par
farmers happily obliged.
\par
``You have to be patient when you work with bees. From the bees I learned to
\par
be patient with my family as well, so they are happier.'' (KII 111, 12 April,
\par
2012)
\par
``When I come home from the farm at the end of a hard day and I see the bees,
\par
I feel so happy.'' (KII 164, 22 April, 2012)
\par
``I use bees to educate my children the lessons of hard work and unity \dots{} Honey
\par
collection day is a social event, everyone comes to help -- my children,
\par
neighbours.'' (KII 26, 29 April, 2012)
\par
Thirty seven percent of the beekeepers interviewed said beekeeping improved family
\par
relations.5 Women beekeepers in particular were effusive. Household tasks are now better
\par
shared and decisions made jointly where it had not been before the Project. Several added
\par
that the income they are bringing to the household has earned them `higher respect' from
\par
their husbands.
\par
``Because I earn money... I have gained respect from my husband \dots{} we are
\par
happier.'' (KII 54, 26 April, 2012)
\par
This shift in decision-making and household responsibilities was confirmed by male
\par
beekeepers.
\par
``I do the most beekeeping myself but when I sell honey my wife keeps the
\par
money and decides how it would be spent''. (KII 84, 19 April, 2012)
\par
``Because we have more hives we must discuss scheduling with each other \dots{}
\par
cooking and laundry very specifically.'' (KII 118, 13 April, 2012)
\par
The impact of women's ability to earn incomes on their status in the household and
\par
quality of life is consistent with observations in other beekeeping projects. In Cameroon
\par
the noticeable impacts included ``women being able to afford to give birth in hospitals
\par
instead of the bush'' (Ingram and Njikeu, 2011, p.47).
\par
4.1.1 Reducing technological complexity through mentoring
\par
In the first year BRDC staff came to Ha Tinh about once every two months, after which
\par
they visited once every three or four months. The commune extension agents visited
\par
more frequently but could only spend part of their time on beekeeping problems. The
\par
farmers said they do not hesitate to phone the extension agents and generally get
\par
responses in less than a week.
\par
All beekeeping trainees, including those with prior experience, encountered serious
\par
problems within the first year of implementation. They were asked ``Who helped you
\par
solve the problems then?'' The most frequent response was `BRDC extension agents'
\par
(88\%), `BRDC beekeeping manual' came second (77\%), followed closely by the
\par
`beekeeping club' (67\%), district and commune extension agents (46\%). Forty percent of
\par
the respondents, including those without previous beekeeping experience, reported they
\par
also tried to solve the problems themselves.112 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
All continue to have problems. `Lack of time', `lack of money', `lack of technical
\par
assistance' and the `weather' received equal ratings. When queried ``This time who helps
\par
you solve your problem?'' the most important source of assistance cited was `BRDC
\par
Manual' (75\%), followed closely by the Beekeeping Club (70\%). `BRDC extension
\par
agents' is cited only by 41\%, and district/commune extension agents, 34\%. Towards the
\par
end of the Project, the Manual and the Beekeeping club replaced BRDC extension agents
\par
as the major source of technical assistance (See Table 2). The beekeepers seem to be
\par
starting to rely on personal and commune-level resources.
\par
Table 2 Source of technical assistance cited by beekeepers
\par
Source Within year 1 of project (\%) Now (\%)
\par
BRDC extension agents 88 41
\par
Beekeeping manual 77 75
\par
Beekeeping club 67 70
\par
Commune extension agents 46 34
\par
4.1.2 Reducing technological complexity through peer-to-peer network
\par
The beekeeping clubs were left to operate as they wished, with BRDC providing support
\par
only when needed, such as bringing in supplies of wax foundation from Hanoi during
\par
their field visits. The clubs coordinate the sharing of equipment such as honey extractors
\par
and most recently, hydrometers for measuring water content of honey.
\par
The size of these clubs varies from 30 to 45, with both men and women members.
\par
This is not consistent with local practice where association memberships are exclusively
\par
men or women. Some clubs restrict membership to Project participants, others have been
\par
more open. Most clubs meet every two months, each meeting lasting between two to four
\par
hours and attended regularly by 90--95\% of members. Asked what benefits they get from
\par
these meetings, all suggested the sharing of largely tacit knowledge.6
\par
``If I do not understand I can ask more experienced beekeepers. And older
\par
people have weaker eyes so they can't see disease so they ask me to look and
\par
tell them what it is.'' (KII 191, 23 April, 2012)
\par
``I told others how to keep bees warm in the winter using newspaper and tell
\par
them that they should feed bees sugar. And others tell me when wasps attack to
\par
use a badminton racket.'' (KII 61, 25 April, 2012)
\par
This is not dissimilar to that reported in beekeeping projects in northern Malawi where
\par
improvements made to traditional beekeeping were introduced through a beekeeping club
\par
(Illigner et al., 1998). The value of information sharing in developing local capacity to
\par
reinvent was articulated by a widely acclaimed woman beekeeper innovator in Tigray.
\par
Copying should be there. I took some ideas from the modern hive \dots{} and I
\par
added mine. People also come and see what I am doing. I know they will copy,
\par
as I did, if they find it good. Thus, we live by sharing. (Araya at al., 2007, p.4)
\par
4.2 Communication channels used
\par
Beekeeping requires both knowledge of bee biology and bee diseases, and skills in
\par
managing the different stages of the lifecycle of bees. The training delivered by theBeekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 113
\par
Project covered both the practices and the underlying scientific principles -- how it works
\par
and why it works (See Table 1).
\par
According to the farmers, what made the training easily understandable was that
\par
lecturing was minimal, the sessions were interactive, the discussion groups small, and
\par
there was abundant use of videos, photographs, diagrams.
\par
The dissemination path followed in the Project fits the mixed information source --
\par
central (in the training sessions) and word-of-mouth (through the beekeeping clubs),
\par
explained by Rogers as particularly effective with homophilous system.7
\par
4.3 Characteristics of the social system
\par
Two other benefits were mentioned by the farmers -- `maintaining cultural tradition'
\par
(41\%) and ``increased respect from the community and relatives'' (34\%).
\par
All but one of the beekeepers gives away between 5\% and 30\% of their honey
\par
to relatives and friends. Asked why, the explanations given were consistent: ``sharing
\par
the good things that one has with friends and loved ones is part of Vietnamese tradition''.
\par
The social system clearly values sharing of information and good fortune.
\par
4.4 The promotion efforts of the change agent
\par
All these said, it is argued that the change agent -- BRDC -- had the most pivotal influence
\par
on the rate of diffusion. They effectively reduced the technological complexity of the
\par
innovation and helped build the farmers' self-confidence.
\par
BRDC unpacked the hardware, through the training, and the software, through the
\par
training but also through the follow up mentoring and the beekeeping clubs. The two
\par
hives given after the first training enabled the farmer to experiment and observe, and the
\par
beekeeping clubs, to continually learn from each other. This clearly enhanced the
\par
farmers' self-confidence. A BRDC staff described one such instance:
\par
``There was a small group of women who were talking about bees that live
\par
under soil, and they discussed the possibility of making clay hives. The women
\par
thought they could do it, and they were successful -- they were able to make the
\par
hives, and the hives cost no money to make\dots '' (KII 207, 13 April, 2012)
\par
The extraordinary efforts made by BRDC trainers were recounted by the Canadian
\par
Project Director (Personal communication, Sept 2012):
\par
``In 2007 we brought in foulbrood, sac brood, and wax moths; we also created a
\par
queenless colony in which laying worker bees developed, destroying the hive in
\par
the process. The trainees would break into smaller groups and go from hive to
\par
hive, discovering the conditions, symptoms of each hive and then talking about
\par
the causes\dots{} In 2008 we could not find any colonies with foulbrood and had to
\par
rely on photos and video. That spurred the idea of photo hives that can be
\par
inspected any season, under any conditions, to diagnose diseases, pests, and
\par
abnormal condition.''
\par
The farmers were asked whether and how BRDC responded to their feedback. There was
\par
no shortage of examples.
\par
``BRDC learned how the locals, rather than hunting bees in the forest, hung log
\par
hives, and the bees would fly to these hanging log hives in the winter... BRDC
\par
learned about the different sizes and measurements for the entrance and exit of114 N.T. Yap and J.F. Devlin
\par
the beehives from the locals. And they incorporated this in the training course.''
\par
(KII 94, 20 April, 2012)
\par
``According to modern beekeeping when adding a new queen you should leave
\par
the queen out of the hive for one day and then put it back in the night.
\par
But in traditional beekeeping you put the bee into a cup of honey and then
\par
put it back in immediately. If you do not put the queen in the honey the bees
\par
will not recognise you as family and they will leave. This technique was
\par
acknowledged by BRDC staff and they changed training to include that
\par
information.'' (KII 124, 13 April, 2012)
\par
BRDC trainers monitored their own effectiveness and made appropriate adjustments
\par
when necessary. The Canadian Project Director recalled:
\par
``In the first two advance courses in 2008, they [BRDC] taught a very technical
\par
method of queen rearing (grafting) in which worker larvae are transferred into
\par
queen cups made by the beekeeper. When we went back a year later \dots{} we
\par
discovered that almost no one was using that technique, so we quickly adjusted
\par
what we planned to do a few days later \dots [We switched to] a simpler
\par
emergency queen rearing method that anyone can do easily.''
\par
5 Conclusions, limitations of the study and areas for future research
\par
The University of Guelph's Beekeeping and Rural Extension Project offers valuable
\par
insights for the design, implementation and evaluation of development projects seeking to
\par
introduce new ideas, practices and technologies.
\par
Unlike many innovations introduced over the last 60 years by various international
\par
development assistance agencies and non-governmental organisations the Project was
\par
remarkably successful. Far more farmers adopted the new beekeeping techniques than
\par
originally targeted and many more households earned increased incomes than anticipated.
\par
The farmers described benefits beyond those anticipated in the original project design:
\par
improved health, happiness, and enhanced family and community relationships.
\par
The successful diffusion of beekeeping innovation in this Project lends strong support
\par
for major elements in Rogers' theory of diffusion, specifically, attributes of the
\par
innovation, characteristics of the social system, communication channel used, and extent
\par
of the change agent's promotion efforts.
\par
The innovation led to readily observable, personally and collectively felt benefits. Not
\par
only did many farming households see increased incomes, many reported feeling
\par
healthier and happier. They could give some honey to relatives and friends and this
\par
earned them greater respect because the social system values sharing. Where women
\par
beekeepers were involved, generating income led to a redistribution of power and
\par
workload in the household, leading women to feel `more equal' to their husbands.
\par
The technological complexity of the innovation was reduced through the interactive
\par
training approach and distribution of beehives to training participants. This allowed
\par
trainees to experiment and observe results at home. The beekeeping clubs helped mentor
\par
and sustain the farmers' capabilities in beekeeping.
\par
It can be argued that the most pivotal contributing factor is the participatory
\par
pedagogical approach and extraordinary outreach efforts by BRDC staff.
\par
However this study was restricted to the farmers trained by the Project. The
\par
performance of the more than 1000 other farmers that the Project participants claimed to
\par
have trained could not be verified. There is no information on the total number of eitherBeekeeping innovation for sustaining rural livelihoods. A success story 115
\par
farmers or potential adopters in the six communes. Hence the time path of adoption could
\par
not be charted, whether the diffusion curve for beekeeping in Ha Tinh is exponential or
\par
logistic.8
\par
The development of a robust, integrative interdisciplinary diffusion of innovation
\par
framework could benefit from an investigation of the characteristics of the farmers in the
\par
target group -- level of education or degree of cosmopolitanness'' -- to see whether this is
\par
consequential in farmers' performance as postulated by Rogers(2003).
\par
From a development perspective the issue of sustainability is doubtless the most
\par
important. All the farmers interviewed said they will continue with and expand their
\par
beekeeping activities even after the Project ends. Whether beekeeping in the region can
\par
be sustained, much less significantly expanded, is uncertain.
\par
A trend of hydro-meteorological changes has been reported in Ha Tinh in the past 5
\par
decades. The extremes in both maximum and minimum temperatures have increased. The
\par
cold season is shorter and the frequency of low temperatures has decreased. Storms are
\par
more frequent, rain intensity more irregular. The frequency and intensity of floods and
\par
droughts have increased. There have been changes in distribution of and seasonality of
\par
crops, specifically a wider expansion of tropical plants and restriction of subtropical
\par
plants and cold season plants. These trends are expected to continue (ISPONRE, 2009).
\par
What are the implications of these trends in terms of honey bee behaviour,
\par
physiology, distribution range, competitive relationships among the different honey bee
\par
species and races, as well as among their parasites and pathogens (Le Conte and Navajas,
\par
2008)? How will these changes impact the floral resources and their distribution, their
\par
flowering pattern, nectar and pollen production and new pathogens and diseases?
\par
Beekeepers will need to learn what changes to make in their apiculture practices to
\par
adjust to the new environmental conditions. This Project provides ample lessons on how
\par
to design effective interventions to develop local innovative capacity.
