\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Most organizations use forecasting techniques in order to achieve their goals and to reduce risk in uncertain
\par
conditions and varying situations. Forecasting by senior managers plays a very important role in the success
\par
or failure of organization and company projects as well as their affairs. However, the scope of forecasting has
\par
not yet structured or regulated and there is no approved or special conceptual framework for it, especially in
\par
relation to the reasons decisions of mangers deviate while forecasting, which can have a crucial influence on
\par
the whole organization. The aim of this study is to develop a conceptual model of the reasons for deviation
\par
from forecasting by senior managers. To understand the subject, a systematic review and meta-analysis of
\par
the research and literature was carried out. A conceptual model was developed according to the results of this
\par
research and the data from a focus group. The results of meta-analysis show that trends in optimistic and
\par
pessimistic forecasting and senior managers are the main reasons for deviations from forecasting by
\par
management. Moreover, it did not confirm that voluntary forecasts are responsible for increased accuracy in
\par
forecasting by management, and that there are still contradictions in this field. The results of our meta-
\par
analysis also indicated that some studies considered voluntary disclosure of forecasting as a factor in the
\par
reliability of the forecasting. Some researchers consider it as a factor of an optimistic trend of forecasting and
\par
others consider it as a factor effecting deviation from forecasting by management directly due to the individual
\par
motivation of managers and inaccuracy of information on which the decisions are made. According to the
\par
findings, it was not confirmed that voluntary forecasting on its own is a factor in the accuracy of forecasting.
\par
\textbf{Keywords:} Combining Forecasts; Decision Making; Error Measures; Rule-Based Forecasting; Optimistic
\par
and Pessimistic Forecasting.
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Based on the system approach, an organization is an open system which constantly exchanges energy and information
\par
with its surroundings. Duties of a manager, especially senior management in the organization, are consistent with the
\par
conditions of the organization including environmental possibilities and limitations. But the environment of an
\par
organization does not constitute a set of constant factors (Sarrafizadeh, 4991). Managers use different types of
\par
forecasting and estimating for precision in the organizational status. Forecasts are both essential and powerful in the short
\par
term and under constant conditions. Managers are needed certainly and reduce the risk of decision-making and this is
\par
what provides forecasts (Lindgren \& Bandhold, 2007). Although forecasts are one of the important resources of
\par
successful decision making, it should not be assumed that forecasts are valid by them. Forecasts are valid only if used in
\par
parallel with specific methods for separating pure from impure information (Gordon, 3142).
\par
Planning and forecasting is important in most organizations. Senior managers are trained based on accurate data and
\par
scientific methods. Making decisions without enough accurate, relevant and timely information is risky and in many
\par
cases create numerous consequences (Haghparast, 3111). Optimal decisions often involve forecasting the tastes and
\par
trends of the future, and future trends may differ from current trends with regard to factors such as the formation of
\par
habits, daily mood, swings, social impact, maturity, and environmental impact (Loewenstein, O'Donoghue, and Rabin,
\par
Volume 4, Issue 2 available at www.scitecresearch.com/journals/index.php/jrbem/index 289Journal of Research in Business, Economics and Management (JRBEM)
\par
ISSN: 2395-2210
\par
2003). Forecasting is a key element in decision making because the final effectiveness of any decision depends on the
\par
outcome and events occurring after the decision. The ability to predict uncontrollable aspects of these events before
\par
decision making can lead to a better choice. For this reason, management systems have forecasting models for planning
\par
and management of operations (Jamali, 3141). The human need to forecast is evident in the numerous occupations of
\par
tellers, forecasters, futurists, economists, sociologists, journalists, financial consultants, market researchers, planner\quotedblbase s
\par
security and defense, technology teachers and advisers and analysts. Sometimes the work of this industry is called
\par
"anticipates" or "future studies''. But these forecasts also fall into conservative topics such as economic analysis, market
\par
research, technology assessment or competitive insight (Gordon, 3142).
\par
Senior managers of companies and organizations are in a type of force field and determining which tasks to undertake
\par
influenced by interacting forces in this field (Sarrafizadeh, 4991). The success of managers in achieving the objectives of
\par
the organization has always been considered as the first and one of the key issues of management science. Managers are
\par
vital members of the organizations and it can be said that the success of managers is the success of the organizations and
\par
their failure is the failure of organizations. As the success and failure of managers is so important, understanding the
\par
factors affecting success and failure is an absolute necessity (Jafari, 4991). Organizational behavior and performance of a
\par
manager is to some extent affected by his perception, attitude and personality, and every job is limited to some degree by
\par
the employee\quotedblbase s ability. If the employee in a particular case has a wide range of ability or information, the duties and
\par
responsibilities of his/her job will develop in that direction. Conversely, lack of ability and insufficient information in a
\par
particular field causes limitation on the job (Sarrafizadeh, 4991). In addition, the forecast for influences on business
\par
decisions, such as budget, compensation, and financial reporting is important. Incorrect forecasting can reduce the
\par
effectiveness of the planning process and have a negative impact on production efficiency, cost management, and
\par
ultimately, the company's performance (Xiaoling et al., 2015). Beaver says: "Forecasting can be done without making a
\par
decision, but the smallest decisions cannot be made without a forecasting." (Jamali, 3141). Forecasting of the future is
\par
sterile unless it is accompanied by systematic, well-grounded, comprehensive actionable plans (Godet, 1994). However,
\par
the area of forecasting is not yet regulated. There are no accepted conceptual frameworks, accepted methods, professional
\par
standards agreements, or guidelines that can be applied in business policy or decision making (Gordon, 3142). The
\par
purpose of this study is to evaluate the reasons for deviation and bias in forecasting by senior managers of
\par
organizations.
\par
\textbf{Main Question of Study}
\par
Can a senior manager be a deviation factor in management forecasting?
\par
\textbf{Sub-Questions of Study}
\par
Are optimistic or pessimistic biases the main causes of deviation in management forecasting?
\par
Can voluntary disclosure of forecasting be an accuracy factor in forecasting?
\par
\textbf{2. Theoretical Research}
\par
\textbf{2.1. Forecasting and the Reasons for its Importance}
\par
\textbf{2.1.1. Forecasting}
\par
In the dictionary, there is forecasting and prediction which means to anticipate and predict. The term can be used to mean
\par
the same thing or have two different meanings. In the Webster dictionary, forecast has been defined as estimating or
\par
calculating something ago and predict has been defined as knowing something ago (Jamali, 1389). Forecasting attempts
\par
to identify the various impacts of our activities and decisions in future (Boshehri and Malekifar, 3112). When a forecast
\par
is made, it is in the best effort to connect with what happens in the future. In practice, forecasting is used to influence, set
\par
expectations and support the planning process extensively (Bliss, 2007).
\par
A forecast is a guess of future events (Jafari, 3142). Forecasts are associated with risk and uncertainties, but are formed
\par
by a logical-scientific method or philosophical foundations (Taghva and Alizadeh, 2006). For those people who are
\par
seemingly able to predict the rewards are high. Some benefits include supporting the decision-making process and
\par
informed planning and resource allocation decisions, and high-quality data can influence its accuracy (Bliss, 2007).
\par
\textbf{2.1.2. Environmental Uncertainty and Forecasting}
\par
According to Donald Rumsfeld (2002), "\textbf{there are known knowns; there are things we know we know.} \textbf{We also know}
\par
\textbf{there are known unknowns; that is to say we know there are some things we do not know. But there are also}
\par
\textbf{unknown unknowns -- the ones we don't know we don't know}". In other words, the data can be incorrect or
\par
incomplete, the past is not always a guide to the future, qualitative data is often influenced by past experiences and peer
\par
pressure; it is difficult to combine external factors such as government policies and media that will create controversy"(
\par
Bliss, 2007). Many aspects of everyday life that people need forecasted are about the results for which uncertainty is
\par
known and are not random (Kahneman and Tversky, 1982).
\par
Volume 4, Issue 2 available at www.scitecresearch.com/journals/index.php/jrbem/index 290Journal of Research in Business, Economics and Management (JRBEM)
\par
ISSN: 2395-2210
\par
\textbf{2.1.3. Forecasting Methods}
\par
Forecasting uses both qualitative and quantitative methods (David, 3142).
\par
\textbf{2.1.3.1. Qualitative Methods}
\par
Qualitative methods include subjective appraisal by expert comments. In qualitative methods, the forecast is based on a
\par
subjective judgment and the predictor predicts future data using their experience and taking into account the current
\par
situation and predictable changes (Jamali, 3141). Qualitative forecasting methods are based on the intuition and common
\par
sense of decision-makers, knowledge and experience of experts and managers, the experience of practitioners, and
\par
research about the market (Khodayari and Rahimi, 3112). A cautious forecast probably involves more of a point of view
\par
that is followed by discussion to achieve a consensus. Under conditions that includes choice, experts can be replaced by a
\par
consumer or focused group. In such cases, the organization should match views and data to formulate their vision of the
\par
future (Bliss, 2007). In the forecasting process, the absence or lack of access to historical information or inadequate
\par
volume necessitates the use of judgment methods (Jamali, 3141).
\par
\textbf{2.1.3.2. Quantitative Methods}
\par
Most of quantitative methods are based on fitting mathematical models than on historical data. By default, this
\par
forecasting is a perception kind of continuity and its stability lies in the past data and finding the law of this integration
\par
can create a model that is extendable into the future (Khodayari and Rahimi, 3112). Quantitative methods are mainly
\par
based on data. Often statistical data are used to identify relationships and extrapolate trends. The data can be affected in
\par
different ways by experts who are ready to forecast (Bliss, 2007). In a quantitative method, the operation is done on the
\par
basis of mathematical calculations. In this way, by examining historical data and reviewing past trends, it is assumed that
\par
the process is sustainable and extendable into the future. Forecasts done using time-series models are one of the
\par
quantitative methods. In statistic methods, forecasting is done based on regular analysis of historical data. This gives
\par
objectivity to the system and leads to effective management of data content. Statistical forecasts act as input in the
\par
subjective evaluation of an aware decision-maker who moderates forecasting by using other relevant information and
\par
their expectations of the future (Jamali, 3141).
\par
\textbf{2.1.4. The Importance of Forecasting}
\par
Forecasting is an attempt to avoid many problems that can occur in the future (O'Brien and Robertson, 2009). If an event
\par
is previously predicted and what might happen is understood, it can be prepared for (Reynolds, 2013). Forecasting is a
\par
key factor in economic decisions. Investors, creditors, managers and other entities make economic decisions based on
\par
forecasting and expectations (Motamedi, 3143). Forecasting (predicts, estimates or calculates something in the future) is
\par
an integral component in the cycle of management control (Bliss, 2007). Forecasting is a link in the chain between the
\par
organization and its environment. Even if forecasting is not always in the form of a direct and formal activity, it has a
\par
very important rolein decision-making and planning. Due to this, forecasting is very
\par
useful for senior management and all functions within the organization, especially marketing, finance and
\par
production. Exact forecasting is an art (Jafarnejad and Faryabi Basmenj, 3112). Forecasting the environment of an
\par
organization begins by identifying vital factors in the external environment. The impact of these factors on the
\par
organization is shown in terms of opportunities or threats determined by the strategist (Ahmadi and Esfahanian, 1999).
\par
The most important foundation for decision-making is forecasting and forward-looking at events and activities of the
\par
organizations. Organizations for creation long-term life and drawing horizon plans, activities and resources is needed that
\par
forecasting is done in terms of policies and long-term strategies (Hamidizadeh, 4991) Forecasting is important from this
\par
aspect because it is a fundamental and key element in the decisions of internal and external users of the organization.
\par
Accordingly, the efficiency and final effectiveness of each decision depends on the results of the events that occur
\par
following any decision. Thus, effective and efficient decisions are made by forecasts based on the correct foundation
\par
(Haghighat, 3144). In fact, forecasts are management\quotedblbase s expectations of the company's future performance, so the
\par
accuracy of these forecasts is a key factor for investors, managers and creditors (Abzari et al., 2012). Financially and
\par
business outlook, forecasting will allow to data recipients to verify that the programs and perspectives can be
\par
accomplished or are available, if proven otherwise and is as a strong forecasting of satisfaction individuals and
\par
organizations, is needed to corrective measures (Bliss, 2007).
\par
Forecasting is a technical method to estimate the future of a business or function. Forecasting is important for short-
\par
term and long-term decisions. In various areas of business, such as technology, economics demand and etc. may be used
\par
for forecasting (Gahirwal and Vijayalakshmi, 2013). Forecasting helps investors to improve their decision-making
\par
process and to reduce the risks of their decisions. They are interested in estimated future benefits of their investment so
\par
that they can judge the receipt of the next dividend and the share value. Investors judge a company by forecasted profit
\par
rather than the current situation because the forecasts determine the allocation of funds to departments and business units
\par
and this is important for potential investors. They proceed to invest and allocate their capital resources by forecasting
\par
future cash flows and profits (Motamedi, 3143). However, organizations skill accept what is emitted, any organization
\par
that is not able to accurately forecast domestic consumption will soon be faced with problems (Bliss, 2007).
\par
