ISSN: 2395-2210
\par
\textbf{2.1.5. Important Reasons of Forecasting for Investors and Managers Forecasts}
\par
Forecasting is important in economic decisions. Investors, creditors, managers and other institutional shareholders
\par
potentially profit from economic decisions based on their expectations. Investors investing in companies and buying
\par
shares require information, such as forecasted earnings per share, and use this information for share pricing, especially in
\par
new companies with little history. Based on these forecasts, investors may sell their shares or keep them (Abzari et al.,
\par
2012). The literature confirms that management forecasts are important and have useful information for investors (Amini
\par
Motlagh, 3141). Since the investors require information about future prospects of the company to make decisions,
\par
companies are required to provide forecasted earnings per share along with other information in order to provide
\par
expectations of the company\quotedblbase s future. If management does not provide adequate information to investors forced to pay
\par
for it or get help from financial analysts (Jamali, 3141). Also, according to Chang et al. (2008) information related to
\par
manager\quotedblbase s forecasts plays an important role in reducing information asymmetry between managers and investors. Lanks
\par
and Part (2006) argue that reducing information asymmetry between managers and investors reduces the opportunity for
\par
some investors to use private information to achieve higher profits, thus reducing information asymmetry reduces
\par
investment cost. Diamond and Verchia (1991) also believe that reducing information asymmetry reduces the cost of
\par
capital and increases liquidity of the stocks (Jafari, 3142).
\par
Investors rely on information, such as forecasted earnings per share, and use it in pricing their stock. Correct forecasting
\par
of profit can help investors and creditors to achieve objectives such as: 1. Helping to assess the power of profitability of
\par
business units; 2. Forecasting future profits; 3. Estimating the risk of investing in the business unit; and 4. Estimating the
\par
risk of lending to the Business Unit (Motamedi, 3143). Houghton and Astaken (2007) studied the impact of fame on the
\par
reaction of investors to the forecasts of the management of profit. They found that the reputation of management in
\par
forecasting was a key factor of forecasting validity among the effective factors on the validity of forecasts of profit
\par
(Amini Motlagh, 3141).
