\textbf{2.4. Perspective View of Opportunistic Behavior and Information Management}
\par
The opportunistic behavior view of managers, as opposed to the information view, has a cynical approach to managers
\par
and believes that managers abuse the opportunities and flexibility that accounting and financial reporting offers them.
\par
According to the authorities, they try to hide the facts from the shareholders and investors. According to this view, the
\par
purpose of corporate managers is to maximize their prosperity. On the other hand, according to the information
\par
perspective, managers make good use of voluntary opportunities which they get in accounting and financial reporting and
\par
try to use this opportunity to convey private information that sometimes managers only have access to it (Jafari, 3142).
\par
\textbf{2.5. The Reasons of Managers for Unrealistic Forecasts}
\par
Managers with incentives such as the prevention of eviction have a successful career or achieve rewards in cases where
\par
compensation is depended on performance; proceeds to increase management of profit (Jafari, 3142).
\par
\textbf{2.5.1. Fear of Loss of Reputation and Litigation}
\par
Managers who forecast profit higher than the reality have adopted the accounting procedures of increasing profit and
\par
when forecast profit is less than the reality have adopted the accounting procedures of reducing profits. In either case,
\par
managers will be reaching to the forecasts. The reason is that they fear losing their reputations or want to be safe from
\par
financial effects of the investors proceeding (Amini Motlagh, 3141). Skinner (1994) concluded that managers may
\par
forecast the bad news to investors and in this way protect themselves against risk of litigation and loss of reputation
\par
(Jafari, 3142). Of course, managers are concerned with their forecasts and want to fulfill them, because, not reaching a
\par
forecast or forecast error (especially if it is negative) will be charged to the managers. Empirical evidence suggests that
\par
the costs of forecast error are often in the form of litigation and lead to lose the reputation of accuracy of management
\par
(Amini Motlagh, 3141).
\par
\textbf{2.5.2. Fear of Job Loss}
\par
Senior managers and financial managers who suddenly emit bad financial forecasts may lose their jobs. Naturally, they
\par
might delay bad news as long as possible and neglect the worsening situation in the hope for a miracle to maintain their
\par
job for a few more months (Bliss, 2007). Trying to keep a job and to upgrade it, setting a favorable target for future
\par
performance, and efforts and pressure from the corporate governance structure of the axis bank are among the reasons
\par
and motives leading managers to avoid loss of forecasting (Jafari, 3142). Who will forecast a budget deficit, if they are
\par
at risk of dismissal, or risk elimination and a bankrupt organization is forecasted surplus economic goods? (Bliss,
\par
2007).
