\textbf{2.6. Forecasting Accuracy and Issues Related to the Accuracy of Management Forecast}
\par
The value of forecasting is dependent on its accuracy and credibility (Brown, Clarke, How, and Lim, 2000). Good
\par
forecasting is needed to be honest on the part of all those interested (Bliss, 2007). Profitability of forecasting is related to
\par
its difference from reality. If forecast accuracy is higher, its deviation will be lower (Abzari et al., 2012). If the prediction
\par
is made with accuracy, some of the losses caused by uncertainty in the decision making process will be reduced or
\par
removed (Jafari, 3142). Accurate forecast of product demand is important for the companies because it has application in
\par
decisions about inventory control, production planning, purchasing, logistics, planning of cash flow and other aspects of
\par
business (Petropoulos, Fildes, and Goodwin, 2014). Despite the risks, forecasting accuracy is essential (Bliss, 2007).
\par
The researchers suggest that forecasting manner should include accuracy of the confidence of managers about the future,
\par
and more accurate forecasts (e.g. point) are generally thought to reflect the management's confidence in forecasting with
\par
less accuracy (i.e. widespread) (Jafari, 3142). Forecasts accuracy is increased by implementing a voluntary policy in
\par
mature companies that have multiple stakeholders and high demand (Gounopoulos, Kraft, and Skinner, 2013). Forecast
\par
accuracy of stock dividend increases with the ratio of retaining ownership in public offerings of the company stock and
\par
forecast ability of its future earnings. In addition, it seems to create a company\quotedblbase s law is started the period that
\par
management forecasts be more accurate (Brown, Clarke, How, and Lim, 2000). Also, strong corporate governance and
\par
the presence of analysts in the capital market are among the factors that can have a direct relationship with the accuracy
\par
of forecast. Research also shows that the accuracy of seasonal or cyclical forecasting by managers is better than annual
\par
forecasting (Jafari, 2013). Economic conditions will affect the manner and accuracy of the managers forecast (Jamali,
\par
3141). Also, if the organizations only intend that the forecast shows the breakeven point (without profit and loss) this
\par
removes any incentive for an accurate forecast. A Breakeven Forecast by the end of the year is easier; however, it is not a
\par
good practice (Bliss, 2007). Some other factors that are inversely related to the accuracy of profit forecast include
\par
fluctuation of efficiency, cost of ownership, exposure to legal liability, duration, and time horizon of forecasting (Jafari,
\par
3142).
\par
\textbf{2.7. Prediction Error and the Factors Affecting the Forecast}
\par
There is uncertainty in the predictions. This fact is due to an unusual component in the time series. The presence of this
\par
element indicates the presence of undefined or unpredictable fluctuations in the data leading to forecast error. If this
\par
unusual component creates a significant error and has considerable effect on the ability to accurately forecast, the result
\par
becomes insignificant, but if it is limited to a higher accuracy, it can be achieved in forecasting by determining annual
\par
and seasonal patterns (Jamali, 3141).
\par
Forecasts are usually associated with errors, but enlargement of forecast errors depends on various factors (Jafari, 3142).
\par
Many factors are involved in the deviation of actual earnings from forecasted earnings. These factors reduce the accuracy
\par
of forecasts and increase the forecast error. Forecast error can be reduced by identifying these factors and considering
\par
them in the calculation of forecast earnings (Abzari et al., 2012).
\par
According to the Haygins\quotedblbase s studies (2008), forecast error is higher in companies with declining sales. He also analyzed
\par
seasonal errors during 1992-1999 in American companies. In this study, he found an inverse relationship between
\par
company size and forecast error (Motamedi, 3143). A sample of 82 initial public offerings of securities (IPO) on the
\par
Euronext Paris Market (2000-2002) has shown that only two variables are dependent on the disclosure of very accurate
\par
predictions: forecast horizon and company age (Cazavan- Jeny \& Jeanjean, 2006). Motamedi (3143) in research
\par
addressing "Analysis Impact of Effective Factors on the Deviation from Forecasting of Listed Companies Dividends in
\par
the Tehran Stock Exchange during 3112-3112" evaluated five hypotheses: the impact of the volume of transactions,
\par
Shareholders, the life of the company, size and time horizon of the forecast on the deviation of the profit forecast in the
