\textbf{2.7.1. Factors Affecting Management Forecast Error}
\par
In economics with the assumption of rational behavior of individuals, it is assumed that all persons are looking to
\par
maximize their wealth and personal motives, so managers are not the exception. However, managers with their freedom
\par
in accounting practices must know how these factors have impact on their wealth so that they can use them in their own
\par
interests to increase their wealth as much as possible. But increasing managers' wealth can be associated with reducing
\par
the wealth of other groups, including shareholders (Jafari, 3142). Managers as decision-makers and suppliers of finance
\par
information likely have confidential information about their company business outlook and production processes of
\par
accruals. Therefore forecast of management revenues can lack reliable errors on the basis of historically reported
\par
accruals. However, in a changing operating environment, a manager\quotedblbase s knowledge of his/her company business prospect
\par
is incomplete which can lead to unintentional errors in assessing the future of the company's performance. When
\par
managers have the flexibility to transfer their incomplete assessment of the business through both accrual and forecasts of
\par
their earnings, both the information disclosures are likely to include common errors (Gong, Li, and Xie, 2009). The
\par
separation of ownership from management in companies, has created the potential for managers to transfer part of the
\par
wealth of the group within organization to themselves because: firstly, they have access to information that other people
\par
don\quotedblbase t; and secondly, due to the preparation and submission of information, such as finance information by managers,
\par
there is a possibility for them to modify this information in their interest. Of course, managers who are less experienced,
\par
and have less flexibility in accounting and those who are exposed to external shocks, are less likely to emit a correct
\par
forecast (Jafari, 3142).
