\textbf{2.8. Optimistic Management Forecast}
\par
When people who speculate about the possible outcomes have an optimistic forecast, this is an important conclusion,
\par
because many aspects of a company\quotedblbase s daily life are tied to the forecast of results which are fully or partially random
\par
(Windschitl, Smith, Rose, and Krizan, 2010). A manager\quotedblbase s forecast of profits is usually different from the real profits due
\par
to the nature of forecasts and the uncertainty of future events. If a manager's estimated forecasted profits are more than
\par
real profits, the forecast was optimistic, and if the profits are less than real profits, the forecast was pessimistic. Some
\par
researches show that profit forecasts are all optimistic (Jafari, 3142). However, the optimistic and the pessimistic
\par
forecasts are done for many reasons, and these biases in the forecast cause the manager\quotedblbase s forecast to deviate from the
\par
actual results.
\par
\textbf{2.8.1. Reasons and Motivations of Managers for Optimistic and Pessimistic Management Forecast}
\par
One of the reasons for optimistic trends in forecasts is that in order to avoid self-blame managers may reduce problems
\par
experienced in similar cases (Pezzo, Litman, and\& Pezzo, 2006). Another reason is a false opinion (false thinking) they
\par
put above all other ideas, especially when this results in the desired forecast (Buehler, Griffin, and MacDonald, 1997).
\par
Managers have various incentives for optimistic forecasts to obtain the desired value of the market. These reasons may
\par
include job security, compensation and reward incentives based on performance or the need for financing, etc. (Jafari,
\par
3142). People may present optimistic forecasts in order to appear more productive and more successful. A study by
\par
Pezzo, Pezzo and Stone (2006) found that participants who presented unknown forecasts had not shown an optimistic
\par
trend (Pezzo, Litman, and Pezzo, 2006). Quito et al. (2009) and Jafari (3142) both stated that more manager confidence
\par
and behavioral prejudices were considered as possible motivations for an optimistic forecast, and concluded that the
\par
opportunism of management is considered the most likely reason for an optimistic forecast by management. Kato and
\par
Skinner (2009) said that in the Japan, it is not important that litigation is related to the managers forecast error. Many
\par
initial forecasts are too optimistic especially for companies that are not profitable, and many managers forecast too
\par
optimistically every year (Amini Motlagh, 3141).
\par
Managers intentionally emit pessimistic forecast which in turn causes market participants to revise their expectations
\par
downward and subsequently cause an easier standard to reach or get closer to the real benefit. Chen (2004) in a studied
\par
sample observed that when the forecasts of management were pessimistic, the real benefit exceeded above the forecasted
\par
profit. Often a pessimistic forecast is used by managers to justify their profit forecasts as a means to reduce market
\par
expectations of company profit (Jafari, 2013).
\par
\textbf{2.9. Forecast Prejudices (Trends) - No Prejudice is More Important than Accuracy}
\par
Predictors are added to time series forecasts in most unstructured emotional reforms. This can be a source of bias and
\par
prejudice. For example, managers can change the sale forecast by pride style to motivate employees. Retailers may
\par
change the forecast so that it can be more easily surpassed. So it is not surprising that some early studies have shown
\par
unstructured reforms often damage forecasts (Armstrong, 2006). A problem more important than the forecast accuracy is
\par
whether the bias in the forecast is a major factor of avoidance. This is a big concern during judgment. Hoggart and
\par
Makridakis (1981) carried out an extensive survey about the prejudices that developed in human judgment; the
\par
investigation revealed how these prejudices could lead to serious errors in forecasting and planning (Erickson, 1987).
\par
How much does a desired result affect possible judgments or establish optimism? The number of published studies on
\par
this question is not convincing (Krizan and Windschitl, 2007). Desirable attitudes (prejudice) are generally too weak or
\par
do not exist in cases where there is uncertainty and forecasting is not a coincidence, unless a person is unsure that he only
\par
guesses about results, or allows only his views and understanding of evidence to guide his forecasts (Windschitl, Smith,
\par
Rose, and Krizan, 2010). Tests that directly checked prejudices have had mixed results (Vosgerau, 2009). This can also
\par
be seen in the research of Barhillel and Bodesko (1995) and in their chapter titled "Wishful Thinking in Forecasting of
\par
the World Cup Results: the Deceptive" (Bar-Hillel, Budescu, and Amar, 2008). Windsaytel, Smith, Rose, and Krizan,
\par
(2010), in the study "The Favorable Trend in the Forecasting: Optimism without Being Realistic", shows that the impact
\par
of favorable results must be comprehensible in the following two ways: First, people may express optimistic or
\par
pessimistic tendencies in no large scale, and how they assess the probability of a desired outcome and the estimation of
\par
these probabilities must be shown to have been done in a common practice. Second, people may have an optimistic
\par
image about the potential consequences, but this optimism becomes apparent only when using some certain measures
\par
(for example, a forecast of the outcome, the special possible measures are used to encourage and facilitate the expression
\par
of opinion).
\par
People normally presume an optimistic attitude because it seems that optimism is essential for achieving targets or that an
\par
optimistic attitude is more consistent with maintaining a positive situation and quite well understood by others
\par
(Segerstrom, Taylor, Kemeny, and Fahey, 1998). In fact, a widespread human tendency is to rely on being optimistic and
\par
maintaining positive thoughts and balanced and stable conditions (Lench and Ditto, 2008). When the judgment is
\par
dependent on elements that can increase pessimism or decrease optimism, most have the tendency to inhibit the bad news
\par
(Windschitl et al., 2010). Although attitudes (prejudice) of optimistic forecast are common, these errors are not created
