\par
\textbf{2.10.Managers Biased Forecasts and Planning Error}
\par
In the first theoretical analysis of planning error, Kahenman and Turesky (1979) adopted a predominantly cognitive
\par
approach of an individual\quotedblbase s tendency to judgment error rather than informed statistical judgments. Kahenman and
\par
Toresky argued that people presented biased forecasts because they focused on specific information, (For example, the
\par
specific features of a given task) regardless of the distributed information (e.g. description of the individual work and the
\par
completion of a typical task for the general population (Pezzo, Pezzo, and Stone, 2006). Also, the manager\quotedblbase s upward
\par
forecast of dividend is biased as a forecast of company earnings. However, the dividend forecasts are substantially more
\par
accurate and less biased than their colleagues incomes (Brown, Clarke, How, and Lim, 2000).
\par
Some studies suggest that participants are encouraged to focus on the specific programs with the aim of completing a
\par
task or increasing your optimistic attitude, (Buehler R., 2003). Studies have found that participants who have done
\par
forecasts as compared to other people (and people who may have no incentive to past failures) have shown less planning
\par
biases (Buehler, et al., 1994; Pezzo, Pezzo, and Stone, 2006). It has been shown that as the systems which are trying to
\par
forecast their behavior become more complex, planning is becoming irrelevant (Lindgern and Bandhold, 3112).
\par
\textbf{2.11.Voluntary and Mandatory Disclosure Management Forecasts}
\par
Mandatory and voluntary disclosures are two important channels through which managers send information to
\par
shareholders. While a significant amount of literature shows that both types of disclosure contain valuable information
\par
and significantly affect the price of securities (government bonds and stocks) (Kothari, 2001). Also, previous research
\par
has shown that voluntary disclosure by managers helps users to process information more effectively (Brown and Zhou,
\par
2015). The optional amount of disclosure to reduce the false allocation of resources in the capital market will depend on
\par
the accuracy of the published information about the economic situation of the business unit. Since the optional disclosure
\par
is dependent on the manager's decision, the accuracy of the information contained therein is not clear (Jafari, 2013). The
\par
literature of voluntary disclosure of management forecasts of profit is based on the tendency of knowing the motivation
\par
of managers to disclose or their tendency to disclose personal information when it is not necessary. This is a fundamental
\par
concept for accounting and security provisions. In this respect, what set of disclosure requirements should be developed
\par
and implemented? As long as managers have a personal motivation for optional disclosure of personal information, it
\par
seems that disclosure provisions are not necessary (Amini Motlagh, 3141).
\par
Managers can try to achieve the correct forecast or forecast with their desired results. Credit of optional disclosure means
\par
the accuracy of the published information by the management; on the other hand, the release of the voluntary disclosure
\par
has an effect on the stock price of the company (Jafari, 2013). Ganopolo's and colleagues (2013), findings in a study on
\par
the "Voluntary Forecasts of Management Profit in IPOs, against Mandatory Forecasts ", indicates that a pessimistic
\par
revenue forecast at a time when companies are required to publicly offer next year profit changes to an optimistic
\par
forecast when shifted to a voluntary period. Comparing two time periods in the study showed that forecasting of
\par
compulsory income could force companies to anticipate what they had neither the motivation nor the ability to do
\par
(Gounopoulos, Kraft, and Skinner, 2013).
\par
Roland et al. (1990) showed that revenue forecast publication could reduce the adverse selection and moral hazard, so it
\par
was easier to attract new capital. They showed companies that have increased their capital by holding down the shares of
\par
former shareholders, by publishing more forecasts. The motivation to do such a work in France where there is less risk of
\par
litigation should be higher (Djankov, La Porta, F, and Shleifer, 2003). Lou and Penman (1990) showed that generally
\par
companies with good news voluntarily disclosed their forecasts to distinguish themselves from the companies with bad
\par
news (Cazavan-Jeny and Jeanjean, 2006). The refuting hypothesis argues that when managers are owner of all the
\par
confidential and unclassified information, even if declaration of all information is not possible for them, the lack of
\par
disclosure or a slight disclosure is more favorable to them (Jamali, 3141). Hiley and Palio (2001) believe that there are
\par
two potential mechanisms to enhance the credibility of voluntary disclosure: (Jafari, 2013):
\par
i) The third parties that play the role of middleman can provide the essential confidence about the quality of
\par
management disclosure.
\par
ii) Confirmation at the time of publication of the financial statement of period end, for example credit of managers
\par
forecast of revenues and net income, can be approved or rejected by publication of the real financial statements
\par
at the period end. These mechanisms, while useful in increasing the credit degree of the manager\quotedblbase s forecast, are
\par
adequate punishment for those managers who knowingly published false forecast.
\par
In Hirest's et al. (1999), Houghton et al. (2003), and Neg et al. (2006), there was a significant relationship between the
\par
reaction of investors and successive changes on management forecasts. These studies have shown that when executives
