\textbf{3. History Research}
\par
\textbf{3.1. International Research}
\par
Chu et al. (2011) examined the optimistic forecast and avoiding behavior from the losses forecast of the management in
\par
Japanese companies. . Their final sample was 1771 companies selected among the 2,079 Japanese companies between
\par
the years of 1988-2005. They showed that companies avoiding behavior from the loss forecast were made up of a small
\par
fraction of the companies (about 25.5\% of companies) but they explained a large percentage (61.6\%) of optimistic bias in
\par
management. They also said that the market recognized this opportunistic behavior and discounted the information of the
\par
profit forecast (Jafari, 2013).
\par
Gong et al. (2009) examined the relationship between the management forecast error of revenues in the year after and
\par
accrual in the current year. They assumed that management earnings forecast showed more optimism (pessimism) when
\par
accruals were relatively high (low). Consistent with this hypothesis, they found that there was a positive relationship
\par
between forecast error of management earning and accrual. This positive relationship for companies operating in a
\par
business environment is more uncertain, and companies in industries that offer a larger synchronize changes between
\par
accruals and activities related to growth is stronger. In addition, when this positive relation is significant, that most likely
\par
reflected the true beliefs of management about perspectives of the company's business, but it disappears when accruals
\par
are manipulated to increase the profits of the managers business (Gong, Li, and Xie, 2009).
\par
Irani (2003) examined the relationship between the bias in the published profit forecast by executives at companies that
\par
had a financial crisis and the results of internal trade. This study was conducted during the period 1990-1995 at US
\par
companies. In his research in 2001, he found optimism in such forecasts and he tried to evaluate whether the observed
\par
optimism is related to the opportunistic behavior of management or their true belief that the company is better. Unusual
\par
Internal Transaction in Management forecasts in the past period showed that their optimistic forecast was associated with
\par
opportunistic behavior (Jafari, 2013).
\par
\textbf{3.2. Internal Research}
\par
Jafari (1392) examined the relationship between optimistic management forecast and avoiding behavior from the loss
\par
forecast. The results showed that a lot of optimistic bias were explained by avoiding behavior from the loss forecast and
\par
the market showed a positive reaction to the benefit forecast of companies that had avoiding behavior from the loss
\par
forecast. Therefore, this behavior is not recognized and new data does not discount the profit forecast.
\par
Mashayekh and Shahrokhi (3112) addressed assessing the accuracy and effective factors of profit forecast by the
\par
managers. They compared the forecast accuracy of managers from the earnings per future share with forecast based on
\par
information from the Random Walk Model. To this end, the forecasting of 279 companies during 1381-1384, including
\par
639 observations were evaluated by the Mean Differences Method. Findings showed that there was a significant
\par
difference between the inaccuracy of the managers forecast and the inaccuracy of forecast based on Random Walk. In
\par
addition, comparing the mean differences of the two models showed that the accuracy of the manager\quotedblbase s forecast was
\par
higher than the forecast based on the Random Walk. Also, results of other assumptions showed that the manager\quotedblbase s
\par
forecasts had optimistic deviation (Mashayekh and Shahrokhi, 3112).
\par
Mehdi and Hossein Abadi (3144) examined the relationship between forecasted profit error and accruals of listed
\par
companies in Tehran Stock Exchange. The main objective of their study was to investigate the relationship between
\par
forecasted profit error by the management and the total accruals. To this end, they collected the information of 112
\par
companies that had the presented characteristics during the period 1387-1382. The results showed that there was a
\par
significant relationship between the forecasted benefit error by management and the total accrual. Also, the relationship
\par
between the forecasted benefit error by management and the total accrual was not accepted in a commercial environment
\par
with high uncertainty (Mahdavi and Zare Hossein Abadi, 2011).
\par
\textbf{4. Research Method}
\par
Research done by the qualitative method is descriptive, and the data collection method is documents.
\par
This study is a systematic review based on local data bases, including Civilica, Ganj and Sid, valid international
\par
databases including Scopus and Science Direct, and valid international journals. The search was done using the
\par
keywords forecast, prediction and foresight. The field management investigation was done with a top Manager.
\par
Then, all the studies and reviewed thesis were adapted to the research questions, evaluated, and data extracted and
\par
classified based on the purpose of research meta-analysis.
