\textbf{5.4. Third sub-question: Can voluntary disclosure of forecasting be an accuracy factor in forecasting?}
\par
According to meta-analysis in this study, there is a paradox in optional disclosure of manager\quotedblbase s forecasts. Some
\par
researchers validated the optional disclosure to mean accuracy of the published information by management, while others
\par
questioned the accuracy of the information due to the dependence of the voluntary disclosure of forecast to the manager\quotedblbase s
\par
decision, and they knew that it depended on the motivation of the managers to disclose it. Results of another study
\par
showed that the forecasts were pessimistic at the time of mandatory disclosure, but the forecasts shifted to optimistic
\par
when companies voluntarily disclosed forecasts. These results are inconsistent with each other and one implicitly gives
\par
credibility to the voluntary disclosure, while others know that information accuracy depends on the motivation of the
\par
manager and considers it as a factor for deviation from forecast concluding that a shift from pessimistic to optimistic
\par
forecasts occurs at the time of voluntary disclosure. A meta-analysis results of previous studies show that voluntary
\par
forecast directly caused the deviation of forecast if the manager published it with personal motivation or the forecast data
\par
is not correct. If the manager of the disclosure doesn\quotedblbase t have personal motivation and the information is correct, the
\par
optimistic forecast is good news. Therefore, answer of this question is no, and the optional disclosure cannot be an
\par
absolute cause of the accuracy of forecast.
