\textbf{6. Findings and Conception Model of Research}
\par
This study is the first systematic comprehensive review in the field of the management forecast of the organization,
\par
especially inspection of personality traits of senior managers and their thoughts and motivations. This research used an
\par
innovative meta-analysis method. The study has also provided a comprehensive set of effective factors for the
\par
organization forecasts in addition to offering a model to identify the effective factors on the deviation of senior
\par
manager\quotedblbase s forecasts according to the results of previous research and literature (note Table 6). In addition, this study has
\par
tried to look at the theories of management that used senior managers roles in the world of management science to
\par
forecast future events in today\quotedblbase s uncertain condition; the providence of managers of organization and their possessions
\par
that have effective factors on the combination of senior managers' duties (see Figure 1 Note). According to the meta-
\par
analysis of past research, the effective factors of the task of senior executives forecast are introduced where the
\par
environment of variable business market is critical for today's organizations.
\par
From the application aspect, this research can provide a wide range of information to the senior managers of
\par
organizations, stakeholder organizations and researchers in the field of management and forecasting. Also, in terms of
\par
compliance, the presented results are completely consistent with past research results and are entirely based on meta-
\par
analysis of past research.
\par
Outputs of the study offer a table of the various factors influencing the forecasts of the organization (Table 6). This
\par
provides a conceptual model of the factors affecting the deviation from forecasts of the senior executives (Figure 4), as
\par
well as factors affecting the task of forecasting the senior executive (Figure 5) and have been presented based on a
\par
systematic review and meta-analysis of previous research and management theories.
