@article{zandRolebasedTypologyInformation2015,
  title = {A Role-Based Typology of Information Technology: Model Development and Assessment},
  shorttitle = {A Role-Based Typology of Information Technology},
  author = {Zand, Fardad and Solaimani, Sam and {van Beers}, Cees},
  year = 2015,
  journal = {Information Systems Management},
  volume = {32},
  number = {2},
  pages = {119--135},
  publisher = {Taylor \& Francis},
  doi = {10.1080/10580530.2015.1018770},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\emph{Information Systems Management}, 32:119--135, 2015
\par
Copyright \copyright{} Taylor \& Francis Group, LLC
\par
ISSN: 1058-0530 print / 1934-8703 online
\par
DOI: 10.1080/10580530.2015.1018770
\par
\textbf{A Role-based Typology of Information Technology:}
\par
\textbf{Model Development and Assessment}
\par
\textbf{Fardad Zand1, Sam Solaimani2, and Cees van Beers1}
\par
\emph{1Faculty of Technology Policy and Management, Delft University of Technology, Delft, The Netherlands}
\par
\emph{2Center for Marketing \& SCM, Nyenrode Business Universiteit, Breukelen, The Netherlands}
\par
\textbf{Managers aim to explain how and why IT creates business value,}
\par
\textbf{recognize their IT-based capabilities, and select the appropriate}
\par
\textbf{IT to enhance and leverage those capabilities. This article syn-}
\par
\textbf{thesizes the Organizational Information Processing Theory and}
\par
\textbf{Resource-Based View into a descriptive typology of IT roles.}
\par
\textbf{On the basis of these roles, the core features and functions of}
\par
\textbf{IT resources can be analyzed and linked to business objectives.}
\par
\textbf{Implications and areas for future research are discussed.}
\par
\textbf{Keywords }classification typology; IT business value; IT roles;
\par
qualitative interview; resource based view
\par
\textbf{INTRODUCTION}
\par
Information Technology Business Value (ITBV) lies at
\par
the core of the IS discipline (Agarwal \& Lucas, 2005).
\par
Demonstrating the value of investing in IT has become fun-
\par
damental to the field since studies were published that docu-
\par
mented diminishing importance of IT managers (Martin, 2007)
\par
or devastating failures in IT projects leading to organizational
\par
bankruptcy (Keil, 1995). Most of the studies so far take a holis-
\par
tic approach and treat the process leading to value creation from
\par
IT investments as a ``black box'' (e.g., Sircar \& Choi, 2009;
\par
Sircar, Turnbow, \& Bordoloi, 2000). Even when implementa-
\par
tion of IT does not fail, its contribution to competitive advantage
\par
of the firm has been questioned (Carr, 2003). Kohli and Grover
\par
(2008) recently challenged the current trajectory of IS research
\par
and suggested a discontinuity in thinking how IT value cre-
\par
ation should be studied in order to address existing doubts and
\par
concerns.
\par
The present article attempts to develop and test a typology
\par
of IT roles in organizations. IT roles relate to basic mecha-
\par
nisms and functionalities through which IS enable or facilitate
\par
a firm to buildup strategic and operational capabilities that can
\par
be transformed into business value. Kohli and Grover (2008)
\par
emphasized that it is not enough to show whether or not IT
\par
Address correspondence to Sam Solaimani, Nyenrode Business
\par
University, Straatweg 25, 3620 AC Breukelen, P.O. Box 130, The
\par
Netherlands. E-mail: s.solaimani@nyenrode.nl
\par
creates value but that it is more important to demonstrate \emph{how,}
\par
\emph{when}, and \emph{why }it does. In other words, in order to come to a right
\par
investment decision, a sharp understanding of how different ITs
\par
function and how they affect organizational performance is an
\par
important requisite. By identifying and analyzing the organiza-
\par
tional roles of IT, we aim to address the ``how'' and, to some
\par
extent, the ``why'' question. The central question of the present
\par
research is: ``What are the primary roles of IS, and how do they
\par
lead to value creation in organizations?''
\par
The review of the literature presented in this research points
\par
out that the existing typologies of IT are not comprehensive,
\par
are not context-specific, and/or are lacking solid grounds in the
\par
(management) theory of the firm. Moreover, they either take an
\par
operational or a strategic perspective towards analyzing and/or
\par
classifying the IT-based capabilities of the firm. Based on man-
\par
agement theories of the firm, this article proposes a conceptual
\par
typology that synthesizes the operational and strategic levels of
\par
analysis into a single framework. Two key attributes underlie
\par
the proposed typology---the \emph{Information Processing Capacity}
\par
based on the \emph{Organizational Information Processing Theory}
\par
(Galbraith, 1974) and the \emph{Organizational Change Intensity}
\par
based on the \emph{Resource Based View of the Firm }(Barney, 1991).
\par
In line with these two dimensions, the study identifies and
\par
defines seven types of IT roles, categorized in four general
\par
classes: first-order roles (\emph{Information }and \emph{Communication}),
\par
second-order roles (\emph{Automation }and \emph{Coordination}), third-order
\par
roles (\emph{Integration }and \emph{Transformation}), and fourth-order roles
\par
(\emph{Innovation)}.
\par
A qualitative multi-method research design (Myers, 2009;
\par
Silverman, 2000) was followed for constructing the typology.
\par
First, the attribute space of the typology is conceptualized.
\par
Then, the relevance of such typology is evaluated through
\par
semi-structured interviews with experts. In the second stage, a
\par
classification system is developed, and different generic types
\par
of IT roles are characterized. The outcomes are finally vali-
\par
dated through a second round of interviews with another panel
\par
of experts. Qualitative evaluation of the framework corrobo-
\par
rates that the proposed typology is perceived as valuable for
\par
(1) analyzing the process of IT business value creation through
\par
119120 F. ZAND ET AL.
\par
linking IT assets to emergent capabilities they can create or
\par
enhance, (2) (re)structuring the minds of corporate managers
\par
to explore and examine their IT needs and to make informed
\par
investment decisions to meet those needs, and (3) evolving the
\par
business--IT relationship towards a more mature synergy.
\par
The article is structured as follows. The next section dis-
\par
cusses the research methodology that is a combination of
\par
theory development from the literature and empirical research
\par
on the theory developed. The section ``Why and How Are IT
\par
Typologies Useful?'' not only explains why and how a new
\par
typology is a valuable contribution but also establishes the
\par
weaknesses of the existing group of typologies. The ``Review
\par
of the Existing ITBV Typologies'' section reviews the litera-
\par
ture on IT value creation and IT categorization models in order
\par
to spot weaknesses of the existing typologies. The theoretical
\par
foundations of the model are explained in the section ``Typology
\par
Development''. In the ``Interview Results'' section, the results of
\par
qualitative interviews with senior IT managers and consultants
\par
are reported and analyzed. Finally, conclusions and implications
\par
for future research are presented in the final section.
\par
\textbf{RESEARCH METHODOLOGY}
\par
In line with the research question posed in the previous
\par
section, this study aims to develop theory and empirically study
\par
the developed theory. Accordingly, a two-stage multi-method
\par
approach is conducted (Myers, 2009; Silverman, 2000):
\par
\textbf{Stage 1. Selection of Key Attributes and Development of}
\par
\textbf{the Attribute Space}
\par
In the first stage, we needed to identify key attributes that
\par
form the dimensions of the typology to be developed. The
\par
objective was in fact to identify those basic characteristics of
\par
IT that can best support us to understand, study, and eval-
\par
uate different roles of IT in the organization. Therefore, we
\par
initially reviewed the existing body of the literature on ITBV
\par
models, with a focus on IT-based typologies (see the section
\par
``Why and How Are IT Typologies Useful?'' for this literature
\par
review). In this regard, we collected and reviewed seminal pub-
\par
lications by first searching in major social science databases
\par
(including SCOPUS, Social Science Citation Index [SSCI], and
\par
Social Science Research Network [SSRN]) for relevant publi-
\par
cations discussing ITBV. At the same time, the authors used
\par
the references of the selected publications to find more relevant
\par
literature. Next, the selected publications were subjected to a
\par
full-length screening by the authors, and the core components
\par
of the models or frameworks in each publication were identi-
\par
fied. Finally, the collected attributes were sorted based on their
\par
frequency of explicit use in the literature and their importance
\par
in explaining the characteristics and effects of the IT resource of
\par
the firm. The two top attributes in the list were then selected for
\par
constructing the typology, contingent upon expert verification
\par
through a first round of interviews discussed below.
\par
\textbf{Stage 2. Empirical Evaluation of the Typology and}
\par
\textbf{Assessment of Its Usability}
\par
The second stage of research concerns qualitative evaluation
\par
and validation of the typology and its theoretical and practical
\par
relevance. This was done through a number of semi-structured
\par
interviews with IT experts, through which characteristics of the
\par
typology and its applications were evaluated in two rounds.
\par
In the first round of interviews, we evaluate the results of our
\par
literature review together with the experts. In the second round
\par
of interviews, we aimed at evaluating the practical usability and
\par
applicability of the proposed typology. This process yielded two
\par
different sets of interview data.
\par
From different qualitative research methodologies, semi-
\par
structured problem-centered interview (SS PCI) with a panel
\par
of experts was chosen as the appropriate method. This is a
\par
commonly used method for empirical investigation and content
\par
validity among IS scholars (Emory \& Cooper, 1991; Silverman,
\par
1998). To construct our panel of experts, we initially compiled
\par
a list of 78 senior IT managers and consultants. The list was
\par
drawn from university and colleague contacts with companies
\par
and individuals. Two primary criteria were used for selecting the
\par
panelists. First, each expert should have had at least five years of
\par
relevant work experience in managing and/or consulting large
\par
IT projects. Second, s/he should have been employed by a
\par
medium or large organization with at least 50 full-time employ-
\par
ees. Seniority of the interviewees was a primary concern, as
\par
senior practitioners tend to have a broader experience with var-
\par
ious IT projects/systems and deeper knowledge of the business
\par
value they deliver (in diverse sectoral contexts). We opted for
\par
larger firms, as small firms have a rather limited internal IT
\par
and external client portfolio and hence could not provide us
\par
with the broad overview and deep insight we aimed for. For ini-
\par
tial screening, the resume of the IT managers/consultants was
\par
strictly verified against the above two criteria. Out of 78 experts,
\par
61 were proved to be eligible for further contacts. Preliminary
\par
telephone calls were used to introduce the researchers, the
\par
content of the research, and the objectives of the interview.
\par
Standard guidelines as suggested by Czaja and Blair (2005)
\par
were followed to increase the response rate. The experts were
\par
assured of confidentiality and anonymity of their inputs and
\par
were promised sight of final results of the research. Thanks
\par
to informal/personal contacts of our university colleagues to
\par
most of the contacted experts, we finally managed to have inter-
\par
view agreements with 54 experts (i.e., a response rate of 89\%).
\par
The remaining seven experts declared no interest or time avail-
\par
ability to conduct the interview within the time frame of our
\par
interest.
\par
The sample of 54 experts was then divided randomly
\par
into two equal parts. The members of a subsample were
\par
interviewed in the first round to evaluate the relevance and
\par
appropriateness of the typology and its components. The
\par
experts in the other subsample were interviewed later in
\par
the second round in order to appraise the usability and
\par
applicability of the typology. Members of the full panelA ROLE-BASED TYPOLOGY OF INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY 121
\par
have an average relevant experience of 11.2 years and rep-
\par
resent the following sectors: chemical/pharmaceutical, steel
\par
and automobile manufacturing, computer and business ser-
\par
vices, construction, energy and public supplies, financial
\par
intermediaries, telecom, logistics and transportation tourism,
\par
and healthcare. Table 1 shows the composition of the
\par
samples.
\par
To improve performance, suggested guidelines in Lindlof
\par
and Taylor (2002) and Myers and Newman (2007) were fol-
\par
lowed. Accordingly, open-ended questions were mainly asked,
\par
sometimes in an ad hoc manner, to give enough room to
\par
the interviewee to explore and examine different aspects and
\par
dimensions of the central issue under question. The interviews
\par
had also a general, pre-formulated guideline to ensure compara-
\par
bility of interviews (in the form of an interview protocol).1 The
\par
interviews in both rounds lasted between 80 and 110 minutes,
\par
while all of them were tape recorded and fully transcribed after-
\par
wards. Additional observations were noted immediately after
\par
each interview was concluded.
\par
To improve the internal validity, all three authors have
\par
reviewed the interview transcriptions. Each case (i.e., interview
\par
transcript) was carefully parsed to mark relevant keywords.
\par
These keywords imply attributes or criterions, which in the
\par
eyes of the interviewee seem important or determining in dif-
\par
ferentiating and/or characterizing different IT implementations
\par
(i.e., systems, applications, or projects). Following this seg-
\par
menting process, the keywords derived from the transcripts
\par
were grouped into a few thematic aspects, each of which
\par
refers to similar properties with a common root. Finally, these
\par
thematic aspects were assessed in terms of their frequency
\par
of appearance and degree of consistency and significance
\par
in each interview individually, as well as in all interviews
\par
collectively.
\par
\textbf{WHY AND HOW ARE IT TYPOLOGIES USEFUL?}
\par
Many researchers have called for opening the black box
\par
of ITBV in order to gain more in-depth understanding of
\par
the phenomenon and to highlight the specific intermediary
\par
channels and mechanisms through which firms use IT to sup-
\par
port their core capabilities and their strategic moves (Barua,
\par
Kriebel, \& Mukhopadhyay, 1995; Kohli \& Grover, 2008;
\par
Mukhopadhyay \& Cooper, 1993; Tanriverdi, 2005). Recent
\par
attempts in response to these calls introduce theoretical frame-
\par
works and empirical models that aim at explaining the mecha-
\par
nisms and stages through which IT assets become effective and
\par
hence influence the performance and/or competitive position
\par
of the firm (e.g., Cao, 2010; Melville, Kraemer, \& Gurbaxani,
\par
2004; Ravichandran \& Lertwongsatien, 2005; Wade \& Hulland,
\par
2004).
\par
It has been a long tradition among ITBV researchers to start
\par
their analysis from IT investments, link them to organizational
\par
capabilities and intermediate impacts, and finally end with firm
\par
performance effects (e.g., Melville et al., 2004). Kohli and
\par
Grover (2008), however, argued that research should first begin
\par
with asking about specific (digital) business capabilities that the
\par
firm requires for increasing its performance and bypassing the
\par
competition. The question of which IT resources are needed
\par
to build those capabilities comes next. They conceptualize this
\par
way of thinking about and studying IT as a process of ``IT-
\par
izing.'' This refers to using IT to enable, magnify, or accelerate
\par
organizational capabilities that are required to execute a busi-
\par
ness imperative. This implies that IT is a catalyst for changing
\par
and leveraging organizational capital to harness business value
\par
(Radhakrishnan, Zu, \& Grover, 2008).
\par
Yet the research direction recommended above requires
\par
answering a set of new questions. Even if we know what busi-
\par
ness capabilities are required, we still need to understand what
\par
