\par
IT resources are appropriate or even necessary to build those
\par
capabilities and how they lead to IT-izing business processes.
\par
This includes identifying various functional processes through
\par
which firm capabilities are digitized. According to Kohli and
\par
Grover (2008), better models and typologies are required to
\par
broaden our understanding of diverse manifestations of IT in
\par
practice and mechanisms through which capabilities accrue.
\par
Theoretical developments should both identify various types
\par
of functional processes involved in the course of IT-izing firm
\par
capabilities and also match capabilities of IT resources to those
\par
of business processes they support or empower.
\par
By providing a review of existing typologies and classifica-
\par
tion schemes of IT and ITBV process, the next section helps us
\par
achieve two goals. First, it helps us to gain theoretical knowl-
\par
edge on the attributes that are important for the analysis of
\par
ITBV process; these attributes will serve as the key categoriza-
\par
tion variables in the construction phase of the typology (where
\par
the attribute space is formed). Second, it helps us to identify the
\par
shortcomings of the existing models.
\par
(Malone \& Crowston, 1994). Capability-based models of ITBV
\par
are a response to this gap in the literature. Among this group
\par
of models, the existing classifications adopt either an \emph{opera-}
\par
\emph{tional }or a \emph{strategic }perspective towards analyzing corporate IT.
\par
Operational models define IT capabilities at a lower level of
\par
organization, according to functionalities or attributes of indi-
\par
vidual IT applications with respect to how they support the
\par
operational routines of the company. This level of analysis
\par
is vulnerable to fragmentation issues as the integrated, strate-
\par
gic value of IT might be ignored (Sambamurthy, Bharadwaj,
\par
\& Grover, 2003; Tallon, Kraemer, \& Gurbaxani, 2000). As a
\par
result, operational typologies might fail to explicitly recognize
\par
and value the strategic objectives of IT (as a collection of orga-
\par
nizational assets and competencies). Strategic models define IT
\par
capabilities at a higher level, according to strategic values of IT
\par
in an integral form, and try to explain how they support spe-
\par
cific business goals and lead to competitive advantage. This
\par
level is vulnerable to aggregation issues, as IT effects might
\par
be best observable at or near the site where the technology
\par
is actually being used (Barua et al., 1995; Mukhopadhyay \&
\par
Cooper, 1993). As a result, strategic typologies might identify
\par
and observe concrete, measurable impacts of IT at different
\par
functional levels of the organization.
\par
We conducted a literature review of the existing typologies
\par
of IT and ITBV process. The ``Typology Development'' section
\par
of this article discusses the methodology of this review. Due to
\par
space constraints, the results of this literature study are summa-
\par
rized in Appendix A. Additional details on the outcomes of the
\par
literature review, including the observed shortcoming per each
\par
model, can be made available upon request. Overall, the litera-
\par
ture review reveals that the analyzed models bear at least one of
\par
the following four shortcomings:
\par
\textbf{REVIEW OF THE EXISTING ITBV TYPOLOGIES}
\par
In an attempt to address the issue of IT-embeddedness
\par
and IT-izing, Nevo and Wade (2010) developed a conceptual
\par
framework to establish how ITBV is created and sustained.
\par
By synthesizing the resource-based view and systems theory,
\par
they tried to expose part of the content of the IT-izing pro-
\par
cess. Central to their framework, IT-enabled resources and their
\par
emergent capabilities are the IT-ized business capabilities that
\par
Kohli and Grover (2008) referred to.
\par
However, Nevo and Wade (2010) only explained the gen-
\par
eral process through which IT assets can be used to adapt
\par
and modify organizational resources without exploring explicit
\par
paths through which this process takes place. They empha-
\par
sized compatibility between IT and organizational resources
\par
as a necessary assumption to realize emergent and synergistic
\par
capabilities. But they did not explore the underlying condi-
\par
tions for this kind of resource compatibility. We argue that a
\par
clearer understanding of the primary features and functional-
\par
ities of different IT resources is required before we can assess
\par
their compatibility with organizational resources. Compatibility
\par
is in fact realized when features and functionalities of the spe-
\par
cific IT resource(s) that is (are) used to IT-ize an organizational
\par
resource fit, or are aligned with, those business features and
\par
functionalities that are needed to create emergent capabilities
\par
and improve performance of the firm. It is only under these
\par
compatibility conditions that the potential value of IT can be
\par
practically realized.
\par
Among existing typologies and taxonomies of ITBV, earliest
\par
models typically employ machine-technology conceptualiza-
\par
tions of IT and ignore adoption issues and business impacts
\par
entirety (see Fry, 1982 for a review of the relevant literature).
\par
On the other hand, the earlier non-technical typologies usu-
\par
ally do not distinguish potential uses of various types of IT
\par
1. the model either adopts an operational or a strategic perspec-
\par
tive to study IT and/or its value creation process and hence
\par
provides a one-sided account of the ITBV phenomenon (e.g.,
\par
focusing on project management as proposed by Bardhan,
\par
Krishnan, and Lin (2007), or on business processes proposed
\par
by Sabherwal and Chan (2001),)
\par
2. the model is context-specific (i.e., it focuses on the appli-
\par
cation of IT in a specific discipline or organizational/task
\par
context and thereby cannot serve as a wide-scope manage-
\par
ment tool in diverse settings), for example, IT application in
\par
Supply Chain (Turban, Leidner, McLean, \& Wetherbe, 2009)
\par
or New Product Development (NPD) (Nambisan, 2003,)
\par
3. the model is not comprehensive (i.e., it is not complete in
\par
recognizing all the different intermediate mechanisms thor-
\par
ough which IT leads to business capabilities and creates
\par
value for the firm), for example, the typology provided by
\par
Remenyi, Money, and Twite (1994), which includes automa-
\par
tion, information, and transformation, but leaves out other
\par
dimensions including integration (Venkatraman, 1994) and
\par
coordination (Mulligan, 2002)A ROLE-BASED TYPOLOGY OF INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY 123
\par
4. the model classifies IT resources without explicitly linking
\par
them to business objectives or organizational performance
\par
of the firm and thereby is not practical for ITBV assess-
\par
ment, for example, the typology proposed by Nambisan
\par
(2003) lacks in providing the link between the model and
\par
the high-level business objectives and performance (perhaps
\par
as a consequence of being focused on a specific context---see
\par
the second shortcoming.)
\par
The next section proposes a conceptual typology, which aims
\par
at overcoming the discussed shortcomings.
\par
\textbf{TYPOLOGY DEVELOPMENT}
\par
Review of the literature presented earlier indicates that IT
\par
can have diverse roles in firms. We define the concept of ``IT
\par
Roles'' as: the primary mechanisms and core functions through
\par
which IT enhances and/or enables the capability of the firm
\par
to (1) execute its working operations, (2) introduce new oper-
\par
ations, (3) achieve its strategic goals, and/or (4) formulate
\par
new strategies. IT roles disentangle the connection between
\par
IT resources and business processes of the firm, indicate the
\par
essential functionalities of IT, and explicate how IT investment
\par
contributes to firm performance.
\par
Uncertainty arises from, among others, the organizational tasks
\par
contingency, instability of the external environment, fluctuation
\par
of service demand, and interdependencies and differentiations
\par
among internal and external subunits of the firm. In essence,
\par
OIPT considers the link between information as a key orga-
\par
nizational resource and managing its usage as the most criti-
\par
cal performance factor of the organization. OIPT argues that
\par
organizations must adopt at least one of the following four
\par
information processing designs to improve performance. Two
\par
of these information processing designs reduce the need for
\par
information processing: \emph{managing the environment }and \emph{creat-}
\par
\emph{ing self-contained tasks}. The other two information processing
\par
designs are meant to create mechanisms that increase the orga-
\par
nization's capacity to process information: \emph{investing in IS }and
\par
\emph{creating lateral relations}. OIPT posits that an organization is
\par
doomed to accept a lower performance, unless it adopts at least
\par
one of the above operational designs, while getting access to
\par
information of a higher quality, richness, and timeliness that
\par
can be translated to organizational intelligence and performance
\par
through better decisions and operations (Daft \& Lengel, 1986;
\par
Fairbank, Labianca, Steensma, \& Metters, 2006; Huber, 1990).
\par
We conclude from the premise of OIPT that the extent
\par
of information processing capacity which a certain IT system
\par
offers to the firm can determine the extent that system supports
\par
the firm to reduce uncertainty and increase operational perfor-
\par
mance. In other words, \emph{information processing capacity }can
\par
serve as a proper attribute to link IT capability to organizational
\par
performance. Different types of ITs would have differential
\par
effects on firm performance, depending on the degree of infor-
\par
mation processing they accommodate (and accordingly their
\par
position along the ``information processing'' dimension of our
\par
typology).
\par
\textbf{Theories Used to Conceptualize Typology Dimensions}
\par
To overcome the shortcomings discussed in the previous
\par
section, this article opts to draw on two theories: \emph{Organizational}
\par
\emph{Information Processing Theory (OIPT) }and \emph{Resource-Based}
\par
\emph{View (RBV). }The decision to adopt these two specific theories
\par
relies on the following reasons.2 First, these theories have the
\par
firm as their unit of analysis. Our typology is also meant to char-
\par
acterize IT roles at the level of the firm. Second, they analyze
\par
firm resources and their attributes from two distinct perspec-
\par
tives. While OIPT primarily adopts an operational perceptive
\par
towards analyzing the firm's IT resources and their impacts,
\par
RBV takes a strategic view. Our typology intends to synthesize
\par
and integrate these two perspectives into one integrative model.
\par
Third, these theories explicate a clear link between resource
\par
attributes and performance of the firm. Our typology is intended
\par
to link IT assets to business capabilities and objectives as well.
\par
Fourth, in these theories, the two attributes that constitute the
\par
foundations of our typology are essential determinants (and
\par
predictors) of organizational performance.
\par
\textbf{Operational Perspective: OIPT}
\par
The OIPT conceives information as the most critical success
\par
factor for organizations to face contingencies and achieve per-
\par
formance (Galbraith, 1974; Tushman \& Nadler, 1978). From
\par
an OIPT perspective, organizations are designed around infor-
\par
mation, information sources/flows, and information processing
\par
capabilities to reduce uncertainty. Uncertainty reflects the lack
\par
of (enough) information on the current or future status of the
\par
internal tasks or the environment surrounding the organization.
\par
\textbf{Strategic Perspective: RBV}
\par
The RBV of the firm argues that firms possess heteroge-
\par
neous resources, a subset of which enables them to achieve
\par
superior performance (Barney, 1991; Grant, 1991). According
\par
to Barney (1991), for firm resources to confer superior perfor-
\par
mance (and accordingly, competitive advantage), they should
\par
be: (1) valuable (i.e., supporting the firm to achieve its (strate-
\par
gic) objectives), (2) rare (i.e., accessible to only a limited num-
\par
ber of firms), (3) (imperfectly) inimitable, non-substitutable,
\par
and non-transferable (i.e., imposing substantial cost and/or time
\par
disadvantages for competitors to imitate), and (4) (structurally)
\par
well-organized and embedded into the organization for proper
\par
exploitation.
\par
Earlier RBV studies tend to focus on the unique value of
\par
IT as a scarce resource, while more recent studies attributed
\par
IT payoff to co-presence or co-development of (IT and non-
\par
IT) organizational resources (Davern \& Kauffman, 2000; Hitt
\par
\& Brynjolfsson, 1997). For instance, Clemons and Row (1991)
\par
referred to an important characteristic of IT as its ability to
\par
change organizational resources and their value. IT does this124 F. ZAND ET AL.
\par
through facilitating the appropriation and leverage of other
\par
resources, increasing their potential value, managing their inter-
\par
actions and integrations, and complementing diverse economic
\par
activities of the firm.
\par
Central to RBV is the notion of resource heterogeneity and
\par
firm idiosyncrasy. Organizational change (whether or not IT-
\par
enabled) is one of the main reasons behind heterogeneity and
\par
idiosyncrasy of firm resources (Brynjolfsson \& Hitt, 2000;
\par
Powell \& Dent-Micallef, 1997). According to Orlikowski and
\par
Iacono (2001), firms adopt ITs that are not homogenous and
\par
undifferentiated entities---rather; they are malleable and dif-
\par
fer in their intrinsic characteristics, their ability to complement
\par
other organizational resources, and the degree of organizational
\par
change that needs to occur when they are implemented. O'Hara,
\par
Watson, and Kavan (1999) also referred to a similar notion by
\par
differentiating IT systems with regard to the extent to which the
\par
firm's processes need to evolve in order to adapt to the require-
\par
ments of the new technology and optimize its performance
\par
payoffs.
\par
As a common element in the RBV studies of ITBV, we con-
\par
clude that \emph{organizational change intensity }of an IT resource
\par
is a fundamental attribute to determine the extent of its per-
\par
formance and competitive implications. Those IT systems that
\par
entail fundamental organizational changes, require significant
\par
organizational complementarities, and/or result in substantial
\par
organizational embeddedness are more complicated, incur sig-
\par
nificantly more costs, require more time to be implemented,
\par
learned, and adapted, and impose more critical risks to the orga-
\par
nization. However, once implemented and deployed properly,
\par
these systems provide much stronger barriers to imitation and
\par
substitution, create more value, and infer much stronger perfor-
\par
mance and competitive advantages for the firm. This means that
\par
different types of IT systems would have different performance
\par
effects (partly) depending on the level of organizational change
\par
they bring with themselves into the firm (and accordingly their
\par
position along the ``organizational change'' dimension of our
\par
typology).
\par
and secure manner. The information role of IT is accom-
\par
plished by simple operations such as sorting, categorizing, and
\par
summarizing data and improves the search and retrieval pro-
\par
cesses. In addition to accessibility and reusability, security and
\par
reliability of data are also of great importance. Data needs to
\par
be protected from leakage, corruption, and damage. Overall,
\par
the information role of IT neither involves high levels of data
\par
processing nor fundamentally changes the way an organization
\par
is structured or operates. An example is an office scanner that
\par
generates data by digitizing a paper document into a computer
\par
file. The existing literature accounts for this role of IT and its
\par
performance effects (e.g., Davenport \& Short, 1990; Laudon \&
\par
Laudon, 2009; Mooney, Gurbaxani, \& Kraemer, 1996; Remenyi
\par
et al., 1994; Sambamurthy \& Zmud, 2000; Weill, 1992).3
\par
\emph{Communication Role of IT}
\par
The communication role of IT is defined as the ability
\par
of IT to promptly transmit and disseminate data and infor-
\par
mation among distant individuals, teams, and organizations.
\par
Similar to the information role, the communication role of IT
\par
by itself neither involves high levels of data processing nor fun-
\par
damentally changes the way the organization functions. Email
\par
exchange, Radio-frequency identification (RFID), videoconfer-
\par
encing tools, corporate intranet, simple groupware software,
\par
and interactive whiteboard are examples of digital tools that
\par
primarily focus on the communication role of IT. The literature
\par
points to this particular role of IT in many aspects (e.g., Bardhan
\par
et al., 2007; Bouwman, Van Den Hoof, Van De Wijngaert, \&
\par
Van Dijk, 2005; Lai, 2001; Straub \& Wetherbe, 1989).
\par
\textbf{Organizational Roles of IT}
\par
We distinguish seven types of IT roles: (1) Information,
\par
(2) Communication, (3) Automation, (4) Coordination,
\par
(5) Integration, (6) Transformation, and (7) Innovation. Below,
\par
different types of IT roles are defined, described, and exem-
\par
plified. In doing so, particular attention is given to the two
\par
primary criteria that we earlier identified based on the adopted
\par
theories. These relate to the extent of information processing
\par
an IT system performs and the extent of organizational change
\par
it enacts.
\par
\emph{Information Role of IT}
\par
The information role of IT is defined as the ability of IT
\par
to generate, gather, and store data in a reusable, organized,
\par
\emph{Automation Role of IT}
\par
The automation role of IT is defined as the ability of IT to
\par
standardize, mechanize, and computerize operational activities
\par
and decision-making processes of the firm by eliminating or
\par
minimizing manual interventions. In fact, automation reflects
\par
the use of technology to replace labor. Labor substitution then
\par
results in efficiency gains through productivity improvements
\par
and cost savings. Computer-aided design (CAD) systems auto-
\par
mate the design processes, robots mechanize the manufacturing
\par
processes and assembly lines, and expert systems and Decision
\par
Support Systems (DSS) facilitate the decision-making pro-
\par
cesses of the firm; these are all examples of the automation role
\par
of IT. The application of IT as an automating tool includes high
\par
levels of computation and information processing in the form of
\par
monitoring, programming, controlling, and commanding. Yet
\par
it does not result in fundamental changes in the concept of
\par
organizational entities or the nature of business processes.4 The
\par
automation role of IT and its impact on firm performance have
\par
been frequently highlighted in the literature (e.g., Davenport \&
\par
Short, 1990; Fiedler, Grover, \& Teng, 1994; Laudon \& Laudon,
\par
2009; Mooney et al., 1996; Remenyi et al., 1994; Weill \& Ross,
\par
2009).\emph{Coordination Role of IT}
\par
The coordination role of IT is defined as the ability of IT
\par
to organize, co-plan, and synchronize activities and parties,
\par
in a synchronous or asynchronous fashion. Existing litera-
\par
ture supports this particular role of IT (e.g., Bardhan et al.,
\par
2007; Bouwman et al., 2005; Mulligan, 2002; Nambisan, 2003).
\par
Electronic inventory management systems of wholesalers coor-
\par
dinate their ordering processes with their sales. Participatory
\par
Project Management (PPM) tools coordinate different parties
\par
involved in a project. Electronic document review systems
\par
synchronize and coordinate editing and reviewing of corpo-
\par
rate documents by different organizational units. Supply Chain
\par
Management (SCM) systems coordinate activities of different
\par
parties along the value chain in order to optimize supply chain
\par
performance. Coordination, like automation, entails high levels
\par
of information processing necessary to link diverse actors (and
\par
their varied objectives) to each other and to attain cooperative
\par
action. As to the degree of organizational change, this IT role
\par
enables different organizational entities to separately exist and
\par
function (as they are used to), while they just share the neces-
\par
sary information for the purpose of co-planning of decisions and
\par
actions in order to reach a common goal.
\par
\emph{Integration Role of IT}
\par
The integration role of IT is defined as the ability of
\par
IT to combine, assimilate, and fundamentally reorganize the
\par
existing processes and domains of the firm into new, unified
\par
processes and domains. IT-enabled integration has been accred-
\par
ited in the scholarly literature from different perspectives (e.g.,
\par
Mulligan, 2002; Sambamurthy \& Zmud, 2000; Song \& Song,
\par
2010). As examples, Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) inte-
\par
grates the procurement, production, and sales functions of the
\par
company into a single, centrally-controlled system. Similarly,
\par
Knowledge Management Systems (KMS) integrate different
\par
knowledge sources within an organization to create a unified
\par
knowledge repository for employees. Integration occurs when
\par
IT is used to couple multiple processes and/or domains in
\par
a tight and intertwined manner such that fundamental (struc-
\par
tural) changes enact, new entities form, and old entities are
\par
not anymore (readily) recognizable. Needless to say, the pro-
\par
cess of integration involves high levels of data manipulation and
\par
information processing.
\par
\emph{Transformation Role of IT}
\par
The transformation role of IT is defined as the ability of IT
\par
to introduce significantly modified production strategies, sales
\par
methods, organizational structures, and business processes, or
\par
to renew the existing ones, which leads to reconfiguration and
\par
transition of the organization as a whole to new forms. IT-
\par
enabled organizational transformation has been the subject of a
\par
number of academic studies (e.g., Brynjolfsson \& Hitt, 2000;
\par
Hitt \& Brynjolfsson, 1997; Mooney et al., 1996; Remenyi
\par
et al., 1994; Teo, Tan, \& Wei, 1997; Venkatraman, 1994).
\par
This IT role manifests itself in the form of, among others,
\par
A ROLE-BASED TYPOLOGY OF INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY 125
\par
restructuring/redesigning of the organization (e.g., flattening of
\par
hierarchies and delegation of management responsibilities in
\par
decentralized organizations) and re-engineering business pro-
\par
cesses (e.g., introduction of lean manufacturing, build-to-order
\par
[BTO] production or just-in-time [JIT] strategy, implementa-
\par
tion of Six Sigma, or engagement in e-commerce). In the above
\par
examples, IT revolutionizes the way an organization is struc-
\par
tured, operated, and/or managed and, in fact, transforms it to a
\par
(totally) new form.
\par
\emph{Innovation Role of IT}
\par
The innovation role of IT is defined as the ability of IT to
\par
develop or leverage completely new products/services, deliv-
\par
ery methods, business models, and marketing channels. The
\par
outcome of an IT-induced innovation is fundamentally new
\par
with respect to the status quo of the organization, but not nec-
\par
essarily encompassing major data processing. In fact, radical
\par
IT-enabled innovations are typically those with considerable
\par
change impacts without heavy information processing. For
\par
example, IT has enabled the formation and expansion of online
\par
insurance companies that allow users to insure themselves tem-
\par
porarily and flexibly by simply sending a text message and to
\par
pay their premium through their mobile phone bill. This is con-
\par
sidered a totally innovative business model in the insurance
\par
industry, one that has shaken many of the traditional insur-
\par
ance policy models and threatened many big players in the
\par
market. As another example, Twitter or social networks such
\par
as Facebook have created completely new viral communica-
\par
tion, marketing, and delivery channels for firms, while they
\par
have made some traditional channels almost completely obso-
\par
lete. This increasingly emergent role of IT, while relying on
\par
not necessarily big investments or complex data processing,
\par
can create huge business implications and competitive advan-
\par
tages for firms. The literature on IT roles accounts for this as
\par
well (e.g., Joshi, Chi, Datta, \& Han, 2010; Koellinger, 2008;
\par
Westerman \& Curley, 2008; Zand, 2011).
\par
\textbf{Categorization of IT Roles}
\par
The intensity of information processing an IT system carries
\par
out and organizational changes it causes provide a solid basis
\par
for mapping the capability of the system to its performance
\par
implications. Operationally, the processing intensity reflects the
\par
richness and significance of the accessible information and
\par
accordingly the potential value of this information to create
\par
business value for the organization. Strategically, the change
\par
intensity reveals the reach of the IT effects throughout the orga-
\par
nization. The higher the intensity of organizational changes
\par
(resulting from an IT implementation), the more diffused and
\par
embedded the IT effects are in the organization, the more dif-
\par
ficult it is to replicate them in another organization, and the
\par
stronger the response-lag drivers5 are. Figure 1 categorizes IT
\par
roles along these two theoretical criteria.126 F. ZAND ET AL.
\par
Fig. 1. IT value creation process and IT roles.
\par
First-order roles reflect the basic, still essential, capabil-
\par
ity of IT. This class includes \emph{information }and \emph{communication}
\par
roles of IT.6 First-order roles provide the necessary foun-
\par
dation for higher-order roles, by providing basic capabilities
\par
such as data generation/digitization, data storage/retrieval, data
\par
security/protection, and data transmission/dissemination. The
\par
level of processing of data and information through these roles
\par
is relatively low as they typically accommodate simple oper-
\par
ations. Similarly, they neither require nor result in substantial
\par
changes in organizational processes and entities. Consequently,
\par
their performance effects do not provide the organization with
\par
substantial advantage and significant leap against the compe-
\par
tition. The very ubiquitous and easy-to-exploit nature of the
\par
first-order roles makes them almost commodity factors of pro-
\par
duction, which are essential for all businesses to survive but pro-
\par
vide distinction to none (Carr, 2003). Electronic mail, databases,
\par
and intranet, for example, are nowadays present in almost any
\par
corporation (of almost any size); doing business without them
\par
seems to be, if not impossible, unbelievably cumbersome.
\par
Second-order roles enhance the capability of IT provided by
\par
the first-order roles through providing high-level processing and
\par
management of information. This processing can take various,
\par
usually complex, computational forms such as control algo-
\par
rithms, numerical calculations, and data mining. This class of IT
\par
roles includes \emph{automation }and \emph{coordination }roles. Although the
\par
intensity of information processing is high, second-order roles
\par
do not result in fundamental structural changes of the organi-
\par
zational elements. Manually operated and/or coordinated tasks
\par
and processes are now computerized, while the involved orga-
\par
nizational entities and processual workflows continue to exist.
\par
The performance effect and business value of second-order
\par
roles are greater than those of their first-order counterparts, due
\par
to availability of new, processed, and rich data as well as result-
\par
ing (operational) efficiency gains. To name a few instances, the
\par
automation of reservation processes of American Airlines and
\par
coordination of sales activities of American Hospital Supply
\par
with its buying hospitals through introducing new IT systems
\par
(i.e., SABRE and ASAP systems respectively), complemented
\par
by specific organizational practices/capabilities, provided the
\par
two companies with considerable revenue growth and competi-
\par
tive advantage for a long time.7
\par
Third-order roles provide a higher level of sophistication
\par
and reconfiguration to an organization. Including \emph{integration}
\par
and \emph{transformation }roles, this category integrates/synergizes
\par
knowledge, assimilates different departments of the firm, com-
\par
bines different units of the firm with those of its partners,
\par
creates new organizational entities, and/or fundamentally alters
\par
the market relations, management structure, and/or strategic
\par
positioning of the firm. Third-order roles are difficult, costly,
\par
and complex capabilities of IT to develop (or acquire). The
\par
social integration, organizational embeddedness and complex
\par
interrelation of IT systems, delivering third-order capabilities,
\par
with other organizational resources make their imitation or sub-
\par
stitution difficult and time-consuming. For instance, IT has
\par
revolutionized the way Dell does business. Introduction of the
\par
JIT and BTO strategies, along with an extensive use of online
\par
services (to bypass sales intermediaries) and computer-aided
\par
manufacturing (CAM) systems has enabled the company to
\par
stand up the competition and boost its sales dramatically in the
\par
past years. Without a strategic and smart use of IT, this whole
\par
organizational transformation would be totally impossible, and
\par
Dell would have never been a role model in the industry.
\par
Finally, the fourth-order role or the innovation role of IT
\par
enables companies to revolutionize the way they do busi-
\par
ness, structure themselves, or manage their clients/suppliers
\par
through fundamentally new approaches, which are not neces-
\par
sarily processing-intensive. For significant IT-enabled organiza-
\par
tional changes (leading to sustained competitive advantage) to
\par
happen, not always an enormous quantity of information needs
\par
to be gathered or processed. By using innovative resources in
\par
innovative ways, companies can introduce new products, deliver
\par
new services, and leverage new business models. A clear-cut
\par
example is how Twitter, as a simple online service, has become
\par
the new, dominant platform for communication and market-
\par
ing to external parties in many contemporary firms. Not all
\par
organizations manage to exhibit fourth-order roles of IT, as
\par
they do not dare to take the risk of adopting these IT systems
\par
and their resulting organizational changes or fail to implement
\par
and institutionalize these systems effectively; yet those which
\par
successfully do can a priori be expected to achieve superior
\par
performance and competitive advantage.
\par
As to another element of the model, the dashed arrow
\par
denotes the evolutionary roadmap of value creation through
\par
IT. In other words, the dashed arrow in Figure 1 reflects the
\par
development path of an IT-implementing enterprise seeking for
\par
higher benefits and business value through more optimized IT
\par
investments. The path starts at the first-order roles and goes
\par
through the second-order and third-order roles until it reaches
\par
the fourth-order role. If the first steps towards achieving radicalA ROLE-BASED TYPOLOGY OF INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY 127
\par
performance improvements are to support the daily operations
\par
of the company as is, to enhance the essential information and
\par
communication capacities, and to automate the existing routines
\par
and processes, the ultimate step should be obliterating and aban-
\par
doning old ways of working and creating and reengineering
\par
entirely new ones (Hammer, 1990). The ultimate challenge of
\par
any IT manager, however, is to innovate using IT systems and
\par
applications that are not essentially complicated or expensive.
