\textbf{CONCLUSIONS AND RECOMMENDATIONS FOR}
\par
\textbf{FUTURE RESEARCH}
\par
IT has long been argued and testified as one of the strate-
\par
gic resources that can leverage the performance and position
\par
of the firm. Still, IT investments can only be rewarding if they
\par
suit the needs and goals of the implementing company. In this
\par
respect, one of the biggest challenges facing the managers of
\par
today's corporations is to get a clear picture about their level of
\par
IT capability, to spot their IT needs and set their IT roadmap
\par
accordingly, and to make informed choices from the wide range
\par
of available technologies in the marketplace. The typological
\par
framework proposed in this article attempts to serve corporate
\par
and IT managers with some features to face this challenge. It is a
\par
step towards opening the black box of IT value-creation process
\par
through demonstrating how and explaining why certain organi-
\par
zational outcomes happen following investments in certain IT
\par
applications.
\par
In line with the vision of Weill and Ross (2009), who
\par
aimed to link business operations to business performance and
\par
strategy, this article brings the idea one step ahead by classify-
\par
ing IT roles as the essential intermediate mechanisms through
\par
which IT resources, capabilities, and investments are trans-
\par
formed into business objectives, processes, and performance
\par
effects. In other words, the framework acknowledges IT roles as
\par
an important bridge between implementation efforts and organi-
\par
zational effects. The article discusses seven primary roles, clas-
\par
sified into four general classes: information and communication
\par
(first-order roles), automation and coordination (second-order
\par
roles), integration and transformation (third-order roles), and
\par
innovation (fourth-order roles), which offer managers a clearer
\par
picture of how IT actually creates value in their organization.
\par
As to a future application of the proposed typology, we sug-
\par
gest its use in line with the (capabilities required [CR] IT
\par
business value) thematic progression proposed by Kohli and
\par
Grover (2008, p. 31). With this paradigm shift, they proposed
\par
that firms must first uncover the specific digital CR for their
\par
organization and then identify and analyze what it takes to build
\par
them and generate business value from them (Kohli \& Grover,
\par
2008, p. 30). Our proposed typology bridges the backward path
\par
from CR to IT and helps us exactly identify and analyze what it
\par
takes to build the required capabilities. Different capabilities or
\par
initiatives of the firm are dependent on or enabled by different
\par
types of IT roles, which themselves are provided by different
\par
types of IT systems or applications. The suggested framework
\par
in this article can thus make it easier for corporate managers to
\par
inspect their required capabilities and cope with the abundance
\par
of ITs available to them in the market.
\par
Despite the positive appraisal of the typology by the inter-
\par
viewed professionals, as with any typology, its true strength will
\par
be revealed over time. Inevitably, the relevancy and appropri-
\par
ateness of the proposed definition and typology of IT roles need
\par
to be empirically studied and evaluated. To this end, the use-
\par
fulness of the proposed typology can be compared against the
\par
competing models in specific contexts and for specific appli-
\par
cations (for instance small companies, governmental institutes,
\par
and different industrial sectors), which, in turn, help improve the
\par
generalizability of the proposed typology. Other fruitful areas
\par
for future research include: (1) discovering new roles; and (2) an
\par
in-depth examination of relationships among different roles and
\par
different classes of firm-level intermediate and output measures
\par
of performance. This line of research can result in a new the-
\par
ory to explain the organizational impact and business value of130 F. ZAND ET AL.
\par
IT; (3) a further operationalization and measurement of the IT
\par
roles, linking them to business performance. Hence, it is impor-
\par
tant to choose strong proxies or construct new instruments that
\par
can distinguish and quantify various roles; (4) a multi-method
\par
validation of the typology, for instance, through quantitative sur-
\par
veys and longitudinal case studies; (5) further assessment and
\par
adjustment (if needed) of our typology with firms of smaller
\par
size. Among others, this can shed light on the characteristics
\par
of the evolution path and determinants of the maturity level of
\par
IT-adopting organizations over time.
