\par
Geoengineering prognoses are often based on data from a limited number of investigations of soil and rock mass. There
\par
is generally a desire to reduce the uncertainty in the prognoses while minimising the investigation costs. Value of
\par
Information Analysis (VOIA) is a support for decisions regarding investigation strategies and the aim of this paper is to
\par
present methodology for VOIA that takes into account four decision alternatives where the input data could be provided
\par
by experts. The methodology will be applied in a case study where the value of information related to an investigation
\par
borehole will be calculated. The results indicate that the value of information of the borehole is low compared with the
\par
realisation costs of the investigation. It was found that models for VOIA in underground construction projects are
\par
complex but that the analysis can be simplified with extensive use of expert knowledge and calculations of the value of
\par
perfect information as a benchmark for investigation strategies.
