underground construction
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Geoengineering prognoses are generally based on field
\par
studies and investigations of soil and rock mass. The
\par
extent of the investigation programme is decided based
\par
on site-specific conditions in each project. Expert judge-
\par
ment has a major impact on the interpretations of the
\par
geological material and typically guides the final invest-
\par
igation strategy together with the budget for the project.
\par
In general, there is a desire to reduce geological
\par
uncertainty while minimising the investigation costs
\par
although finding the optimal level of investigation is
\par
difficult.
\par
Value of Information Analysis (VOIA) provides
\par
support for decisions regarding the extent of investiga-
\par
tion programmes. The aim of the method is to assess the
\par
economic value of an investigation before the investiga-
\par
tion has actually taken place, and the method is
\par
developed based on Bayesian statistics and traditional
\par
cost--benefit analysis. Decision theory for geoengineer-
\par
ing problems have previously been described by Einstein
\par
and Baecher (1982), Einstein (1996), Sturk (1998),
\par
Karam, Karam, and Einstein (2007), Norrman (2004)
\par
and others. In the early 1990s, Freeze et al. (1990) and
\par
Freeze et al. (1992) developed the method of Data Worth
\par
Analysis, which corresponds to VOIA, for hydrogeolo-
\par
gical investigations, and the method has been used in
\par
environmental engineering for remediation of contami-
\par
nated soils (Back 2006; Norberg and Ros\'en 2006; and
\par
others). The method has been described for geoengineer-
\par
ing purposes by Zetterlund et al. (2011, 2008). The two
\par
latter examples were focused on grouting-related issues
\par
in tunnelling for just two decision alternatives in one
\par
position. However since decisions in tunnelling projects
\par
are generally more complex, and include several possible
\par
alternatives, there is a need to expand the VOIA model
\par
to include more decision alternatives. Experience from
\par
previous work with VOIA in rock engineering has also
\par
shown that there is the potential to facilitate its use by
\par
developing methods where expert judgements can be
\par
used to generate input data instead of complex models.
\par
This paper aims to present methodology for VOIA
\par
that takes into account two decision alternatives in two
\par
alternative positions where the input data could be
\par
provided by experts. An application of the methodology
\par
in a case study at \"Asp\"o Hard Rock Laboratory (\"Asp\"o
\par
HRL) in south-east Sweden is presented. The purpose of
\par
the VOIA was to find out if it is justified in economic
\par
terms to invest in a core-drilled borehole to provide
\par
information for use in deciding on the location of a niche
\par
in a tunnel, and deciding whether or not to plan for
\par
grouting. The VOIA calculations were based on expert
\par
knowledge which was acquired through a structured
\par
elicitation process. The planning and execution of that
\par
process is only described briefly in this paper. For details
\par
see Zetterlund et al. (2014).
\par
