\par
The models for VOIA tend to become complicated
\par
for decision problems in underground construction
\par
projects and they become even more complicated if
\par
quantitative simulation models are used as input data.
\par
The extended use of expert knowledge is therefore
\par
important as a simplification and to make it easier to
\par
use the model in practice. In this paper, a methodology
\par
for VOIA has been developed where the input data could
\par
be provided by experts.
\par
The main conclusions of this study are that:
\par
. The resulting EVI and EVPI were significantly
\par
lower than the investigation costs, but the values
\par
are considered site specific and due to the special
\par
conditions since the project is located in a rock
\par
laboratory with exceptional requirements of the
\par
investigations.
\par
. The mathematical model quickly becomes large
\par
and complex for decisions in more than one
\par
position.
\par
. A simplification is to calculate EVPI to acquire
\par
important information about the boundaries for
\par
reasonable realisation costs for investigations.
\par
7. Conclusions
\par
In this paper, the concept of VOIA has been developed
\par
for deciding between four alternatives. Input data for the
\par
presented methodology are project costs and probabilit-
\par
ies of different events related to the decision alternatives.
\par
The methodology was applied in a case study of a niche
\par
in a tunnel. Using VOIA, the economic value of a core-
\par
drilled borehole was estimated for decisions regarding
\par
the position of the niche and regarding grouting prepara-
\par
tion. EVI was calculated for each separate tunnel
\par
position but due to incoherency in statistical simulations,
\par
discussed in Chapter 2.3, it was not possible to calculate
\par
the total EVI for both positions.
\par
In the case study, neither the EVI nor the EVPI
\par
exceeds the investigation costs and therefore the inves-
\par
tigations are not economically justified. However the
\par
project at \"Asp\"o HRL had a complex structure with many
\par
research groups involved from different geoscientific
\par
fields, and the results from the investigation borehole
\par
were intended to serve different purposes other than
\par
grouting and groundwater pressure issues. The invest-
\par
igation costs could therefore also be split between
\par
different purposes, with the result that the borehole
\par
may be justified any way. A comprehensive view of
