Objectives: Value of Information (VOI) analyses can help align research investments
\par
with areas that could have the greatest impact on patient outcomes, but many ques-
\par
tions remain concerning its feasibility and acceptability to inform real-world prior-
\par
itization decisions. Our objective was to develop a process for calculating VOI in ``real
\par
time'' to inform trial funding decisions within SWOG, a large cancer clinical trials
\par
group. Methods: We adapted a novel and efficient modeling approach - minimal
\par
modeling VOI - using a sample of nine phase II/III trial proposals from the Breast,
\par
Gastrointestinal, and Genitourinary committees reviewed by SWOG's leadership
\par
between 2008-2013. We created decision models for each trial proposal and devised
\par
an efficient process to characterize prior uncertainty in treatment effect by linking
\par
evidence-based assumptions in a trial's sample size calculations with the historical
\par
success rates of SWOG trials. Expected clinical and economic VOI was calculated using
\par
Bayesian updating methods. We customized the process using iterative stakeholder
\par
input. Resul ts: The VOI modeling process was feasible and sufficiently captured key
\par
expected differences in comprehensive outcomes and attendant uncertainty for 8 of 9
\par
trial proposals. Model construction and calculations took one researcher {$<$} 1 week per
\par
proposal. We accommodated stakeholder input by: a) deconstructing VOI metrics into
\par
expected health benefits and incremental healthcare costs, b) assuming treatment
\par
decisions were based on health benefits alone, and c) providing both individual and
\par
population level results. Following this customization, SWOG generally accepted the
\par
VOI framework and results for the retrospective analyses and felt that VOI analyses
\par
would likely be useful in informing future trial proposal evaluations. Conclusions:
\par
We developed an efficient and customized process for calculating the expected VOI
\par
of cancer clinical trials that is feasible for use in real-time decision-making and is
\par
acceptable to stakeholders. Prospective use and assessment of this approach is cur-
\par
rently underway within SWOG.
