\par
Alternative Methods For Generating aRbiTRary Marginal
\par
DisTRibutions And The iMPlications For Simulation Outcomes
\par
Zhuo J.X.
\par
Merck, North Wales, PA, USA
\par
Objectives: Generating multivariate random variables is essential in disease
\par
simulation applications. In this study we examine the implications of alternative
\par
approaches to generate marginal distributions and correlation matrix on simula-
\par
tion outcomes. Methods: We adopt three alternative methods including Cholesky
\par
Decomposition (CD), CD with conditional matching, and the NORmal-To-Anything
\par
(NORTA) method to generate a hypothetical simulation sample with arbitrary mar-
\par
ginal distributions and correlation matrix. As the comparator, we also create an
\par
independent and identically distributed (iid) simulation sample. The samples are
\par
individually populated in a previously developed type 2 diabetes microsimulation
\par
model to predict the major clinical endpoints over 15 years. The endpoints include
\par
all-cause mortality, diabetes-related mortality, and major cardiovascular events. We
\par
examine the goodness of fit by total deviance, i.e., the aggregated values of the rela-
\par
tive difference between the individual predictions with the endpoints observed in the
\par
actual data, in the overall and stratified samples. Resul ts: The results show that,
\par
the model predications deviate from the observed data with an iid sample. Over 15
\par
years, the model over-predicts all the numbers of endpoint events by 20\%, with the
\par
total deviance of 0.73, and the over-prediction is particularly more pronounced in the
\par
younger patients. With a sample of a constructed multivariate normal distribution
\par
using the CD and CD plus conditional matching approach, the deviance is reduced
\par
to 0.41 and 0.58 respectively. A further improvement is observed when using the
\par
NORTA method, with the deviance of the endpoints between model prediction and
\par
actual data further reduced to 0.11. The reduction was mainly contributed by better
\par
approximations in the dispersion of the risk factors among patients. Conclusions:
\par
Random sequences generation has important ramification for simulation outcomes.
\par
Poorly-defined multivariate distributions may significantly distort the simulation
\par
performance. Given its flexibility for both continuous and discrete variables, NORTA
\par
method appears to be a preferable approach.
