PRM65
\par
An Evaluation Of CoMPeting Models For Predicting Cv Event Rates
\par
fRom Ldl-C Levels In Secondary Prevention
\par
Conner C.
\par
Health Strategy, LLC, Remdond, WA, USA
\par
Objectives: This study aims to evaluate four alternative models that each describe
\par
the relationship between LDL-C and CV event rates in secondary prevention. A sec-
\par
ondary aim of this analysis is to promote an approach to develop more reliable disease
\par
simulation models in cardiovascular disease. Methods: Data describing LDL-C and
\par
nfMI+CV Death outcomes were abstracted from several landmark secondary preven-
\par
tion statin trials in clinically stable patient populations (4S, CARE, LIPID, HPS, TNT,
\par
IDEAL, GREACE, AVERT, and LIPS). Linear (L), quadratic (Q), one-knot linear spline
\par
(S1L) and two-knot quadratic spline (S2Q) models were fit. RMSE, leave-one-out cross
\par
validated (LOOCV) and Monte Carlo cross validated (MCCV) RMSE (90\% training, 1,000
\par
replicates) were used to evaluate predictive performance. Predicted event rates based
\par
on LDL-C =
\par
50mg/dL were estimated to illustrate the clinical implications of each
\par
model. To encourage full reproducibility and transparency, all raw data and code (R)
\par
will be made freely available for download online via authors Git repository. Resul ts:
\par
A total of eight models were fit and evaluated, including four distinct functional
\par
forms (L, Q, S1L, and S2Q) across two sets of data (all data (A) and data censured for
\par
high leverage studies(C)). Of all the models evaluated, S2Q-C exhibited the lowest
\par
RMSE (0.317) while L-C produced both the lowest LOOCVRMSE (0.463) and MCCVRMSE
\par
(0.389). Model selection had an impact on predicted percentage of events/yr at 50mg/
\par
dL (range=
\par
0.769 to 2.09). Conclusions: This analysis suggests that a linear model
\par
may be among the most appropriate when describing the relationship between LDL
\par
and nfMI+CV Death in secondary prevention; however, other models (linear spline
\par
and quadratic) warrant further investigation. These findings along with additional
\par
research will help inform more reliable and accurate models of long-term outcomes
\par
and economic benefit of LDL-C lowering treatment modalities.
\par
PRM66
\par
The world health organization and universal diagnostic testing
\par
for suspected malaria in children: is the new policy cost-effective
\par
and affordable for sub-saharan africa?
\par
Phillips V.
\par
Emory University, Atlanta, GA, USA
\par
Objectives: Malaria imposes a substantial global disease burden with 198 million
\par
cases reported worldwide in 2013. It disproportionately affects sub-Saharan Africa,
\par
particularly young children and accounts for 14\% of the region's childhood deaths.
\par
In an effort to improve disease management, the World Health Organization (WHO)
\par
in 2010 recommended countries test children (age {$<$} 5) who present with suspected
\par
malaria fever and confirm diagnosis rather than treat them presumptively with
\par
antimalarial drugs, the standard of care to date. While all 47 African countries
\par
designated as malaria-endemic, have adopted the policy, significant barriers to
\par
implementation exist. These include costs, uncertainty about the overall health
\par
benefits, shortfalls in testing supplies and physician practice patterns. Methods:
\par
We use a decision-analytic approach to assess the policy's cost effectiveness in three
\par
countries in sub-Saharan Africa: Angola, Tanzania and Uganda, each representing
\par
different prevalence/income combinations. Our model includes country-specific
\par
epidemiologic, cost and behavioral data, including that of physician and caregiver.
\par
Our primary data sources are national Malaria Indicator Surveys and information
\par
from each country's National Malaria Control Program. We use a Markov specifi-
\par
cation to account for multiple fever episodes and estimate the incremental cost-
\par
effectiveness of the testing policy through two-stage micro-simulation models.
\par
These models capture key dimensions of uncertainty associated with our projec-
\par
tions. Resul ts: We find that diagnostic testing for malaria is cost-saving in Angola.
\par
In Tanzania the cost per life-year gained is \$5.54 and \$94.58 in Uganda. Both are cost-
\par
effective compared to the WHO standard of \$150 per life-year gained. Our results
\par
are robust under varying cost, prevalence and behavioral assumptions. Probabilistic
\par
sensitivity analyses indicate that testing is cost-saving or cost-effective: 80\% of the
\par
time in Angola, 89\% in Tanzania, and 69\% in Uganda. Conclusions: Our findings
\par
strongly suggest pursuit of policies that facilitate full implementation of testing,
\par
including promoting clinician adherence to test results.
\par
PRM67
\par
Whole-Disease Model aPPRoach: Methodologies And Challenges In
\par
CoMMunicating The Economic Burden Of Rare Diseases
\par
Shah A., Proach J.
\par
Market Access Solutions LLC, Raritan, NJ, USA
\par
Objectives: The concept of whole-disease model has rarely been applied in prac-
\par
tice or considered in the published literature. No studies to date have addressed the
\par
applicability of this approach in rare diseases. This research aims to demonstrate
\par
the applicability, technique, and framework of the model designed to educate US
\par
Payers of the estimated patient-level costs and resource utilization, using a rare
\par
disease as a case study. Methods: The lack of specific ICD-9-CM code, prevalence
\par
overestimation, poorly documented US epidemiology data, complex diagnostics,
\par
and off-label pharmacotherapy were evident in rare diseases. In order to estimate
\par
patient-level resource utilization, diagnosis costs, and 12-month treatment cost
\par
from the US Payer's perspective, a clinical guideline-based approach was developed.
\par
We reviewed diagnosis and treatment guideline recommendations to develop a
\par
resource utilization algorithm which was subsequently validated by clinicians to
\par
reflect the real-world clinical practice. Model cost inputs were derived from pub-
\par
lished resources including the most recent year Medicare Fee Schedules and H-CUP
\par
National Inpatient Sample database. Adverse event costs associated with the treat-
\par
ments were not accounted for in the model. Results: An interactive, MS-Excel
\par
based economic model was constructed based on guideline recommendations. The
\par
model inputs were customizable, allowing flexibility in plan/Payer specific disease
\par
burden estimation. Cost of diagnosis, pharmacotherapy, and surgical therapies were
\par
calculated for a single patient over a 12-month period. Conclusions: The study
\par
demonstrates that the whole-disease model approach can be applied to rare dis-
\par
eases to provide the disease burden estimation, serving as an information tool for
\par
Payers, for diseases with small patient volume and unknown cost burden. The model
\par
also provides a platform for manufacturers to: 1) incorporate real-world data as they
\par
become available; 2) add/remove interventions as the market evolves and; 3) add
\par
various economic elements to further calculate budget impact or cost-effectiveness
\par
of an intervention over a product life-cycle.},
